/* Índice del buscador en inglés (generado por _src-publicaciones/ingles/indice_en.py, no editar a mano). */
window.CATTO_INDICE = (window.CATTO_INDICE || []).concat([{"t":"Tolerances: worst case and statistics","d":"General-purpose passive components","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/pasivos-uso-general#tolerancias","x":"04 Tolerances: worst case and statistics The output of a circuit depends on all of its components, and each has a tolerance and drifts with temperature. There are two ways to analyze it: the worst case, which combines all the extremes and guarantees compliance, but oversizes; and the statistical analysis or Monte Carlo, which draws thousands of circuits and estimates what fraction complies. \\[ V_o = V_i\\,\\frac{R_2}{R_1 + R_2} \\] In a divider, the worst case is \\( R_2 \\) at its maximum and \\( R_1 \\) at its minimum. If both resistors come from the same integrated network, their variations track each other and the ratio is much more stable than each value. Lab · Monte Carlo of a divider 4000 dividers of 10 V with two 10 kΩ resistors, each drawn uniformly within its tolerance and its temperature coefficient. The histogram shows the output; the red lines, the worst case. Tolerance = Temperature coefficient = Temperature change ΔT ="},{"t":"In the lab","d":"General-purpose passive components","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/pasivos-uso-general#laboratorio","x":"05 In the lab Activity 1 · Real distribution Measure fifty resistors from the same lot and plot the histogram. Does it fill the whole tolerance or is it concentrated? Activity 2 · Ceramic capacitors Measure a 10 μF X7R capacitor with the LCR meter applying different bias voltages and compare with the manufacturer's curve. Activity 3 · Drift Heat a divider with a heat gun and measure the variation of its output, with discrete resistors and with an integrated network."},{"t":"Common mistakes","d":"General-purpose passive components","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/pasivos-uso-general#errores","x":"06 Common mistakes Using the nominal value of an X5R or X7R ceramic. With the applied DC voltage it can lose more than half of its capacitance. Forgetting power derating. A 1/4 W resistor does not dissipate 1/4 W inside a hot enclosure. Designing with the tolerance alone. Drift with temperature and aging add to it. Placing an electrolytic next to the heat sink. Every 10 °C more takes away half of its life."},{"t":"Self-assessment","d":"General-purpose passive components","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/pasivos-uso-general#autoevaluacion","x":"07 Self-assessment How many values per decade does the E24 series have? Twenty-four. A 1000 h electrolytic at 105 °C operates at 65 °C. How long does it last, according to the rule of thumb? About 16,000 hours. What is the worst-case error of a 1:2 divider with 1 % resistors? ±1 % of the output: ±50 mV on 5 V. What ceramic capacitor is used in a precision oscillator or filter? A class 1 one, C0G or NP0."},{"t":"Further reading","d":"General-purpose passive components","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/pasivos-uso-general#ampliar","x":"08 Further reading Charles A. Harper (ed.). Passive Electronic Component Handbook. 2nd ed., McGraw-Hill, 1997. The technology of resistors, capacitors and inductors. Paul Horowitz and Winfield Hill. The Art of Electronics. 3rd ed., Cambridge University Press, 2015. The opening chapter and the appendices on real components and how to choose them. Henry W. Ott. Electromagnetic Compatibility Engineering. Wiley, 2009. The behavior of real passives at high frequency."},{"t":"The planning hierarchy","d":"Planning and inventories","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/planificacion-inventarios#planificacion","x":"01 The planning hierarchy Planning production means deciding what, how much and when to manufacture with the available resources. It is done in levels, from the general to the particular: Level Horizon What it decides Aggregate plan Months to a year Total output by family, staffing, shifts, subcontracting and inventory Master production schedule Weeks to months How many units of each finished product and in which week Material requirements planning (MRP) Weeks Which components to order or make, how many and when Shop scheduling Days and hours Which order goes to which machine and in what sequence MRP starts from the master schedule and the bill of materials —each unit takes so many boards, each board so many components— and, subtracting inventory and adding lead times, calculates the necessary orders: this is the explosion of the bill. Integrated with purchasing, finance and sales, it became the ERP systems. Shop scheduling uses Gantt charts and priority rules, such as serving first the order with the shortest processing time or the one with the nearest due date. Just in time, by contrast, does not push production from a plan but pulls it from demand, with kanban."},{"t":"Inventories","d":"Planning and inventories","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/planificacion-inventarios#inventarios","x":"02 Inventories Inventories —raw materials, work in process and finished goods— buffer the differences between the pace of purchasing, of production and of sales, but they tie up money, take up space, deteriorate or become obsolete, and hide problems. In electronics the risk of obsolescence is high: a discontinued component can stop a line. ABC classification ranks items by their annual value: typically 20 % of them, the A items, account for about 80 % of the value and deserve the strictest control."},{"t":"The economic order quantity and the reorder point","d":"Planning and inventories","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/planificacion-inventarios#lote","x":"03 The economic order quantity and the reorder point With a constant annual demand \\( D \\), a cost \\( S \\) for each order and a cost \\( H \\) of holding one unit in inventory for a year, the total annual cost as a function of the lot size \\( Q \\) and its minimum, Harris’s economic order quantity (1913), are: \\[ CT(Q) = \\frac{D}{Q}\\,S + \\frac{Q}{2}\\,H \\] \\[ Q^* = \\sqrt{\\frac{2 D S}{H}} \\] At the optimum, the ordering cost and the holding cost are equal. The order is placed when inventory falls to the reorder point, the demand during the lead time \\( L \\) plus a safety stock \\( z\\,\\sigma_d \\sqrt{L} \\), which covers the variability of daily demand with a service level given by \\( z \\). Lab · economic order quantity The curves are the annual ordering cost (violet), holding cost (green) and total cost (blue) as a function of the lot size. The yellow dot is the chosen lot. For the reorder point, 250 working days, a lead time of 10 days, a standard deviation of daily demand of 20 % and a service level of 95 % (z = 1.645) are assumed. Annual demand D = Cost per order S = Holding cost H = Chosen lot Q ="},{"t":"In the laboratory","d":"Planning and inventories","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/planificacion-inventarios#laboratorio","x":"04 In the laboratory Activity 1 · Bill of materials Build the multilevel bill of materials of a laboratory unit and carry out the explosion for one hundred units. Activity 2 · ABC classification With the laboratory component inventory and its prices, classify the items and plot the Pareto curve. Activity 3 · Scheduling Schedule five orders on two machines with different priority rules and compare the delays on a Gantt chart."},{"t":"Common mistakes","d":"Planning and inventories","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/planificacion-inventarios#errores","x":"05 Common mistakes Forgetting the safety stock. With variable demand, ordering exactly at the expected demand leaves you out of stock half of the time. Applying the economic order quantity with highly irregular demand. The model assumes constant demand. Controlling all items equally. ABC classification concentrates the effort where the money is. Planning materials without considering capacity. An MRP can order more than the machines can make."},{"t":"Self-assessment","d":"Planning and inventories","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/planificacion-inventarios#autoevaluacion","x":"06 Self-assessment What is the economic order quantity with D = 12,000, S = 100 and H = 4? \\( \\sqrt{2 \\cdot 12\\,000 \\cdot 100 / 4} \\approx 775 \\) units. How many orders per year does that imply? About 15.5. What happens to the economic order quantity if demand quadruples? It doubles, because it depends on the square root of demand. What is the explosion of the bill of materials? The calculation of the quantities of each component needed to produce the master schedule."},{"t":"Further reading","d":"Planning and inventories","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/planificacion-inventarios#ampliar","x":"07 Further reading Jay Heizer and Barry Render. Principles of Operations Management. Pearson. (Spanish edition: “Principios de administración de operaciones”.) Planning, MRP and inventory models. Richard B. Chase, F. Robert Jacobs and Nicholas J. Aquilano. Operations and Supply Chain Management. McGraw-Hill. (Spanish edition: “Administración de operaciones: producción y cadena de suministros”.) Production and inventory planning with cases. Steven Nahmias. Production and Operations Analysis. McGraw-Hill. (Spanish edition: “Análisis de la producción y las operaciones”.) The mathematical models of inventory and planning."},{"t":"Why an electronics engineer needs complex numbers","d":"The complex plane and its regions","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/plano-complejo#porque","x":"01 Why an electronics engineer needs complex numbers The word “imaginary” is a historical misfortune: it suggests that they do not exist. In electronics, the complex plane is the place where the most concrete things of the whole degree live. Object in the complex plane What it is in electronics A point in polar form A phasor: amplitude and phase of a sinusoidal signal A number \\( R + jX \\) An impedance: resistance and reactance Multiplying by \\( e^{j\\theta} \\) Phase-shifting a signal by \\( \\theta \\) radians A point in the left half-plane A stable pole: the response dies out The unit circle The edge of stability of a discrete system The circle \\( |\\Gamma| = \\text{const} \\) A circle of the Smith chart This whole course is built on that plane: poles and zeros, the Laplace transform, the Z-transform and the frequency response are readings of points and regions inside it."},{"t":"The three ways of writing the same thing","d":"The complex plane and its regions","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/plano-complejo#formas","x":"02 The three ways of writing the same thing \\[ z = a + jb = r(\\cos\\theta + j\\,\\operatorname{sin}\\theta) = r\\,e^{j\\theta} \\] Rectangular, trigonometric and exponential. In engineering you write \\( j \\) and not \\( i \\), because \\( i \\) is already the current. For Use Because Adding and subtracting Rectangular The real and imaginary parts are added separately Multiplying and dividing Polar or exponential The moduli are multiplied and the angles are added Raising to a power Exponential \\( z^n = r^n e^{jn\\theta} \\), De Moivre’s formula Differentiating and integrating Exponential The exponential is its own derivative, up to a factor Euler’s formula \\[ e^{j\\theta} = \\cos\\theta + j\\,\\operatorname{sin}\\theta \\qquad \\cos\\theta = \\frac{e^{j\\theta} + e^{-j\\theta}}{2} \\qquad \\operatorname{sin}\\theta = \\frac{e^{j\\theta} - e^{-j\\theta}}{2j} \\] It is the bridge between what oscillates and what grows: it turns sines and cosines into exponentials, which are far easier to differentiate, integrate and multiply. The whole phasor method used in alternating current comes from here. Lab · the complex plane Click or tap the plane to move the point, or use the sliders. The operations are drawn on the same plane. Just the number z Conjugate of z Multiply by w Inverse 1/z Square it Cube roots of z Mark a region Modulus |z| = 1.50 Argument θ = 40 ° |z − 1| < 1 · a disk 1 < |z| < 2 · an annulus Re(z) > 0 · right half-plane Re(z) < 0 · left half-plane, the stable zone |z| < 1 · unit disk, discrete-time sta"},{"t":"What each operation does in the plane","d":"The complex plane and its regions","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/plano-complejo#operaciones","x":"03 What each operation does in the plane Every operation with complex numbers is a geometric motion. Seeing it that way avoids memorizing formulas: Operation In the plane Where it appears Adding Placing the vectors head to tail Adding two out-of-phase voltages Multiplying by \\( r\\,e^{j\\theta} \\) Scaling by \\( r \\) and rotating by \\( \\theta \\) Passing through a network that attenuates and shifts phase Multiplying by \\( j \\) Rotating 90° without changing the size Differentiating a sinusoid; inductive reactance Conjugating Reflecting across the real axis Impedance matching: \\( Z_L = Z_S^* \\) Inverting \\( 1/z \\) Inverting the modulus and changing the sign of the angle Going from impedance to admittance Squaring Doubling the angle and squaring the modulus A mixer, which generates twice the frequency The n roots A complex number has n nth roots, spread over the vertices of a regular polygon inscribed in a circle of radius \\( \\sqrt[n]{r} \\): \\[ z_k = \\sqrt[n]{r}\\ e^{\\,j\\left(\\frac{\\theta + 2\\pi k}{n}\\right)}, \\qquad k = 0, 1, \\ldots, n-1 \\] Those roots are the ones that appear when you solve the characteristic equation of a system, and their position in the plane decides whether the response oscillates, dies out or grows."},{"t":"Regions: where an inequality draws a figure","d":"The complex plane and its regions","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/plano-complejo#regiones","x":"04 Regions: where an inequality draws a figure An inequality between complex numbers does not define an interval, as with the reals, but a region of the plane. Recognizing them by heart saves a lot of work: Condition Region What it means in the degree \\( |z - z_0| = r \\) Circle centered at \\( z_0 \\) A locus of constant modulus \\( |z - z_0| \\lt r \\) Open disk Neighborhood of a point: the basis of limits \\( r_1 \\lt |z| \\lt r_2 \\) Annulus Region of convergence of the Z-transform \\( \\operatorname{Re}(z) \\lt 0 \\) Left half-plane Stable zone of Laplace \\( |z| \\lt 1 \\) Unit disk Stable zone of the Z-transform \\( |\\arg z| \\lt \\alpha \\) Angular sector Minimum damping of a system The two highlighted rows are the reason this course starts with complex variable: the question “is this system stable?” is answered by looking at where its poles fall in the plane."},{"t":"The phasor, which is all of this together","d":"The complex plane and its regions","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/plano-complejo#fasores","x":"05 The phasor, which is all of this together A sinusoidal signal \\( v(t) = V_p \\cos(\\omega t + \\varphi) \\) can be written as the real part of a rotating complex exponential: \\[ v(t) = \\operatorname{Re}\\left\\{ V_p\\,e^{j(\\omega t + \\varphi)} \\right\\} = \\operatorname{Re}\\left\\{ \\underbrace{V_p e^{j\\varphi}}_{\\text{phasor}} \\cdot e^{j\\omega t} \\right\\} \\] The phasor stores amplitude and phase; \\( e^{j\\omega t} \\) is the rotation, which is common to all the signals in the circuit and cancels out. With that, a circuit with derivatives and integrals becomes an algebraic one: differentiating is multiplying by \\( j\\omega \\) and integrating is dividing by \\( j\\omega \\). From there come \\( Z_L = j\\omega L \\) and \\( Z_C = 1/(j\\omega C) \\), and Ohm’s law works again, now with complex numbers. The phasor assumes sinusoidal steady state It holds when everything oscillates at the same frequency and the transient has already died out. For switch-on, for a pulse or for an arbitrary signal, you need the more general tool studied later: Laplace, which is this same idea with an exponential that also grows or dies out, \\( e^{st} \\) with \\( s = \\sigma + j\\omega \\)."},{"t":"In the lab","d":"The complex plane and its regions","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/plano-complejo#laboratorio","x":"06 In the lab Activity 1 · Measuring a phasor With a generator and a two-channel oscilloscope, measure the amplitude and phase shift in an RC circuit. Draw the input phasor and the output phasor on graph paper and check the angle with \\( \\varphi = -\\arctan(\\omega RC) \\). Activity 2 · The roots, on the screen Compute by hand the five fifth roots of unity and plot them. Check that they form a regular pentagon and that their sum is zero. Activity 3 · Impedance and admittance For a series RLC at three frequencies, compute \\( Z \\) in rectangular and in polar form, and its inverse \\( Y = 1/Z \\). Verify that inverting reflects the point across the unit circle and changes the sign of the angle, as in the lab on this page."},{"t":"Common mistakes","d":"The complex plane and its regions","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/plano-complejo#errores","x":"07 Common mistakes Confusing \\( j \\) with \\( i \\). In electronics \\( i \\) is current; the imaginary unit is \\( j \\). Adding in polar form. To add you have to convert to rectangular; only the product and the quotient are convenient in polar. Losing the quadrant when computing the angle with \\( \\arctan(b/a) \\): you have to look at the signs of \\( a \\) and \\( b \\), or use the two-argument function. Mixing degrees and radians in the same calculation. Forgetting that the nth root has n results and keeping only one. Writing the phasor with the peak value when you had been working in RMS, or the other way around."},{"t":"Self-assessment","d":"The complex plane and its regions","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/plano-complejo#autoevaluacion","x":"08 Self-assessment Write \\( z = 3 + j4 \\) in polar form. \\( r = \\sqrt{9+16} = 5 \\) and \\( \\theta = \\arctan(4/3) \\approx 53.13^\\circ \\), that is, \\( 5\\,e^{j53.13^\\circ} \\). What does multiplying a phasor by \\( j \\) do to it? It rotates it 90° counterclockwise without changing its modulus. It is what differentiating a sinusoid does. What region is \\( 1 \\lt |z| \\lt 2 \\)? An annulus centered at the origin, between radii 1 and 2. It is the typical shape of a region of convergence in the Z-transform. Where do the poles of a continuous-time system have to be for it to be stable? In the left half-plane, with negative real part: that way the exponential \\( e^{st} \\) dies out instead of growing. How many cube roots does 8 have and where are they? Three, of modulus 2, spaced 120° apart: \\( 2 \\), \\( 2e^{j120^\\circ} \\) and \\( 2e^{j240^\\circ} \\). Only the first is real. Why does the conjugate appear in impedance matching? Because maximum power transfer occurs with \\( Z_L = Z_S^* \\): the same resistance and the opposite reactance, so that they cancel."},{"t":"Further reading","d":"The complex plane and its regions","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/plano-complejo#ampliar","x":"09 Further reading Murray R. Spiegel. Complex Variables. Schaum’s Outline Series (Spanish edition, “Variable compleja”), McGraw-Hill, 2011. Concise theory and hundreds of solved problems: the classic companion for this part of the course. Erwin Kreyszig. Advanced Engineering Mathematics. Wiley (Spanish edition, “Matemáticas avanzadas para ingeniería, volumen 2”, 3rd ed.), Limusa Wiley, 2003. Complex variable from the engineer’s point of view, with good application examples. Alan V. Oppenheim and Alan S. Willsky. Signals and Systems. 2nd ed. (Spanish edition, “Señales y sistemas”), Pearson, 1997. The book for the whole course; worth having from day one. Charles K. Alexander and Matthew N. O. Sadiku. Fundamentals of Electric Circuits. 5th ed. (Spanish edition, “Fundamentos de circuitos eléctricos”), McGraw-Hill, 2013. The chapter on phasors, to see these complex numbers at work measuring real circuits."},{"t":"The direction in which the field oscillates","d":"Polarization","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/polarizacion#estados","x":"01 The direction in which the field oscillates The fact that the electromagnetic wave is transverse leaves a freedom that longitudinal waves do not have: the electric field can oscillate in any direction in the plane perpendicular to the propagation. That direction is the polarization, and sound does not have it because it oscillates in the same direction in which it travels. State What the field does How it is achieved Linear Always oscillates in the same plane A polarizer, or a single-element antenna Circular Rotates, describing a circle Two equal components shifted by 90° Elliptical Rotates, describing an ellipse The general case: any amplitudes or phase shift Unpolarized Changes randomly all the time Any thermal source: the Sun, a lamp The last row is the natural state of almost all light: a lamp emits photons from atoms that are not coordinated with each other, and the result averages over all directions. Polarizing it is filtering, and that is why it always costs energy. Circular polarization is not a detail In satellite communications circular polarization is used because the relative orientation between the satellite and the ground antenna changes, and with linear polarization it would have to be tracked. Also a right-hand circular wave and a left-hand one are independent: two channels can be transmitted on the same frequency. And in the ionosphere, Faraday rotation turns the plane of a linear wave unpredictably, something that does not affect a circular one."},{"t":"Malus’s law","d":"Polarization","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/polarizacion#malus","x":"02 Malus’s law A polarizer lets through only the component of the field parallel to its axis. Since the intensity goes with the square of the field, the fraction that passes is: \\[ I = I_0\\cos^2\\theta \\] With \\( \\theta \\) the angle between the incoming polarization and the axis of the polarizer. If the light enters unpolarized, the first polarizer lets through exactly half, whatever its orientation: it is the average of \\( \\cos^2 \\) over all angles. Lab · cascaded polarizers Three polarizers with adjustable axes. Start with the first and the third crossed at 90°: nothing gets through. Then insert the middle one at 45° and see what happens. The incoming light is unpolarized Use the middle polarizer Axis of the first = 0° Axis of the second = 45° Axis of the third = 90° Why the middle one lets light through It seems contradictory that adding a filter could increase what gets through, and it is not. The middle polarizer does not merely filter: it reorients. What comes out of it is polarized at 45°, and that direction does have a component along the axis of the third. Without it, what reaches the third is perpendicular to its axis and has none. It is the cleanest demonstration that a polarizer projects a vector, rather than simply letting through “the light that was already there.”"},{"t":"Polarizing without a polarizer","d":"Polarization","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/polarizacion#brewster","x":"03 Polarizing without a polarizer Reflection polarizes. When light strikes a transparent surface at the Brewster angle, what is reflected is completely polarized in the plane parallel to the surface: \\[ \\tan\\theta_B = \\frac{n_2}{n_1} \\] For air and glass it gives about 56°, and for air and water, 53°. That is the basis of polarized sunglasses: the glare from asphalt or water is mostly horizontal, so a vertical filter removes it and lets the rest through. Phenomenon What it does Where it appears Brewster reflection Polarizes what is reflected Polarized sunglasses, laser windows Rayleigh scattering Polarizes the sky at 90° from the Sun Photography filters; orientation of some insects Birefringence Two indices depending on the polarization Quarter-wave plates, calcite Optical activity Rotates the plane of polarization Measuring sugar concentration The screen you are looking at An LCD is pure polarization. Behind it there is a white lamp, in front two crossed polarizers —which by themselves would give black— and in between a layer of liquid crystal whose molecules rotate the plane of polarization. With no voltage, the layer rotates it 90° and the light passes through both polarizers: pixel on. With voltage, the molecules align, stop rotating anything and the second polarizer blocks: pixel off. Each pixel is a Malus experiment, and that is why the screen of a laptop goes dark when viewed through rotated polarized sunglasses."},{"t":"In the lab","d":"Polarization","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/polarizacion#laboratorio","x":"04 In the lab Activity 1 · Verifying Malus With two polarizers, a stable source and a photodiode with an amplifier, measure the intensity every ten degrees of rotation of the second. Plot it against \\( \\cos^2\\theta \\) and verify that it gives a straight line. Activity 2 · The third one in the middle Cross two polarizers until extinction and measure. Insert a third between them and sweep it from 0 to 90°, measuring. Locate the maximum and compare it with the 12.5 % predicted by theory. Activity 3 · Brewster angle Reflect a beam off a glass plate while varying the angle and observe the reflection through a polarizer. Find the extinction angle and from it obtain the refractive index of the glass."},{"t":"Common mistakes","d":"Polarization","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/polarizacion#errores","x":"05 Common mistakes Believing that a polarizer “lets through the light that was already in its direction.” It projects: it transmits the component. Expecting the first one to let through more than half. With unpolarized light it is always 50 %, in any orientation. Thinking that adding a filter cannot increase what gets through. The middle one proves otherwise. Using Malus with unpolarized light. Malus holds from the second polarizer onward. Confusing circular polarization with unpolarized light. Circular is perfectly defined; it just rotates. Forgetting that polarization is transverse. Sound is not polarized: it oscillates in the direction in which it travels."},{"t":"Self-assessment","d":"Polarization","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/polarizacion#autoevaluacion","x":"06 Self-assessment How much light passes through the first polarizer? Half, if it enters unpolarized, whatever its orientation. What fraction passes through three polarizers at 0°, 45° and 90°? One eighth: \\( \\tfrac12 \\times \\cos^2 45° \\times \\cos^2 45° = 12.5\\ \\% \\) of the light that entered. Why does the middle one let light through if the other two are crossed? Because it reorients: what comes out of it is at 45°, and that direction does have a component along the axis of the third. What is the Brewster angle? The angle of incidence at which the reflected light is totally polarized. Its tangent is the ratio of the indices. Why do satellites use circular polarization? Because it does not depend on the relative orientation, and the ionosphere rotates the plane of a linear wave unpredictably. How does an LCD pixel work? Two crossed polarizers with liquid crystal in between: with no voltage it rotates the polarization 90° and light passes; with voltage it does not rotate it and the second polarizer blocks it."},{"t":"Further reading","d":"Polarization","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/polarizacion#ampliar","x":"07 Further reading Eugene Hecht. Optics. 5th ed. (Spanish edition, “Óptica”), Pearson, 2017. The whole of chapter 8: states of polarization, Malus, Brewster, birefringence and retarder plates. Raymond A. Serway and John W. Jewett. Physics for Scientists and Engineers with Modern Physics. 10th ed. (Spanish edition, “Física para ciencias e ingeniería”), Cengage, 2019. The introductory treatment, with the exercises on cascaded polarizers. Ernst Lueder. Liquid Crystal Displays: Addressing Schemes and Electro-Optical Effects. 2nd ed., Wiley, 2010. How a display is built from two polarizers and a layer of liquid crystal."},{"t":"Why there is technology policy","d":"Policies for national and regional development","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/politicas-desarrollo#por-que","x":"01 Why there is technology policy No country buys development off a supermarket shelf. What can be bought are products; the capacity to design, maintain and improve them is built, and that building takes decades and decisions. Technology policy is the set of public decisions about what gets researched, what is produced locally, what is imported and who pays the bill. The underlying question Should a country produce what it already knows how to make cheaply —comparative advantages— or build capabilities in sectors where it is not yet competitive, betting that it will become so? Comparative advantages are built: nobody was born making semiconductors. The discussion is about how much it costs to build them, who finances it and for how long. For an electronic engineer this is not abstract: it determines whether you will work designing or assembling, whether there are laboratories where a product can be certified, whether funding exists for a development, and whether your knowledge has somewhere to be applied without emigrating."},{"t":"Sábato’s triangle","d":"Policies for national and regional development","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/politicas-desarrollo#sabato","x":"02 Sábato’s triangle In 1968, the Argentine physicist Jorge Sábato and the political scientist Natalio Botana published the most cited model in the region. It says that a country’s technological capacity does not depend on having good scientists or good factories separately, but on the relationships among three vertices: Lab · the triangle, vertex by vertex Tap each vertex or each side to see what it represents, and then try the cases: the diagnosis changes depending on which relationship is weak. — Choose a case to diagnose — A country that assembles products designed abroad Plenty of published research, little transfer A national satellite developed by a state-owned company A small electronics company that wants to certify a product Sábato distinguished three types of relationships: intra-vertex (how each one is organized internally), inter-vertex (the sides of the triangle) and extra-vertex (the links with the outside world). His thesis: underdeveloped countries tend to have reasonable vertices and weak sides. Good scientists who do not talk to industry; industry that imports technology instead of asking for it domestically; a State that funds without coordinating."},{"t":"Development models and their effects","d":"Policies for national and regional development","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/politicas-desarrollo#modelos","x":"03 Development models and their effects Model Idea Risk Import substitution Produce locally what was previously bought abroad, protecting the domestic market Without a competitiveness requirement, a country can end up making expensive, outdated goods Openness and specialization Produce what you are already competitive in and import the rest Capabilities are lost that later take decades to rebuild Sector promotion Tax benefits or credit for a chosen sector If the benefit does not require local design in return, assembly is funded with no transfer Public procurement as an engine The State buys from local suppliers and thereby creates a market Requires technical capacity in the buyer to specify and control properly The assembly trap Putting together equipment from imported kits creates jobs and saves foreign currency in the short term, but if the regime does not require incorporating design, product engineering and local suppliers, no installed capacity remains when the benefit ends. The industrial promotion regime of Tierra del Fuego —in force since 1972 under Law 19,640— is the Argentine case where that debate was most intense, with strong arguments on both sides: jobs and productive sovereignty on one, fiscal cost and little local integration on the other."},{"t":"The Argentine system, in concrete terms","d":"Policies for national and regional development","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/politicas-desarrollo#argentina","x":"04 The Argentine system, in concrete terms Argentina has a science and technology system that is comparatively dense for the region: Institution What it does Where it touches electronics CONICET Argentina’s National Scientific and Technical Research Council: research in all disciplines, with its own institutes and in universities Materials, microelectronics, signal processing INTI The National Institute of Industrial Technology: industrial technology, testing and certification Laboratories for electromagnetic compatibility, electrical safety, metrology CNEA The National Atomic Energy Commission: nuclear energy and its associated technologies Nuclear instrumentation, control electronics, detectors CONAE The National Space Activities Commission: space activities Satellites, communication systems, telemetry Agencia I+D+i The national R&D&I Agency: funds projects through competitive grants Subsidies and loans for technological development in companies Universities They educate, and they also do research and transfer The UTN, with its network of regional faculties, is a clear case of a link with industry The underlying legal framework is Law 25,467 on Science, Technology and Innovation (2001), which organizes the system and defines responsibilities. Multi-year plans and later funding laws were built on it. Two cases worth knowing INVAP, a state-owned company from Río Negro province born of an agreement with the CNEA, designs and exports nuclear research reactors, radars and satell"},{"t":"The regional dimension","d":"Policies for national and regional development","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/politicas-desarrollo#regional","x":"05 The regional dimension No country in the region has, by itself, the market that would justify a state-of-the-art semiconductor fab: a modern plant costs tens of billions of dollars. Hence the importance of regional integration —common standards, joint purchasing, shared projects— and of choosing well the niches where it is possible to compete: scientific instrumentation, electronics for agriculture, embedded systems, control software, engineering services. The realistic strategy is not “make everything” but to position yourself in the higher-value links: designing, integrating, certifying and maintaining, even if the wafer comes from abroad. That requires trained engineers, testing laboratories and continuity in the rules."},{"t":"Activities","d":"Policies for national and regional development","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/politicas-desarrollo#laboratorio","x":"06 Activities Activity 1 · Diagnosis with the triangle Choose an electronic product made in Argentina and map its triangle: what each vertex does, which side is weakest and what specific measure would strengthen it. Activity 2 · Tracking down a policy instrument Find a currently open call for funding technological development, read the terms and summarize: who can apply, what it funds, what counterpart it requires and what outcome is expected. Activity 3 · Debate with data Prepare, in two groups, the arguments for and against the Tierra del Fuego promotion regime, with figures on employment, fiscal cost and local integration. Each group must be able to state the best version of the opposing argument."},{"t":"Ideas worth revisiting","d":"Policies for national and regional development","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/politicas-desarrollo#errores","x":"07 Ideas worth revisiting “Good scientists are enough.” Sábato showed that without a link to production, knowledge does not turn into capacity. “Technology is just imported, end of story.” The artifact is imported; the capacity to maintain it, adapt it and improve it does not come in the box. Confusing assembling with manufacturing. The difference lies in where the design is done. Assuming that technology policy is only money. Public procurement, standards, certification and training weigh as much as funding. Measuring success only in publications or only in revenue: the triangle asks you to look at both and, above all, at the relationship between them."},{"t":"Self-assessment","d":"Policies for national and regional development","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/politicas-desarrollo#autoevaluacion","x":"08 Self-assessment What are the three vertices of Sábato’s triangle? Government, the productive structure and the scientific-technological infrastructure. What does it mean for the sides to be weak? That the vertices exist but are not linked: research does not reach production, industry does not demand local knowledge and the State funds without coordinating. What is the risk of industrial promotion with no local design requirement? That the assembly of imported kits becomes entrenched: there are jobs while the benefit lasts, but no technological capacity is left installed. Name two institutions of the Argentine system and what they contribute to electronics. For example, the INTI with electromagnetic compatibility and electrical safety testing, and the CONAE with the development of satellite systems. Why do people speak of “built” comparative advantages? Because competitiveness in a sector is not a natural given: it is built with investment, training and time. No country was born making semiconductors."},{"t":"Further reading","d":"Policies for national and regional development","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/politicas-desarrollo#ampliar","x":"09 Further reading Jorge A. Sábato and Natalio Botana. La ciencia y la tecnología en el desarrollo futuro de América Latina. 1968; reprinted in several anthologies (in Spanish). The original text of the triangle, short and still relevant. Oscar Varsavsky. Ciencia, política y cientificismo. CEAL, 1969 (in Spanish). An uncomfortable and fundamental critique: what research is for and for whom it is done in a peripheral country. Amílcar Herrera. Ciencia y política en América Latina. Siglo XXI, 1971 (in Spanish). Distinguishes explicit science policy from implicit science policy, which is the one that really rules. Jorge Katz and Bernardo Kosacoff. El proceso de industrialización en la Argentina. CEPAL / CEAL (in Spanish). Argentine industrial history with data, useful for discussing the models in section 3."},{"t":"The two numbers that define a circuit","d":"Poles and zeros","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/polos-y-ceros#que","x":"01 The two numbers that define a circuit A lumped-parameter transfer function is always a quotient of polynomials, and every polynomial can be factored. So: \\[ H(s) = K\\,\\frac{(s - z_1)(s - z_2)\\cdots(s - z_m)}{(s - p_1)(s - p_2)\\cdots(s - p_n)} \\] The \\( z_i \\) are the zeros: there \\( H \\) is zero. The \\( p_k \\) are the poles: there \\( H \\) goes to infinity. That factorization says something powerful: a constant and a handful of points of the plane contain all the information about the circuit. The frequency response, the step response, the stability and even the settling time come from reading where those points are. How to read each one The real part of a pole tells how fast that component of the response dies out: a pole at \\( \\sigma \\) contributes \\( e^{\\sigma t} \\). The imaginary part tells at what frequency it oscillates. A real pole gives an exponential; a conjugate pair, a damped sinusoid."},{"t":"The frequency response, read with a ruler","d":"Poles and zeros","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/polos-y-ceros#geometria","x":"02 The frequency response, read with a ruler To obtain the frequency response, \\( H \\) is evaluated on the imaginary axis, that is, at \\( s = j\\omega \\). Taking the modulus and phase of the factorization: \\[ |H(j\\omega)| = |K|\\,\\frac{\\prod_i |\\,j\\omega - z_i\\,|}{\\prod_k |\\,j\\omega - p_k\\,|}, \\qquad \\angle H(j\\omega) = \\sum_i \\angle(j\\omega - z_i) - \\sum_k \\angle(j\\omega - p_k) \\] Each factor \\( j\\omega - p_k \\) is the vector that goes from the pole to the operating point on the axis. The magnitude of the response is the product of the distances to the zeros divided by the product of the distances to the poles. With a ruler and a protractor on the diagram you can estimate the Bode plot without computing anything. From this come the two rules that an electronics engineer uses all the time: bringing a pole closer to the imaginary axis raises a peak at that frequency, because the denominator distance becomes small; and a zero on the axis produces a null, because the numerator vanishes. Lab · the diagram and its response On the left, the \\( s \\)-plane with the poles (×) and zeros (○), and the vectors going to the operating point. On the right, the magnitude in decibels and the phase. Click or tap the plane to move the pole pair. First-order low-pass Second-order low-pass Second-order high-pass Band-pass Notch · band-reject All-pass Show the vectors Operating frequency ω = 1.00 rad/s Q = 0.71"},{"t":"Stability: a single line divides the plane","d":"Poles and zeros","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/polos-y-ceros#estabilidad","x":"03 Stability: a single line divides the plane The natural response of a system is the sum of the terms \\( e^{p_k t} \\), one per pole. Whether it dies out or not depends only on the sign of the real part: Where the pole is What it contributes to the response Result \\( \\operatorname{Re}(p) \\lt 0 \\) \\( e^{\\sigma t} \\) with \\( \\sigma \\lt 0 \\) Dies out · stable \\( \\operatorname{Re}(p) = 0 \\), simple Oscillation of constant amplitude Marginally stable \\( \\operatorname{Re}(p) = 0 \\), double \\( t\\cos\\omega t \\), grows Unstable \\( \\operatorname{Re}(p) \\gt 0 \\) \\( e^{\\sigma t} \\) with \\( \\sigma \\gt 0 \\) Grows without bound · unstable Zeros do not affect stability: they can be anywhere. They do affect the shape of the response, and a zero in the right half-plane —what is called non-minimum phase — makes the output start off in the wrong direction before correcting. This is what happens in a boost converter and is one of the reasons they are hard to compensate. The oscillator lives on the line A sinusoidal oscillator needs a pair of poles exactly on the imaginary axis. Since that cannot be sustained with real components, they are designed slightly to the right and an amplitude control is added —a small lamp, a JFET, a limiter— that pushes them toward the axis when the amplitude grows. The circuit settles by itself onto the line."},{"t":"The cases you have to recognize by heart","d":"Poles and zeros","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/polos-y-ceros#casos","x":"04 The cases you have to recognize by heart Function Poles and zeros What it does \\( \\dfrac{1}{s + \\omega_0} \\) One real pole Low-pass, −20 dB/dec, −45° at \\( \\omega_0 \\) \\( \\dfrac{s}{s + \\omega_0} \\) A zero at the origin First-order high-pass \\( \\dfrac{\\omega_0^2}{s^2 + \\frac{\\omega_0}{Q}s + \\omega_0^2} \\) Conjugate pair Low-pass with a peak if \\( Q \\gt 0.707 \\) \\( \\dfrac{s^2 + \\omega_0^2}{s^2 + \\frac{\\omega_0}{Q}s + \\omega_0^2} \\) Zeros on the axis Notch: exact null at \\( \\omega_0 \\) \\( \\dfrac{s - a}{s + a} \\) Zero and pole in mirror image All-pass: flat magnitude, only the phase changes The all-pass deserves attention because it is counterintuitive: it has a zero in the right half-plane and yet is perfectly stable, because the pole is on the left. Its magnitude is 1 at all frequencies and all it does is delay. It is used to equalize delays and to correct phase without touching the amplitude."},{"t":"In the lab","d":"Poles and zeros","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/polos-y-ceros#laboratorio","x":"05 In the lab Activity 1 · Measuring the peak and deducing Q Build a series RLC with variable \\( R \\) and measure the frequency response. Locate the peak, measure its height relative to the passband and solve for \\( Q \\). Compare with \\( Q = \\frac{1}{R}\\sqrt{L/C} \\) and locate the poles in the plane. Activity 2 · The power-line notch Design a notch at 50 Hz with \\( Q = 10 \\), compute the positions of its two zeros and two poles, and verify in the lab how many decibels of attenuation it gives at 50 Hz and how much it affects 45 Hz and 55 Hz. Activity 3 · The phase of the all-pass Build a first-order all-pass with an operational amplifier and measure magnitude and phase between \\( 0.1\\,a \\) and \\( 10\\,a \\). Check that the magnitude stays flat while the phase sweeps 180°."},{"t":"Common mistakes","d":"Poles and zeros","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/polos-y-ceros#errores","x":"06 Common mistakes Believing that a zero in the right half-plane makes the system unstable. Stability is decided by the poles, nothing else. Forgetting the zeros at infinity. If the denominator has a higher degree than the numerator, there are zeros at infinity, and that fixes the final slope of the Bode plot. Confusing \\( \\omega_0 \\) with the frequency of the peak. The peak is at \\( \\omega_0\\sqrt{1 - 1/(2Q^2)} \\), slightly below. Drawing a conjugate pair without its mirror partner. With real coefficients, complex poles always come in pairs. Canceling an unstable pole with a zero. On paper they cancel; in the real circuit the cancellation is never exact and the unstable mode is still there."},{"t":"Self-assessment","d":"Poles and zeros","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/polos-y-ceros#autoevaluacion","x":"07 Self-assessment What is a pole and what is a zero? A zero is a value of \\( s \\) where \\( H \\) vanishes; a pole, where it becomes infinite. They are the roots of the numerator and of the denominator. How do you estimate \\( |H(j\\omega)| \\) by looking at the diagram? Product of the distances to the zeros divided by the product of the distances to the poles, all times \\( |K| \\). A pole at \\( -0.1 \\pm j10 \\): what response does it give? An oscillation of 10 rad/s that decays with a time constant of 10 s. It is a high \\( Q \\), around 50: in frequency it will show a pronounced peak. Why does the all-pass have constant magnitude? Because the zero and the pole are mirror images across the imaginary axis: for each \\( j\\omega \\) the two distances are equal and the quotient is 1. What does bringing a pole closer to the imaginary axis do to the circuit? It raises a peak at that frequency and makes the time response take longer to die out. On the axis, it oscillates without damping. What is a non-minimum-phase system? One with zeros in the right half-plane. It is stable, but its output starts off in the opposite direction before heading to the final value."},{"t":"Further reading","d":"Poles and zeros","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/polos-y-ceros#ampliar","x":"08 Further reading Alan V. Oppenheim and Alan S. Willsky. Signals and Systems. 2nd ed. (Spanish edition, “Señales y sistemas”), Prentice Hall, 1998. The Laplace chapter, with the relationship between pole location and time response. Adel S. Sedra and Kenneth C. Smith. Microelectronic Circuits. 7th ed. (Spanish edition, “Circuitos microelectrónicos”), Oxford University Press, 2016. The filters chapter: each classic response with its pole-zero diagram alongside. Katsuhiko Ogata. Modern Control Engineering. 5th ed. (Spanish edition, “Ingeniería de control moderna”), Pearson, 2010. The root locus, which is the study of how the poles move as a gain is varied."},{"t":"The power that travels along the line","d":"Power in transmission lines","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/potencia-lineas#potencia","x":"01 The power that travels along the line In a transmission line the power travels in two waves: the incident one, which goes from the generator to the load, and the reflected one, which comes back. Since the characteristic impedance is real in a low-loss line, each wave carries its own power and what the load receives is the difference: \\[ P_{inc} = \\frac{|V^+|^2}{2 Z_0} \\] \\[ P_{ref} = |\\Gamma_L|^2\\, P_{inc} \\] \\[ P_L = P_{inc}\\,\\left(1 - |\\Gamma_L|^2\\right) \\] With peak amplitudes. A directional power meter, the instrument of every transmitter, measures the incident and the reflected power separately; the SWR comes from their ratio. With an SWR of 2, 11% of the incident power is reflected; with an SWR of 3, 25%. That power is not necessarily lost: it goes back toward the generator and, if the generator is not matched, it is reflected again toward the load. What is lost is what the line itself dissipates on each trip."},{"t":"The losses of the line","d":"Power in transmission lines","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/potencia-lineas#perdidas","x":"02 The losses of the line A real line attenuates the wave as it advances. The attenuation is given in decibels per 100 m, with the line matched, and it has two causes with different dependence on frequency: \\[ \\alpha_{dB} \\approx k_1 \\sqrt{f} + k_2\\, f \\] The first term is the resistance of the conductors, which grows with the square root of frequency because of the skin effect; the second is the dielectric, which dissipates in proportion to frequency and dominates at microwaves. Cable (50 Ω) Diameter At 146 MHz At 435 MHz At 2.4 GHz RG-58 5 mm about 20 dB/100 m about 36 dB/100 m about 100 dB/100 m RG-213 10 mm about 9 dB/100 m about 16 dB/100 m about 44 dB/100 m LMR-400 (foam) 10 mm about 5 dB/100 m about 8.5 dB/100 m about 22 dB/100 m The values are typical; each manufacturer publishes its own table. A thicker cable loses less because the current is spread over more surface, and a foam dielectric loses less than a solid one. With 3 dB of loss, half of the power arrives; with 10 dB, one tenth."},{"t":"Mismatch and losses together","d":"Power in transmission lines","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/potencia-lineas#desadaptacion","x":"03 Mismatch and losses together If the line has losses and the load is also mismatched, the reflected wave is attenuated too on its trip back. This has two consequences. The first: the total loss is greater than that of the matched line, because the power that goes and comes back travels along the cable more than once. With \\( a \\) the attenuation of the matched line as a power ratio (\\( a = 10^{A/10} \\)): \\[ |\\Gamma_{in}| = \\frac{|\\Gamma_L|}{a} \\] \\[ \\frac{P_{in}}{P_L} = \\frac{a^2 - |\\Gamma_L|^2}{a\\,(1 - |\\Gamma_L|^2)} \\] The first expression gives the reflection seen from the transmitter, attenuated twice. The second is the total loss in the line, between the net power that goes in and the power the load receives; with \\( \\Gamma_L = 0 \\) it reduces to \\( a \\). The second consequence is misleading: the cable improves the SWR that the transmitter measures. An antenna with an SWR of 3 at the end of 20 m of RG-58 at 146 MHz looks like SWR 1.5 from the equipment. The transmitter is satisfied, but less than a third of the power reaches the antenna. That is why the SWR is measured, when possible, at the antenna feed point. Lab · how much power reaches the antenna A transmitter delivers 100 W net to a 50 Ω cable that ends in an antenna. The graph shows the power of the incident wave and of the reflected one along the cable, from the transmitter (on the left) to the antenna. The attenuations are typical catalog values. RG-58 RG-213 LMR-400 28 MHz 146 MHz 435 MHz 900 MHz 2.4 GHz Cabl"},{"t":"How much power a cable can take","d":"Power in transmission lines","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/potencia-lineas#limites","x":"04 How much power a cable can take A cable has two power limits. The thermal one: what it dissipates heats it, and the dielectric softens above a certain temperature; since the losses grow with frequency, the power rating drops with it. The voltage one: with standing waves, the voltage maxima are \\( (1 + |\\Gamma_L|) \\) times those of the matched line, and the dielectric can break down. Manufacturers give the power rating with the line matched and a derating factor for each SWR. For a transmitter, mismatch is also a risk: the reflected power returns to the output stage and the transistors can receive voltages higher than the design values. That is why solid-state transmitters automatically reduce their power when the SWR exceeds a threshold."},{"t":"Where it appears in electronics","d":"Power in transmission lines","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/potencia-lineas#electronica","x":"05 Where it appears in electronics Case What decides the useful power Amateur radio station The length and type of cable up to the antenna, more than the SWR at the equipment Cellular base station Thick foam cables, or the radio mounted next to the antenna to eliminate the cable Television antenna with amplifier The amplifier goes on the mast, ahead of the cable losses Broadcast transmitter SWR protection and a permanent directional meter The complete link, with the gain of the antennas and the path loss, is put together in the unit on antennas; the cable losses enter that budget by subtracting decibels, just as in the microwave links of the technician program."},{"t":"In the lab","d":"Power in transmission lines","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/potencia-lineas#laboratorio","x":"06 In the lab Activity 1 · Measuring the attenuation of a cable With a generator and a power meter, measure the output power of a roll of RG-58 loaded with 50 Ω at several frequencies. Plot the decibels per 100 m against frequency and separate the square-root term from the linear one. Activity 2 · The misleading SWR Terminate the same roll in 150 Ω and measure the SWR at its input with a directional meter. Repeat with a short section and compare with the SWR of the load. Activity 3 · Choosing the cable For an antenna 30 m from the equipment at 435 MHz, compare in catalogs the cost per meter and the power that arrives with RG-58, RG-213 and a foam cable."},{"t":"Common mistakes","d":"Power in transmission lines","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/potencia-lineas#errores","x":"07 Common mistakes Trusting the SWR measured at the equipment. A very lossy cable artificially improves it. Believing that all the reflected power is lost. What is lost is what the line and the generator dissipate, not the reflected power itself. Using the attenuation at one frequency for another. It grows with frequency, and at 2.4 GHz a thin cable is useless. Ignoring the voltage limit at high SWR. A cable that withstands the matched power can break down with a badly mismatched load."},{"t":"Self-assessment","d":"Power in transmission lines","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/potencia-lineas#autoevaluacion","x":"08 Self-assessment What fraction of the incident power is reflected at SWR 3? \\( |\\Gamma| = 0.5 \\), so \\( |\\Gamma|^2 = 25 \\)%. How much power reaches the matched load through a cable with 6 dB of loss if 100 W go in? \\( 100 / 10^{0.6} \\approx 25 \\) W. An antenna with SWR 3 is at the end of a cable with 4 dB of loss. What SWR does the transmitter measure? \\( |\\Gamma_{in}| = 0.5 / 10^{0.4} \\approx 0.2 \\), and the SWR is \\( 1.2 / 0.8 = 1.5 \\). Why does the attenuation of a coaxial cable grow faster than the square root of frequency at microwaves? Because the dielectric loss grows in proportion to frequency and comes to dominate over that of the conductors."},{"t":"Further reading","d":"Power in transmission lines","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/potencia-lineas#ampliar","x":"09 Further reading H. Ward Silver (ed.). The ARRL Antenna Book. ARRL, periodic editions. Its chapter on transmission lines covers losses with SWR using examples from real stations. David M. Pozar. Microwave Engineering. 4th ed., Wiley, 2011. Power in lossy lines and the rigorous definition of return loss and mismatch loss. Walter C. Johnson. Transmission Lines and Networks. McGraw-Hill, 1950. A classic on real lines, with the attenuation of conductors and dielectrics derived step by step."},{"t":"Why power is measured and not voltage","d":"RF power measurement","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/potencia-rf#porque","x":"01 Why power is measured and not voltage At radio frequency the voltage changes along a line with standing waves, and it depends on where it is measured. The power traveling toward the load, on the other hand, is well defined. That is why RF instruments measure power, in watts or in dBm, with a sensor that absorbs the signal and a meter that reads its output. The relationship between voltage, current and power on lines was covered in power on lines, in Transmission Media."},{"t":"The sensors","d":"RF power measurement","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/potencia-rf#sensores","x":"02 The sensors Sensor Principle Typical range Feature Thermistor (bolometer) The power heats a thermistor in a bridge, which is rebalanced with DC −20 to +10 dBm DC substitution: traceable, slow; today used in standards Thermocouple The power heats a thermoelectric junction −30 to +20 dBm Measures the true RMS value of any waveform Diode Rectifies the signal; in the square-law region the output is proportional to the power −70 to +20 dBm Very sensitive and fast; outside the square-law region it depends on the waveform Below about −20 dBm, the current of a detector diode is proportional to the square of the voltage, that is, to the power, for any modulation. Above that it begins to behave as a peak detector, and with modulated signals the result depends on the waveform. Modern diode sensors combine several paths or correct in software, and peak power sensors sample the envelope to measure radar pulses or bursts of digital communications."},{"t":"Calibration factor and mismatch uncertainty","d":"RF power measurement","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/potencia-rf#desadaptacion","x":"03 Calibration factor and mismatch uncertainty No sensor absorbs all the power it receives: part is reflected and part is lost before reaching the sensing element. The manufacturer measures its calibration factor as a function of frequency, and the meter applies it. What cannot be corrected without measuring phases is the interaction between the source and the sensor: the multiple reflections between the two add or subtract depending on the phase, and leave a mismatch uncertainty: \\[ M_u = 20 \\log_{10}\\big(1 \\pm |\\Gamma_s|\\,|\\Gamma_L|\\big)\\ \\ \\mathrm{dB} \\] \\[ |\\Gamma| = \\frac{\\mathrm{SWR} - 1}{\\mathrm{SWR} + 1} \\] \\( \\Gamma_s \\) is the reflection coefficient of the source and \\( \\Gamma_L \\) that of the sensor. With a source of SWR 1.5 and a sensor of 1.2, the uncertainty is ±0.16 dB, more than that of many meters. In practice it is often the largest component of the uncertainty budget. Lab · mismatch uncertainty The reflections between the source and the sensor. The chart shows the measured power relative to the true one as a function of the phase between the two reflections, which is not known: the band between the maximum and the minimum is the uncertainty. Source SWR = Sensor SWR = The way to reduce it is to improve the match on whichever side can be improved: a 10 dB attenuator between the source and the sensor improves the match seen by the sensor, at the cost of level. Mismatch uncertainty also affects the uncertainty budget of any RF measurement."},{"t":"In the laboratory","d":"RF power measurement","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/potencia-rf#laboratorio","x":"04 In the laboratory Activity 1 · Square-law region Use a diode sensor to measure a continuous signal and a 100% AM-modulated one at several levels, and observe where the reading stops matching the true power. Activity 2 · Mismatch Measure the power of a generator through a cable of variable length with a poorly matched load in the middle, and observe how the reading changes with the length. Activity 3 · Attenuator Repeat the measurement with a 10 dB attenuator at the generator output and compare the scatter."},{"t":"Common mistakes","d":"RF power measurement","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/potencia-rf#errores","x":"05 Common mistakes Forgetting the calibration factor for the working frequency. It can change by several percent across the band. Measuring modulated signals with a diode outside its square-law region. The reading depends on the waveform. Ignoring the mismatch. It is often the largest source of uncertainty. Exceeding the maximum power of the sensor. Diode sensors are damaged a few dBm above their range."},{"t":"Self-assessment","d":"RF power measurement","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/potencia-rf#autoevaluacion","x":"06 Self-assessment What reflection coefficient corresponds to an SWR of 2? 1/3. What is the mismatch uncertainty with an SWR of 2 at the source and 1.3 at the sensor? \\( |\\Gamma_s||\\Gamma_L| = 0.333 \\cdot 0.130 = 0.043 \\): from +0.37 to −0.39 dB. Which sensor would you use to measure the RMS value of a heavily modulated signal? A thermocouple, or a diode in its square-law region. Why does an attenuator reduce the mismatch uncertainty? Because the reflections returning toward the source pass through it twice and arrive attenuated: the sensor sees a better-matched source."},{"t":"Further reading","d":"RF power measurement","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/potencia-rf#ampliar","x":"07 Further reading Keysight Technologies. Fundamentals of RF and Microwave Power Measurements. Application Note 1449, in four parts, Keysight. The sensors, the meters and the calculation of the uncertainty. David M. Pozar. Microwave Engineering. 4th ed., Wiley, 2012. The reflection coefficient and mismatch in microwave networks. Clyde F. Coombs Jr. (ed.). Electronic Instrument Handbook. 3rd ed., McGraw-Hill, 1999. RF power meters and their sensors."},{"t":"Accidents have causes","d":"Techniques for preventing accidents","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/prevencion-accidentes#causas","x":"01 Accidents have causes Prevention starts from a simple idea: an accident is not bad luck but the result of causes that can be identified and eliminated. Three models shaped the way we think about it: Model Central idea Consequence for prevention Heinrich’s domino (1931) A chain of factors ends in the injury; unsafe acts and conditions are the central domino Removing one domino stops the chain Bird’s pyramid (1969) For every serious injury there are many minor ones, plus property damage and hundreds of incidents without consequences Incidents are warnings: recording them makes it possible to act sooner Reason’s Swiss cheese (1990) Every barrier has holes; the accident occurs when they line up Several independent barriers, and a search for the latent failures of the organization Reason’s model changed the focus: the operator’s error is usually the last failure in a series that began in design, maintenance or organizational decisions. Blaming the person does not eliminate those latent failures."},{"t":"Analytical and operational techniques","d":"Techniques for preventing accidents","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/prevencion-accidentes#tecnicas","x":"02 Analytical and operational techniques Prevention techniques are divided into analytical ones, which look for the causes, and operational ones, which act on them: Safety inspections: planned walk-throughs with checklists to detect hazardous conditions before they cause an accident. Accident and incident investigation: the causal tree, developed in France in the 1970s, starts from the event and asks, for each antecedent, what was necessary and sufficient for it to occur, until it reaches facts that can be corrected. It looks for facts, not culprits. Job analysis: dividing the task into steps and listing the hazards and controls for each one. Accident statistics: measuring in order to compare periods and sectors, and to see whether the measures work. Training, signage, standards and procedures: the operational techniques covered in industrial hygiene and safety and in the unit on safe work procedures."},{"t":"Accident rates","d":"Techniques for preventing accidents","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/prevencion-accidentes#indices","x":"03 Accident rates Counting accidents is not enough: a large company has more accidents than a small one even if it is safer. The rates relate them to the exposure: \\[ I_I = \\frac{\\text{lost-time accidents}}{\\text{workers}} \\times 10^3 \\] \\[ I_F = \\frac{\\text{lost-time accidents}}{\\text{hours worked}} \\times 10^6 \\] \\[ I_G = \\frac{\\text{workdays lost}}{\\text{hours worked}} \\times 10^3 \\] The incidence rate \\( I_I \\) counts accidents per thousand workers; the frequency rate \\( I_F \\), per million hours, corrects for the length of the working day; the severity rate \\( I_G \\), workdays lost per thousand hours. The average duration of the absences is the ratio of workdays lost to accidents. Lab · rates and pyramid of a plant One year of data from a plant. On the right, the rates; in the chart, Bird’s pyramid scaled to the recorded injuries, using the 1: 10: 30: 600 ratio of the original study, which is only indicative: each activity has its own. Workers = Hours per worker in the year = Lost-time accidents = Workdays lost = The Superintendency of Occupational Risks publishes incidence rates by economic activity, which make it possible to compare a plant with its sector. A low rate with many unrecorded incidents does not indicate safety, but a lack of information."},{"t":"In the laboratory","d":"Techniques for preventing accidents","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/prevencion-accidentes#laboratorio","x":"04 In the laboratory Activity 1 · Causal tree Take an accident described in a public report and build its causal tree as a group. Identify which of the facts could have been corrected and with what measure. Activity 2 · Inspection Design a checklist for an electronics laboratory, apply it and classify the findings by severity. Activity 3 · Incident log Propose a short form for recording no-injury incidents in the workshop and discuss how to prevent it from being used to look for culprits."},{"t":"Common mistakes","d":"Techniques for preventing accidents","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/prevencion-accidentes#errores","x":"05 Common mistakes Ending the investigation at human error. It is the starting point for asking why it was possible. Comparing numbers of accidents between companies of different sizes. Rates are what get compared. Ignoring incidents. They are the base of the pyramid and the cheapest information for prevention. Taking Bird’s ratio as a law. It is an average from one study; what matters is the shape, not the numbers."},{"t":"Self-assessment","d":"Techniques for preventing accidents","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/prevencion-accidentes#autoevaluacion","x":"06 Self-assessment A company of 400 workers had 10 lost-time accidents. What is its incidence rate? 25 per thousand workers. If those workers each worked 1800 hours, what is the frequency rate? \\( 10/720\\,000 \\times 10^6 \\approx 13.9 \\). What does the Swiss cheese model propose for prevention? Several independent barriers, and correcting the latent failures of the organization, not just the last error. What question guides the construction of a causal tree? For each fact: what was necessary and sufficient for it to occur."},{"t":"Further reading","d":"Techniques for preventing accidents","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/prevencion-accidentes#ampliar","x":"07 Further reading James Reason. Human Error. Cambridge University Press, 1990. The origin of the Swiss cheese model and the distinction between active and latent failures. Sidney Dekker. The Field Guide to Understanding 'Human Error'. 3rd ed., CRC Press, 2014. How to investigate accidents without stopping at individual blame. Frank E. Bird and George L. Germain. Practical Loss Control Leadership. International Loss Control Institute, 1985. The accident pyramid and loss control."},{"t":"The First Law: energy is conserved","d":"First and Second Laws","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/principios-termodinamica#primero","x":"01 The First Law: energy is conserved The internal energy of a system changes in two ways, and only two: the heat that enters and the work that is done. Written like that it seems little, and it is the most general conservation principle there is. \\[ \\Delta U = Q - W \\] With the usual convention: \\( Q \\) positive when heat enters the system, \\( W \\) positive when the system does work on its surroundings. In a complete cycle the system returns to its initial state, so \\( \\Delta U = 0 \\) and all the net heat has been converted into net work. Process What is held constant Consequence Isothermal The temperature \\( \\Delta U = 0 \\), so \\( Q = W \\) Isobaric The pressure \\( W = P\\Delta V \\) Isochoric The volume \\( W = 0 \\), all the heat goes into internal energy Adiabatic No heat exchange \\( Q = 0 \\), the work comes out of \\( U \\) For electronic equipment in steady state the balance is trivial and therefore useful: there is no mechanical work and no accumulation, so all the electrical power that goes in comes out as heat. A 500 W power supply feeding a computer heats the room with 500 W, no matter what it is computing. That is the starting point of any room-cooling calculation."},{"t":"The Second Law: conserving is not enough","d":"First and Second Laws","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/principios-termodinamica#segundo","x":"02 The Second Law: conserving is not enough The First Law does not forbid a cold cup from heating up by itself by cooling the air: energy would be conserved all the same. What forbids it is the Second, which introduces a quantity that never decreases in an isolated system: \\[ dS = \\frac{\\delta Q_{rev}}{T} \\qquad\\qquad \\Delta S_{universe} \\ge 0 \\] Entropy is what gives time its direction. All spontaneous processes —heat flowing down, a gas expanding, things mixing— go in the direction of increasing it. From this comes the limit that no machine can exceed. A heat engine that takes heat from the hot reservoir, at \\( T_c \\), and delivers it to the cold one, at \\( T_f \\), cannot have an efficiency greater than: \\[ \\eta_{Carnot} = 1 - \\frac{T_f}{T_c} \\] With temperatures in kelvin. It does not depend on the fluid, the design or the technology: it is a ceiling imposed by physics. And it would only be reached with reversible processes, which take infinite time. Lab · efficiency and its limits The same pair of temperatures, four different machines. The diagram shows the energy flows to scale. Look at the Peltier: it is the only one that does not come anywhere near its own Carnot limit, and there lies its whole story. heat engine · converts heat into work refrigerator · removes heat from the cold side heat pump · heats Peltier cell · solid-state cooling Cold reservoir temperature = 0 °C Hot reservoir temperature = 25 °C Why a heat sink cannot cool A heat sink is a passive exchanger: it"},{"t":"The thermodynamic cost of erasing a bit","d":"First and Second Laws","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/principios-termodinamica#landauer","x":"03 The thermodynamic cost of erasing a bit Here thermodynamics meets computing in a surprising way. Erasing information is an irreversible process: two possible states become one, and that loss of uncertainty is a decrease in the entropy of the system. For the universe not to violate the Second Law, that entropy has to appear in the surroundings as heat: \\[ E_{\\min} = k T \\ln 2 \\approx 2.9\\times10^{-21}\\ \\mathrm{J} \\ \\text{ at 300 K} \\] Landauer's limit, 1961. It is the minimum energy dissipated when erasing a bit, and it does not depend on the technology: it holds for transistors, for relays and for anything that gets invented. That is about three zeptojoules. A current transistor switches spending on the order of a femtojoule, so today's electronics is about three hundred thousand times above the limit. There is room, but the room is not infinite, and that floor explains why reversible computing is researched: if an operation does not destroy information, in principle it has no minimum energy cost. The connection with noise The same \\( kT \\) that appears here is the one in the diode equation, in Arrhenius and in thermal noise \\( \\overline{v^2} = 4kTRB \\). It is no coincidence: in all four cases what is being measured is how much energy thermal agitation brings, and that number —26 meV at room temperature— is the one that sets the scale for almost all of electronics."},{"t":"In the lab","d":"First and Second Laws","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/principios-termodinamica#laboratorio","x":"04 In the lab Activity 1 · Everything ends up as heat Power a resistor of known power rating inside an insulated container with water and record the temperature rise against time. Calculate the absorbed energy and compare it with the electrical energy delivered. Discuss where the difference went. Activity 2 · A real Peltier Power a Peltier cell with a heat sink on the hot face and measure the temperature of both faces and the electrical power consumed. Calculate the real COP and compare it with the Carnot COP for that same temperature difference. Activity 3 · Efficiency of a power supply Measure input and output power of a switching power supply with a variable load. Plot the efficiency against the load and calculate how much heat the enclosure has to remove in the worst case."},{"t":"Common mistakes","d":"First and Second Laws","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/principios-termodinamica#errores","x":"05 Common mistakes Believing the Carnot efficiency can be reached. It requires reversible processes, which take infinite time. Using Celsius in the Carnot formula. The temperatures go in kelvin, and with Celsius the result means nothing. Thinking a heat sink cools. It only transports heat, and for that it needs a temperature difference in its favor. Confusing COP with efficiency. The COP of a refrigerator is greater than 1 without violating anything: it is not an efficiency. Believing entropy never decreases. It decreases locally all the time; what does not decrease is that of the universe. Assuming an efficient device does not heat up. All the power that goes in and does not come out as useful signal ends up as heat."},{"t":"Self-assessment","d":"First and Second Laws","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/principios-termodinamica#autoevaluacion","x":"06 Self-assessment How much heat does a computer that consumes 500 W dissipate? 500 W. There is no mechanical work and no accumulation: all the electrical power ends up as heat. Which quantity gives time its direction? Entropy: it never decreases in an isolated system, and all spontaneous processes increase it. What does the Carnot efficiency depend on? Only on the two temperatures, in kelvin. Not on the fluid or the design. Why can a COP be 4? Because it is not an efficiency: it measures heat moved per unit of work, and moving heat costs less than creating it. How much energy does it cost to erase a bit? At least \\( kT\\ln 2 \\), about 2.9 zeptojoules at 300 K. That is Landauer's limit. Can you cool something below ambient with a heat sink? No. It would require heat to flow uphill by itself. For that you need a machine that consumes work."},{"t":"Further reading","d":"First and Second Laws","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/principios-termodinamica#ampliar","x":"07 Further reading Raymond A. Serway and John W. Jewett. Physics for Scientists and Engineers with Modern Physics. 10th ed., Cengage, 2019 (Spanish edition, “Física para ciencias e ingeniería”). First and second laws, heat engines, the Carnot cycle and entropy. Enrico Fermi. Thermodynamics. Dover, 1956 (Spanish edition, “Termodinámica”, Eudeba, reissue). One hundred and fifty pages that explain the whole subject. It remains unsurpassed as a first serious reading. Charles H. Bennett. The Thermodynamics of Computation — a Review. International Journal of Theoretical Physics, vol. 21, 1982. Where Landauer's limit comes from and what reversible computing means."},{"t":"Three axioms and nothing more","d":"Probability","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/probabilidad#axiomas","x":"01 Three axioms and nothing more The whole theory is built on three rules. A random experiment has a set of possible outcomes —the sample space \\( S \\)—, and every subset of that space, called an event, is assigned a number between 0 and 1: \\[ 0 \\le P(A) \\le 1 \\qquad P(S) = 1 \\qquad P(A\\cup B) = P(A) + P(B) \\ \\text{ if they are mutually exclusive} \\] Everything else follows from these three by deduction, including the general rule \\( P(A\\cup B) = P(A)+P(B)-P(A\\cap B) \\): when you add separately, the intersection is counted twice and you have to subtract it once. \\[ P(A\\mid B) = \\frac{P(A\\cap B)}{P(B)} \\qquad\\qquad A \\perp B \\iff P(A\\cap B) = P(A)\\,P(B) \\] Conditional probability shrinks the sample space to the cases in which \\( B \\) occurred. Two events are independent when knowing that one occurred does not change the probability of the other. Independent is not the same as mutually exclusive They get confused all the time, and they are almost opposites. Two mutually exclusive events cannot occur together, so knowing that one occurred tells you with certainty that the other did not: that is maximum dependence. Two independent events, on the other hand, can occur together, and the probability that both occur is precisely the product of the two."},{"t":"Independence and reliability","d":"Probability","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/probabilidad#confiabilidad","x":"02 Independence and reliability The most direct application in engineering is to calculate whether a system works from whether its parts work. With components that fail independently: Configuration Works if Reliability Series All work \\( R = R_1 R_2 \\cdots R_n \\) Parallel At least one works \\( R = 1 - (1-R_1)\\cdots(1-R_n) \\) The first row is relentless: a hundred components in series, each with 99.9 % reliability, give a system with 90.5 %. That is why equipment with many parts needs each one to be much better than the whole. And the second row is the basis of redundancy: two units in parallel with 95 % each give 99.75 %. Independence is almost never completely true Redundancy works as long as the failures are independent, and often they are not: two redundant power supplies fed from the same line go down together if that line is cut, and two identical boards share the same design flaw. This is called a common-cause failure, and it is the reason why critical systems require diverse redundancy: not the same thing twice, but two different solutions."},{"t":"Bayes’ theorem","d":"Probability","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/probabilidad#bayes","x":"03 Bayes’ theorem The theorem lets you flip a conditional probability: to go from “how often the test comes out positive when the part is defective” to what you actually want to know, “how often the part is defective when the test came out positive.” \\[ P(A\\mid B) = \\frac{P(B\\mid A)\\,P(A)}{P(B)} \\qquad\\qquad P(B) = P(B\\mid A)P(A) + P(B\\mid \\bar{A})P(\\bar{A}) \\] The second one is the law of total probability: the denominator is built by adding up all the ways of arriving at \\( B \\). It is what makes the result depend on how frequent \\( A \\) is to begin with. Lab · a test with false positives A quality-control test with its sensitivity and specificity, applied to a batch. You move the proportion of defective parts and watch what happens to the confidence in a positive result. Parts that are actually defective = 1 % Sensitivity · detects a defective part = 99 % Specificity · passes a good part = 99 % The result that baffles everyone A test that is right 99 % of the time, applied to a batch with 1 % defective parts, produces as many false positives as true positives: of every two parts it flags as bad, one is fine. The test is not badly made. What happens is that the good parts are ninety-nine times more numerous, so their 1 % error contributes as many cases as the 99 % accuracy on a tiny group. The practical conclusion is that in populations where the defect is rare, every positive has to be confirmed."},{"t":"In the lab","d":"Probability","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/probabilidad#laboratorio","x":"04 In the lab Activity 1 · Frequency and probability Roll a die two hundred times recording the results, and plot the relative frequency of each face against the number of rolls. Observe how it approaches 1/6 and estimate how many rolls are needed to be within 1 %. Activity 2 · Reliability of a chain Calculate the reliability of a piece of equipment with twenty components in series, each with 99.5 %. Add redundancy to the weakest component and recalculate. Discuss how much it would cost to improve each one instead of duplicating one. Activity 3 · Building the Bayes table For an imaginary batch of ten thousand parts with 2 % defective and a test with 95 % sensitivity and 90 % specificity, build the table of the four possible cases and calculate the positive predictive value by counting parts, without using the formula."},{"t":"Common mistakes","d":"Probability","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/probabilidad#errores","x":"05 Common mistakes Confusing \\( P(A\\mid B) \\) with \\( P(B\\mid A) \\). It is the mistake that makes Bayes necessary. Taking independent for mutually exclusive. They are almost opposites. Multiplying probabilities of dependent events. The product holds only if they are independent. Ignoring the base rate. Without knowing how frequent the defect is, a positive does not mean anything. Assuming that redundancy always multiplies reliability. It does not, if there is a common-cause failure. Believing that after many heads it is “due” to come up tails. The coin has no memory."},{"t":"Self-assessment","d":"Probability","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/probabilidad#autoevaluacion","x":"06 Self-assessment When are two events independent? When \\( P(A\\cap B) = P(A)P(B) \\): knowing that one occurred does not change the probability of the other. What is the reliability of a hundred components in series at 99.9 %? \\( 0.999^{100} = 90.5\\ \\% \\). In series, the reliability of the whole is always worse than that of the worst part. What is Bayes’ theorem for? To invert a conditional: to go from the probability of the result given the state, to that of the state given the result. Why does a 99 % test give so many false positives? Because if the good parts are ninety-nine times more numerous, their 1 % of error contributes as many cases as the 99 % accuracy on the few defective ones. What is a common-cause failure? One that takes down the redundant units at the same time, such as a shared power supply or a repeated design flaw. What reliability do two 95 % units in parallel give? 99.75 %: the system fails only if both fail, and \\( 0.05^2 = 0.0025 \\)."},{"t":"Further reading","d":"Probability","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/probabilidad#ampliar","x":"07 Further reading Douglas C. Montgomery and George C. Runger. Applied Statistics and Probability for Engineers. 7th ed. (Spanish edition, “Probabilidad y estadística aplicadas a la ingeniería”), Wiley, 2018. All of Chapter 2: axioms, conditional probability, independence and Bayes with engineering problems. Sheldon Ross. Introduction to Probability and Statistics for Engineers and Scientists. 5th ed. (Spanish edition, “Introducción a la probabilidad y estadística para ingenieros”), Elsevier, 2018. A more formal treatment, with good exercises on total probability and compound events. Patrick D. T. O'Connor and Andre Kleyner. Practical Reliability Engineering. 5th ed., Wiley, 2012. System reliability, redundancy and common-cause failures applied to real equipment."},{"t":"From the smokestack to the planet","d":"Environmental problems and their evolution","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/problemas-ambientales#escalas","x":"01 From the smokestack to the planet Environmental problems have changed scale as human activity has grown. The first ones were local and visible; today’s are global, slow, and unevenly distributed between those who cause them and those who suffer them. Scale Example Feature Local The London smog of December 1952, which caused thousands of deaths in a few days Cause and effect in the same place and at the same time Regional Acid rain in Europe and North America in the 1970s and 1980s The emissions of one country damage the forests and lakes of another Global The ozone hole and climate change Everyone contributes and everyone is affected, with a delay of decades In Argentina there are examples at every scale: the pollution of the Matanza–Riachuelo basin, which led to the 2008 ruling of the Supreme Court in the Mendoza case; the loss of native forests in the Chaco, which led to the Forest Law 26,331, and the retreat of the glaciers, protected by Law 26,639. The chemical bases of pollution were covered in chemistry and the environment, in General Chemistry."},{"t":"The ozone layer: a global problem half solved","d":"Environmental problems and their evolution","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/problemas-ambientales#ozono","x":"02 The ozone layer: a global problem half solved Chlorofluorocarbons (CFCs), used in refrigeration, aerosols and in cleaning electronic boards, release chlorine in the stratosphere and destroy the ozone that filters ultraviolet radiation. In 1985 the measurement of the hole over Antarctica was published, and in 1987 the Montreal Protocol was signed, which progressively phased out their production. It is considered the most successful environmental agreement: scientific assessments foresee the layer recovering by the middle of the century. The electronics industry had to change its cleaning solvents, which is why no-clean fluxes are used today."},{"t":"Climate change","d":"Environmental problems and their evolution","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/problemas-ambientales#clima","x":"03 Climate change Carbon dioxide, methane and other gases absorb the infrared radiation emitted by the Earth. As their concentration increases, the surface has to warm up in order to re-emit as much energy as it receives from the Sun. CO₂ went from about 280 parts per million before industrialization to more than 420 today. Its effect is measured with the radiative forcing, the energy imbalance it produces, and the response of temperature with the climate sensitivity: \\[ \\Delta F = 5.35 \\ln \\frac{C}{C_0}\\ \\ \\mathrm{W/m^2} \\] \\[ \\Delta T_{eq} = S\\, \\frac{\\ln(C/C_0)}{\\ln 2} \\] \\( C_0 \\) = 280 ppm. \\( S \\) is the equilibrium climate sensitivity, the warming when CO₂ doubles; the IPCC estimates 3 °C as the best value, with a likely range of 2.5 to 4 °C. The forcing is logarithmic: each doubling adds the same amount. Lab · CO₂ concentration and equilibrium warming The equilibrium warming corresponding to CO₂ alone, for a given concentration and climate sensitivity. The band is the range of 2.5 to 4 °C per doubling; the dot, the chosen concentration. CO₂ concentration = Climate sensitivity = The warming observed so far, a little more than 1 °C, is different from the equilibrium warming: the oceans take decades to warm up, the aerosols from pollution cool, and other gases such as methane warm. But the logarithmic relationship and the sensitivity are what determine where the system is heading."},{"t":"Planetary boundaries","d":"Environmental problems and their evolution","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/problemas-ambientales#limites","x":"04 Planetary boundaries In 2009, a group of scientists led by Johan Rockström proposed nine planetary boundaries: processes of the Earth system with thresholds that, if crossed, can lead to abrupt changes. Besides climate and ozone, they include biodiversity loss, the nitrogen and phosphorus cycles, land and freshwater use, ocean acidification, aerosols and novel entities, such as plastics and synthetic compounds. Waste electrical and electronic equipment, which is covered in the unit on ICT and the environment, falls into this last category because of its metals and flame retardants."},{"t":"In the laboratory","d":"Environmental problems and their evolution","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/problemas-ambientales#laboratorio","x":"05 In the laboratory Activity 1 · The Keeling curve Download the CO₂ concentration series from the Mauna Loa observatory, plot it and fit the trend and the annual oscillation. Estimate the growth rate per decade. Activity 2 · A CO₂ sensor Build a CO₂ meter with a non-dispersive infrared sensor and a microcontroller, and record the concentration in a classroom over the course of a class. Activity 3 · A local problem Choose an environmental problem of the region and describe its scale, its causes, whom it affects and which rules regulate it."},{"t":"Common mistakes","d":"Environmental problems and their evolution","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/problemas-ambientales#errores","x":"06 Common mistakes Confusing the ozone hole with climate change. They are different problems, with different causes and effects. Expecting the warming to be proportional to the concentration. The forcing grows with the logarithm. Reading the equilibrium warming as the current one. The ocean delays the response by several decades. Thinking that a global problem has no local component. Its causes and its damages are local and unequal."},{"t":"Self-assessment","d":"Environmental problems and their evolution","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/problemas-ambientales#autoevaluacion","x":"07 Self-assessment What forcing does doubling the preindustrial CO₂ produce? \\( 5.35 \\ln 2 \\approx 3.7 \\) W/m². With a sensitivity of 3 °C, what equilibrium warming corresponds to 420 ppm? \\( 3 \\cdot \\ln 1.5/\\ln 2 \\approx 1.75 \\) °C, from CO₂ alone. Which international agreement phased out CFCs? The Montreal Protocol, of 1987. Why is acid rain a regional problem? Because the emissions travel hundreds of kilometers and damage ecosystems far from the source, often in another country."},{"t":"Further reading","d":"Environmental problems and their evolution","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/problemas-ambientales#ampliar","x":"08 Further reading IPCC. Climate Change 2021: The Physical Science Basis. Sixth Assessment Report, Working Group I, Cambridge University Press, 2021. The reference assessment on forcing, sensitivity and scenarios. David Archer. Global Warming: Understanding the Forecast. 2nd ed., Wiley, 2012. The physics of the greenhouse effect explained with simple models, for science students. Johan Rockström et al. A safe operating space for humanity. Nature, 461, 2009. The article that proposed the planetary boundaries."},{"t":"What the degree authorizes, and what it does not","d":"The profession in Argentina and professional ethics","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/profesion-etica#titulo","x":"01 What the degree authorizes, and what it does not A university degree certifies an education. What entitles you to practice is the professional license (matrícula), which is granted and overseen by the professional councils. In electronics and telecommunications, at the national level, the body is COPITEC, the Professional Council of Telecommunications, Electronics and Computer Engineering; the provinces have councils and professional associations of their own. Concept What it means Scope of practice (incumbencias) The activities reserved to the degree: designing, directing, certifying, appraising, giving expert opinions. They define what each profession may sign Professional license (matrícula) The registration that authorizes practice and subjects the professional to the council’s ethical and disciplinary oversight Technical supervision Taking responsibility for ensuring that a work or installation is carried out according to the design and the standards Professional commission (encomienda profesional) The formal registration of the work with the council, which records who is responsible for what Signing is not a formality A professional signature carries civil liability —repairing the damage caused—, criminal liability when there is an offense or gross negligence, and administrative liability before the council, which can suspend the license. You do not sign “as a favor” or lend your signature: besides being an ethical breach, whoever signs is answerable even if they did"},{"t":"The code of ethics, in four obligations","d":"The profession in Argentina and professional ethics","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/profesion-etica#etica","x":"02 The code of ethics, in four obligations The codes of the Argentine professional councils, and the international ones of IEEE and similar bodies, agree on the order of loyalties: 1 · To society Public safety, health and welfare come first, above the interest of the client or the employer. It is the only hierarchy that is not negotiable. 2 · To the client or employer Competence, diligence, confidentiality and honesty about costs and deadlines. Accepting only work for which you are qualified. 3 · To colleagues Fair competition, acknowledgment of authorship, no unfounded disparagement, and not taking on a job by displacing another professional without notifying them. 4 · To the profession Keeping up to date, training those who are starting out, not lending your signature, and reporting illegal practice. The hard part is not understanding these rules but applying them when they collide with each other. That is why it helps to have thought through the cases before they arise."},{"t":"Dilemmas to decide on before you face them","d":"The profession in Argentina and professional ethics","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/profesion-etica#dilemas","x":"03 Dilemmas to decide on before you face them Lab · what would you do There is no obvious answer in every case, but there are clearly better and clearly worse options. Choose one and look at the feedback. Another case"},{"t":"Three cases that the whole profession studies","d":"The profession in Argentina and professional ethics","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/profesion-etica#casos","x":"04 Three cases that the whole profession studies Challenger (1986). The night before the launch, engineer Roger Boisjoly and his colleagues warned that the rocket seals were not reliable in cold weather. The decision was made anyway. Seven people died. The case is studied for the organizational pressure on technical judgment and for the difference between “it hasn’t been proven to fail” and “it has been proven not to fail.” Therac-25 (1985-1987). A software-controlled radiotherapy machine delivered massive overdoses to six patients, at least three of whom died. The causes: removal of the mechanical interlocks in reliance on software alone, a race condition in the code, reuse of software from an earlier machine, and a lack of risk analysis. It is the reference case in the safety of embedded systems. Diesel engines with cheating software (2015). A program detected the test situation and changed the engine’s behavior to pass the emissions measurement. It was not a mistake: it was a deliberate development, signed off by engineers. It shows that the most frequent ethical problem is not incompetence but obedience. The pattern that repeats In almost every serious case there was someone who saw the problem and said so, and a chain of decisions that diluted it. That is why the codes require putting technical objections on record in writing: it is not defensive bureaucracy, it is the only way for the warning to survive the meeting."},{"t":"A gender perspective in engineering","d":"The profession in Argentina and professional ethics","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/profesion-etica#generos","x":"05 A gender perspective in engineering Engineering remains one of the degree programs with the lowest female participation, and electronics and computing are among the most uneven of the lot. The figures vary by year and source, but the trend is consistent across the region: women are a minority among those who enroll, and the gap widens in decision-making positions. The causes that research identifies are cumulative, not a single one: Before university: family and school expectations about what is “for boys.” During: a lack of role models, hostile or condescending environments, group work in which roles are assigned by gender. Afterwards: bias in hiring and promotion, and work organization that penalizes family caregiving, which falls disproportionately on women. It is not only a matter of fairness: a homogeneous team designs for people like itself. The consequences are documented —from crash-test dummies based on male bodies to recognition systems that work worse for certain people—, and they are engineering failures, not merely failures of representation."},{"t":"Contemporary issues","d":"The profession in Argentina and professional ethics","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/profesion-etica#contemporaneas","x":"06 Contemporary issues Topic The professional question Personal data What is collected, for what purpose, for how long and who has access. In Argentina, Law 25,326 on personal data protection applies Cybersecurity An insecure connected device is a risk to third parties, not only to its owner Artificial intelligence Who answers when a system makes a decision: whoever trained it, whoever integrated it or whoever used it Sustainability Designing to last and to be repaired, as seen in sustainable development Continuing education Keeping up to date is an ethical obligation, not a career option"},{"t":"Activities","d":"The profession in Argentina and professional ethics","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/profesion-etica#laboratorio","x":"07 Activities Activity 1 · Read the full code Download the code of ethics of the relevant professional council and choose three articles that could apply to a situation experienced in an internship or on a job. Explain how. Activity 2 · Reconstruct a case Research the Therac-25 and build a timeline of the technical and organizational decisions that led to each accident. Identify at what point a different decision would have prevented it. Activity 3 · Write the objection Draft, in half a page, the technical memo with which an objection to an unsafe design would be put on record: facts, risk, standard violated, proposed alternative and a request for a written reply. It is an exercise you will be glad to have done before you need it."},{"t":"Ideas worth revisiting","d":"The profession in Argentina and professional ethics","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/profesion-etica#errores","x":"08 Ideas worth revisiting “My degree entitles me.” It entitles you to the license; the degree certifies the education. “If I’m ordered to, the responsibility is the boss’s.” A signature is personal and cannot be delegated. “Ethics is common sense.” Common sense does not resolve conflicts between loyalties; the code does establish a hierarchy. “Meeting the standard is enough.” The standard is the floor, not the ceiling: there are situations where meeting it is not enough to be safe. Believing that a lack of diversity is a problem foreign to engineering: it affects what is designed and for whom it works."},{"t":"Self-assessment","d":"The profession in Argentina and professional ethics","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/profesion-etica#autoevaluacion","x":"09 Self-assessment What is the difference between a degree and a professional license? The degree certifies the academic education; the license, granted by the professional council, authorizes practice and subjects the professional to ethical and disciplinary oversight. What is the first obligation according to the codes of ethics? The safety, health and welfare of society, above the interest of the client or the employer. What kinds of liability does the person who signs a project assume? Civil, criminal and administrative before the professional council. What does the Therac-25 case teach? That in systems where a failure can harm people, safety cannot be entrusted to software alone: independent interlocks, risk analysis and specific testing are needed. Why is it a good idea to put a technical objection in writing? Because a verbal warning gets diluted in the chain of decisions. A written one forces a reply and records who knew what and when. Why is a lack of diversity in teams also a technical problem? Because a homogeneous team tends to design for people like its members, and that produces products that work worse —or do not work— for everyone else."},{"t":"Further reading","d":"The profession in Argentina and professional ethics","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/profesion-etica#ampliar","x":"10 Further reading Mike W. Martin and Roland Schinzinger. Ethics in Engineering. McGraw-Hill (Spanish edition: “Ética en la ingeniería”). The most widely used textbook: decision frameworks and dozens of analyzed cases. Nancy G. Leveson and Clark S. Turner. An Investigation of the Therac-25 Accidents. IEEE Computer, 1993 (free to read). The technical investigation of the case, written with a rigor that made it required reading. Diane Vaughan. The Challenger Launch Decision. University of Chicago Press, 1996. How an organization normalizes deviance until the risk stops being seen. CONFEDI. Libro Rojo: propuesta de estándares de segunda generación para la acreditación de carreras de ingeniería. Consejo Federal de Decanos de Ingeniería (Argentina’s Federal Council of Engineering Deans), 2018 (in Spanish). Defines the competencies —among them the social and ethical ones— expected of an Argentine engineer."},{"t":"Every variable has an address","d":"Advanced programming in C","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/programacion-avanzada#direcciones","x":"01 Every variable has an address A variable is a name for a place in memory. That place has an address, and C lets you work with it directly, something most languages hide. Operator Reads as What it gives &x address of x Where x is stored *p contents of p The value at the address that p holds int *p; p is a pointer to int Declares a variable that holds addresses A pointer is just another variable It takes up space in memory, has its own address and can be assigned a value. The only special thing is that this value is an address, and that the type says how big the pointed-to object is: that is what makes p++ advance 4 bytes on an int* and 1 byte on a char*. Lab · memory, step by step Walk through the program with the slider and watch what happens in memory on each line. Array and pointer arithmetic Pass by reference: swapping two variables Dynamic memory with malloc and free Step 0"},{"t":"Arrays, strings and pointers","d":"Advanced programming in C","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/programacion-avanzada#arreglos","x":"02 Arrays, strings and pointers In C the name of an array is the address of its first element. That is why these four expressions mean the same thing: v[i] ≡ *(v + i) ≡ *(i + v) ≡ i[v] The last one is a curiosity best left unused, but it shows how far the square brackets are merely a convenient notation for pointer arithmetic. The array does not know its own size C does not check bounds. Writing to v[10] of a 5-element array compiles without complaint and overwrites whatever is next to it: another variable, the function’s return address, anything at all. That is the cause of half the security flaws in the history of software, and it is why you must always pass the size along with the pointer. A string is an array of char ended by a zero byte. That zero is not decorative: it is the only thing that tells strlen or printf where the text ends."},{"t":"Pass by value and pass by reference","d":"Advanced programming in C","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/programacion-avanzada#funciones","x":"03 Pass by value and pass by reference C always passes arguments by value: the function receives a copy. If something in the caller has to be modified, you pass its address: void double_it(int *x) { *x = *x * 2; } … double_it(&a); /* a is now twice as large */ Arrays seem to be an exception, but they are not: what gets passed is the address of the first element, so the function can modify the contents. That is why void f(int v[]) and void f(int *v) are exactly the same thing to the compiler. Function pointers You can also store the address of a function and call it through that address. This is how dispatch tables, callbacks and state machines are built, and it is the mechanism behind a microcontroller’s interrupt vectors."},{"t":"Dynamic memory","d":"Advanced programming in C","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/programacion-avanzada#dinamica","x":"04 Dynamic memory Region Who manages it Lives until Global and static variables The compiler The program ends Stack (local variables) Automatic The function returns Heap (malloc) The programmer Someone calls free int *v = malloc(n * sizeof(int)); if (v == NULL) { /* out of memory */ } … free(v); v = NULL; The three rules that prevent almost all problems: always check that malloc did not return NULL, free exactly once whatever was allocated, and set the pointer to NULL after freeing it so that a later use fails immediately instead of silently corrupting memory. The four classic errors Memory leak: allocating and not freeing. The program grows until the system kills it. Dangling pointer: using memory that has already been freed. Double free: calling free twice with the same pointer. Uninitialized pointer: using a pointer that never received a valid address. In an embedded device that runs for months without restarting, a memory leak of a few bytes per hour ends in a failure after weeks. That is why many embedded projects flatly forbid malloc after startup."},{"t":"Modularizing","d":"Advanced programming in C","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/programacion-avanzada#modular","x":"05 Modularizing A serious program does not live in a single file. The criterion is to separate interface from implementation: sensor.h: what can be used —prototypes, types and constants—, protected against multiple inclusion with #ifndef SENSOR_H. sensor.c: how it is built. Whatever is not declared in the header is marked static and stays private to the file. Each.c is compiled separately and the linker joins the object files. That organization is what lets you replace one sensor module with another without touching the rest, and it is the basis of the libraries used in building software."},{"t":"In the lab","d":"Advanced programming in C","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/programacion-avanzada#laboratorio","x":"06 In the lab Exercise 1 · Seeing the addresses for real Write a program that prints, with %p, the addresses of a global variable, a local one, one allocated with malloc and a string constant. Sort them and deduce the memory map of the process. Exercise 2 · Implementing strlen and strcpy Write them using only pointers, without square brackets. Compare with the standard library versions and measure times with long strings. Exercise 3 · Hunting a memory leak Compile a program with a malloc and no free and run it under valgrind. Interpret the report, fix the program and check again that no memory is leaked."},{"t":"Common mistakes","d":"Advanced programming in C","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/programacion-avanzada#errores","x":"07 Common mistakes Confusing *p with p. One is the contents; the other, the address. Returning the address of a local variable. Once the function returns, that memory is no longer valid. Forgetting the zero byte when building a string by hand. Allocating the wrong size: malloc(n) instead of malloc(n * sizeof(int)). Comparing strings with ==, which compares addresses and not contents: you have to use strcmp. Using sizeof on a pointer expecting the size of the array: it gives the size of the pointer."},{"t":"Self-assessment","d":"Advanced programming in C","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/programacion-avanzada#autoevaluacion","x":"08 Self-assessment If int v[5] starts at 0x1000, what address is v + 2 ? 0x1008: pointer arithmetic advances in steps of sizeof(int), that is, 4 bytes per element. What is the difference between char s[] = \"data\" and char *s = \"data\" ? The first is a modifiable 5-byte array, a copy of the text. The second is a pointer to a read-only constant: writing to it is undefined behavior. Why can’t void f(int x) modify the caller’s variable? Because it receives a copy. You have to pass the address: void f(int *x). What happens if you call free twice with the same pointer? Undefined behavior: in general it corrupts the allocator’s internal structures. Setting the pointer to NULL after freeing it prevents this, because free(NULL) is safe. What is the value of sizeof(v) inside void f(int v[]) ? The size of a pointer —4 or 8 bytes—, not that of the array. The size has to be passed as a separate parameter. What is the point of declaring a function static in a.c file? To make it private to that file and keep it out of linking: it avoids name clashes and makes clear what is interface and what is implementation."},{"t":"Further reading","d":"Advanced programming in C","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/programacion-avanzada#ampliar","x":"09 Further reading Brian W. Kernighan and Dennis M. Ritchie. The C Programming Language. 2nd ed. (Spanish edition, “El lenguaje de programación C”), Pearson, 1991. Chapter 5, on pointers and arrays, is still the best written explanation of the subject. Randal E. Bryant and David R. O'Hallaron. Computer Systems: A Programmer's Perspective. 3rd ed., Pearson, 2015. What really happens in memory when a C program runs. Peter van der Linden. Expert C Programming: Deep C Secrets. Prentice Hall, 1994. The traps of the language explained with humor and real cases. Herbert Schildt. C: The Complete Reference. 4th ed. (Spanish edition, “C: manual de referencia”), McGraw-Hill, 2001. A quick reference to the standard library, handy to keep next to the keyboard."},{"t":"From jumps to blocks","d":"Structured programming language","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/programacion-estructurada#paradigma","x":"01 From jumps to blocks The first programs were written with jumps: “if such-and-such happens, go to line 340.” They worked, but beyond a certain size nobody could follow the thread, because any line could be reached from any other. In 1968 Edsger Dijkstra published a letter with a title that became famous, Go To Statement Considered Harmful: the unrestricted jump makes it impossible to reason about a program. Structured programming is the answer: write everything with the three structures of Böhm and Jacopini, each with a single entry and a single exit, and split the problem into functions that can be understood, tested and reused separately. The program reads from top to bottom and each block can be replaced by a black box. Why C C, created by Dennis Ritchie at Bell Labs between 1969 and 1973 to write Unix, is a structured language that also lets you see the machine: addresses, bits and sizes. That is why it is the language of operating systems and microcontrollers, and why it is taught in electronic engineering. The basic elements of the language are covered in basic elements of the C language; here the focus is on how to think about a structured program and what the machine does with it."},{"t":"From source to executable, hands-on","d":"Structured programming language","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/programacion-estructurada#compilar","x":"02 From source to executable, hands-on The gcc compiler lets you stop at each stage. It is worth doing once to lose the fear of error messages, which tell you at which stage they failed: gcc -E hello.c -o hello.i # preprocess only: see what is left of the #include lines gcc -S hello.c -o hello.s # compile to assembly gcc -c hello.c -o hello.o # assemble: object code gcc hello.o -o hello # link with the standard library gcc -Wall -Wextra -std=c11 hello.c -o hello # all in one, with warnings Message Stage Typical cause fatal error: stdio.h: No such file Preprocessor Header name misspelled expected ';' before … Compiler A semicolon is missing on the previous line implicit declaration of function Compiler A function is used without a prototype or #include undefined reference to 'sqrt' Linker The library is missing: add -lm Warnings are errors that have not blown up yet Always compile with -Wall -Wextra and do not leave any warning without understanding it. An if (x = 5) compiles, but the compiler warns: it is an assignment, not a comparison."},{"t":"Types and expressions: what C does without warning","d":"Structured programming language","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/programacion-estructurada#expresiones","x":"03 Types and expressions: what C does without warning In C every expression has a type, and the type decides the operation. 7 / 2 is an integer division and gives 3, because both operands are integers; 7.0 / 2 gives 3.5. The result does not depend on where it is stored: double x = 7 / 2; stores 3.0, because the division was done first. When types are mixed, C applies the usual arithmetic conversions: types smaller than int are promoted to int, and in an operation between two different types the “smaller” one is converted to the “larger” one. The best-known trap: int a = -1; unsigned int b = 1; if (a < b) // false! printf ( \"logical\" ); When an int is compared with an unsigned int, −1 is converted to unsigned and becomes 4,294,967,295 (with 32 bits), which is greater than 1. The bits did not change: the reading changed, exactly as in number systems. Operators, from highest to lowest precedence Associativity () [] ->. and the postfix ++ -- left to right ! ~ - * & sizeof, prefix operators and casts (type) right to left * / % left to right + - left to right << >> left to right < <= > >=, then == != left to right &, then ^, then | left to right &&, then || left to right ?: and the assignments = += -= … right to left There is a surprise in the table: & has lower precedence than ==. That is why if (x & 0x08 == 0) does not do what it seems to; write if ((x & 0x08) == 0). When in doubt, use parentheses."},{"t":"Control structures in C","d":"Structured programming language","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/programacion-estructurada#control","x":"04 Control structures in C Structure Pseudocode C Simple or double selection If … then … else if (c) { … } else { … } Multiple selection Case switch (x) { case 1: … break; default: … } Iteration with a pre-test condition While while (c) { … } Iteration with a post-test condition Repeat … until do { … } while (c); (note: the condition is one for continuing, not for stopping) Iteration with a counter For for (i = 0; i < n; i++) { … } In C any nonzero value is true. break exits the loop or the switch and continue jumps to the next iteration: they break the single-exit rule, but in a controlled way, and they are accepted when they simplify things. goto exists; in structured code it is only tolerated for leaving several nested levels when an error occurs. The switch falls through If a case does not end with break, execution continues into the next one. Sometimes this is done on purpose to group cases; almost always it is an oversight."},{"t":"Functions and parameter passing","d":"Structured programming language","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/programacion-estructurada#funciones","x":"05 Functions and parameter passing A function is an algorithm with a name, inputs (parameters) and an output (return value). In C all parameters are passed by value: the function receives a copy and cannot modify the original variable. To let it do so, you pass the variable’s address, a pointer: void swap ( int *p, int *q) { int aux = *p; // what is at address p *p = *q; *q = aux; }... swap (&x, &y); // the addresses of x and y are passed For the same reason scanf(\"%d\", &n) takes &: it needs to know where to write. Pointers are covered in complex data containers. Variable Scope (where it is visible) Lifetime (how long it lives) Local The block where it is declared While the block executes: it is created and destroyed on each call Local static The block where it is declared The whole program: it keeps its value between calls Global The whole file (and others, with extern ) The whole program Global static Only its own file The whole program"},{"t":"The call stack: where local variables live","d":"Structured programming language","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/programacion-estructurada#pila","x":"06 The call stack: where local variables live How can a function have “its own” n if it calls itself? Each call creates in memory a frame (also called a stack frame ) with its parameters, its local variables and the address to which it must return. Frames are stacked: the last one in is the first one out. On return, the frame is discarded and its variables cease to exist. Follow the execution of a recursive factorial: Lab · the call stack, live fact( ) Next step Step back Reset Stack (the top frame is the one being executed) Screen output Never return the address of a local Since the frame is discarded on return, a function that returns &local hands over the address of something that no longer exists. The program may work by chance and fail later, far from the error. And a recursion without a base case stacks frames until the stack is exhausted: this is the stack overflow. On a microcontroller, with 2 KiB of RAM, the stack runs out much sooner."},{"t":"Programs in several files","d":"Structured programming language","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/programacion-estructurada#modulos","x":"07 Programs in several files A structured program grows split into modules: a.c file with the code and a.h file with what the rest of the program needs to know about it, namely the prototypes, types and constants. /* resistors.h — the interface: what the module offers */ #ifndef RESISTORS_H // guard against double inclusion #define RESISTORS_H double parallel ( const double r[], int n); double series ( const double r[], int n); #endif /* resistors.c — the implementation: how it does it */ #include \"resistors.h\" static int has_short ( const double r[], int n); // private to the module... It is compiled with gcc main.c resistors.c -o calc. Separating interface from implementation allows changing the how without touching whoever uses the module: it is the same idea as layered abstraction, at the scale of a program."},{"t":"Standard input and output","d":"Structured programming language","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/programacion-estructurada#es","x":"08 Standard input and output Specifier Type Example %d / %u int / unsigned printf(\"%d\", -5) %ld long printf(\"%ld\", 100000L) %f in printf, %lf in scanf double printf(\"%.2f\", 3.14159) → 3.14 %e / %g double, scientific or automatic printf(\"%e\", 4.7e-9) %x / %o integer in hexadecimal / octal printf(\"0x%02X\", 12) → 0x0C %c / %s character / string printf(\"%s\", \"hello\") scanf returns how many items it managed to read. A robust program checks it: if (scanf(\"%lf\", &r) != 1) means that the user typed something that is not a number."},{"t":"In the lab","d":"Structured programming language","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/programacion-estructurada#laboratorio","x":"09 In the lab Exercise 1 · A modular resistor calculator Program the equivalent resistance example from the topic on algorithms in three files ( main.c, resistors.c, resistors.h ), with input validation and the unusual cases. Check the results with the site’s resistor calculator to read off the values of real components. Exercise 2 · Seeing the stack with the debugger Compile the factorial with gcc -g and run it in gdb (or in the VS Code debugger). Set a breakpoint at the base case and use bt ( backtrace ) to see all the stacked frames, with the value of n in each one. Exercise 3 · Reading the assembly Compile a function that adds two integers with gcc -S -O1 and locate in the.s file the add instruction and the return. Repeat with -O0 and compare the length. Development environments are covered in C and C++ development environments."},{"t":"Common mistakes","d":"Structured programming language","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/programacion-estructurada#errores","x":"10 Common mistakes = instead of == inside an if. A semicolon after the for: for (i = 0; i < n; i++); runs an empty loop. Forgetting the & in scanf: the program writes to an arbitrary address. Unintended integer division: 1/2 * x is always 0. Uninitialized local variables: they are not zero; they hold whatever was on the stack. Using %d for a double or %f for an int: it prints garbage without warning."},{"t":"Self-assessment","d":"Structured programming language","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/programacion-estructurada#autoevaluacion","x":"11 Self-assessment What is the value of double x = 5 / 2; ? 2.0. The integer division is done first and gives 2; only afterward is it converted to double. Why does scanf need &n while printf needs only n ? Because C passes everything by value. printf only needs the value; scanf has to modify the variable and for that it needs its address. How many frames are on the stack at most when computing fact(5) called from main? Six: main’s and five of fact’s, with n = 5, 4, 3, 2 and 1. What is the difference between an ordinary local variable and a static one? Both are visible only in their block, but the static one lives for the whole program and keeps its value between calls; the ordinary one is created and destroyed on each call. Which stage gives the error undefined reference and what does it mean? The linker: the function was declared, but it cannot find its code in any object file or library. What does if (-1 < 1u) printf(\"a\"); else printf(\"b\"); print? b. The −1 is converted to unsigned and becomes the maximum value."},{"t":"Further reading","d":"Structured programming language","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/programacion-estructurada#ampliar","x":"12 Further reading Brian W. Kernighan and Dennis M. Ritchie. The C Programming Language. 2nd ed. (Spanish edition, “El lenguaje de programación C”), Prentice Hall Hispanoamericana, 1991. By the creators of the language. Brief, dense and with exercises that teach you to think in C. Harvey M. Deitel and Paul J. Deitel. Cómo programar en C/C++ y Java. 4th ed., Pearson Educación, 2004 (in Spanish). Step by step, with a great many complete examples and good practices. Luis Joyanes Aguilar and Ignacio Zahonero Martínez. Programación en C. Metodología, algoritmos y estructura de datos. 2nd ed., McGraw-Hill, 2005 (in Spanish). Connects the pseudocode of the previous topic with the language, in Spanish. K. N. King. C Programming: A Modern Approach. 2nd ed., W. W. Norton, 2008. The best modern C textbook: rigorous about the standard and very clear. Edsger W. Dijkstra. Go To Statement Considered Harmful. Communications of the ACM, 11(3), 1968. A page and a half that changed the way people program."},{"t":"Twenty-four orders of magnitude","d":"Electrical properties of matter","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/propiedades-electricas#tres","x":"01 Twenty-four orders of magnitude No other physical property spans such a large range. Between silver and quartz there is a factor of \\( 10^{24} \\), and in the middle is silicon, which can be placed almost wherever you want. \\[ \\rho = \\frac{RA}{L} \\qquad\\qquad \\sigma = \\frac{1}{\\rho} \\qquad\\qquad \\vec{J} = \\sigma\\vec{E} \\] The last one is Ohm's law in local form, which is the one that really holds: the \\( V = IR \\) version comes from integrating it over a conductor of uniform cross-section. Material \\( \\rho \\) in Ω·m Class Silver \\( 1.6\\times10^{-8} \\) The best conductor there is Copper \\( 1.7\\times10^{-8} \\) Almost the same and much cheaper Nichrome \\( 1.1\\times10^{-6} \\) Deliberately poor conductor Doped silicon \\( 10^{16} \\) \\( 5\\times10^{-3} \\) Working semiconductor Intrinsic silicon \\( 2.3\\times10^{3} \\) Pure semiconductor Glass \\( 10^{12} \\) Insulator Fused quartz \\( 10^{16} \\) The best common insulator The fourth and fifth rows are the same substance. Between them there is a factor of almost a million, and the only thing that changed is one phosphorus atom for every five million silicon atoms. That is the property that makes electronics possible, and no other family of materials has it."},{"t":"What temperature does to it","d":"Electrical properties of matter","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/propiedades-electricas#temperatura","x":"02 What temperature does to it Here metals and semiconductors behave in opposite ways, and the reason lies in what limits conduction in each case. Carriers On heating Result Metal Fixed: one or two per atom The lattice vibrates more and scatters them \\( \\rho \\) rises Semiconductor Few, and they have to be created Thermal agitation promotes many more \\( \\rho \\) drops, exponentially \\[ \\rho(T) = \\rho_{20}\\left[1 + \\alpha\\,(T - 20)\\right] \\] For metals, over a moderate range. The coefficient \\( \\alpha \\) of copper is \\( 3.93\\times10^{-3} \\) per degree: the resistance of a copper coil rises almost 40% between 20 and 120 °C, and this is used to measure the temperature of a motor without fitting a sensor. Lab · resistivity versus temperature Metals, alloys and semiconductors on the same logarithmic scale. Choose the material and move the temperature; below, the resistance of a specific length of wire is calculated. silver copper aluminum gold tungsten nichrome constantan carbon doped silicon 10¹⁶ Temperature = 20 °C Wire of 1 m and 1 mm diameter Materials that deliberately do not change Constantan —copper with nickel— has an almost zero temperature coefficient, about twenty parts per million per degree, hence its name. That is why it is used in standard resistors, in measurement shunts and in strain gauges: you need the resistance to change with what you want to measure and with nothing else. Nichrome shares that virtue and also withstands red heat, which is why it is used in ovens"},{"t":"On the insulator side","d":"Electrical properties of matter","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/propiedades-electricas#dielectricos","x":"03 On the insulator side An insulator has no free charges, but its molecules do respond to the field: they polarize. There are three mechanisms and they act on different time scales, which explains why permittivity depends on frequency. Polarization What moves Up to what frequency it responds Electronic The electron cloud inside the atom Optical: \\( 10^{15} \\) Hz Ionic The ions within the lattice Infrared: \\( 10^{13} \\) Hz Orientation Polar molecules that rotate Microwave: \\( 10^{10} \\) Hz The third row is the microwave oven: at 2.45 GHz water molecules can still rotate following the field, and that forced rotation against the friction of their neighbors is what heats. And it is also the reason the permittivity of water falls from 80 at DC to 1.8 at optical frequencies: above a certain frequency the molecules can no longer follow the field. That lag between the field and the polarization is what produces dielectric losses, which are reported as \\( \\tan\\delta \\). In an FR-4 laminate they are about 0.02, and that is why beyond a few gigahertz it is no longer usable: the trace turns into a heater. Radio-frequency boards use materials with a \\( \\tan\\delta \\) ten times lower."},{"t":"The other extreme","d":"Electrical properties of matter","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/propiedades-electricas#superconductores","x":"04 The other extreme Below a critical temperature, some materials lose all resistance. It is not a very small resistance: it is exactly zero, and a current set flowing in a superconducting ring keeps going for years without decaying. Material \\( T_c \\) Cooled with Aluminum 1.2 K Liquid helium Niobium-titanium 9.3 K Liquid helium; it is the one in MRI magnets Ceramic YBCO 93 K Liquid nitrogen, which is cheap The third row changed everything in 1987: going from needing helium to being able to use liquid nitrogen, which costs less than milk, moved superconductors from the laboratory to application. Today there are superconducting cables in some urban power grids, and the Josephson junction —two superconductors separated by a very thin barrier— is the basis of the primary voltage standard and of a good part of quantum computing."},{"t":"In the lab","d":"Electrical properties of matter","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/propiedades-electricas#laboratorio","x":"05 In the lab Activity 1 · Temperature coefficient of copper Using a four-wire measurement, measure the resistance of a copper coil immersed in a bath, between 20 and 90 °C. Plot \\( R \\) against \\( T \\), obtain \\( \\alpha \\) from the slope and compare it with the tabulated value. Activity 2 · Metal versus semiconductor Repeat the measurement with an NTC thermistor in the same bath. Plot both on a logarithmic scale and discuss why one rises and the other falls. Activity 3 · Resistivity of a trace Using a four-wire measurement, measure the resistance of a copper trace of known geometry on a board with 35 µm copper. Calculate the resistivity and compare it with that of pure copper. Discuss the concept of resistance per square."},{"t":"Common mistakes","d":"Electrical properties of matter","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/propiedades-electricas#errores","x":"06 Common mistakes Confusing resistance with resistivity. One depends on the geometry; the other belongs to the material. Believing every material increases its resistance when heated. Semiconductors and carbon do the opposite. Using the linear formula for \\( \\alpha \\) far from 20 °C. It is an approximation valid over a moderate range. Assuming permittivity is a constant. It depends on frequency, and strongly. Measuring low resistances with two probes. The leads and the contacts weigh more than what you want to measure: use four wires. Thinking a superconductor has a very low resistance. It has zero, which is something else."},{"t":"Self-assessment","d":"Electrical properties of matter","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/propiedades-electricas#autoevaluacion","x":"07 Self-assessment Why does the resistivity of a metal rise with temperature? Because the number of carriers is fixed and the lattice, vibrating more, scatters them more often. And why does that of a semiconductor fall? Because thermal agitation creates new carriers, and that effect dominates over scattering. What is constantan for? For standard resistors, shunts and gauges: its temperature coefficient is almost zero. Why does the microwave oven work at 2.45 GHz? Because at that frequency water molecules still rotate following the field, and that forced rotation heats. What does \\( \\tan\\delta \\) mean? The losses of the dielectric, due to the lag between the field and the polarization. In FR-4 it is about 0.02. Why was YBCO so important? Because its critical temperature of 93 K allows cooling it with liquid nitrogen, which is cheap, instead of helium."},{"t":"Further reading","d":"Electrical properties of matter","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/propiedades-electricas#ampliar","x":"08 Further reading Raymond A. Serway and John W. Jewett. Physics for Scientists and Engineers with Modern Physics. 10th ed., Cengage, 2019 (Spanish edition, “Física para ciencias e ingeniería”). Current, resistivity, the conduction model and superconductivity. William D. Callister and David G. Rethwisch. Materials Science and Engineering: An Introduction. 9th ed., Reverté, 2019 (Spanish edition, “Introducción a la ciencia e ingeniería de los materiales”). Electrical properties of metals, semiconductors and dielectrics, with the materials-science treatment. Charles Kittel. Introduction to Solid State Physics. 8th ed. (Spanish edition, “Introducción a la física del estado sólido”, Reverté). Where metallic conduction, phonon scattering and superconductivity come from."},{"t":"The table you need to know how to read","d":"Properties of the transform","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/propiedades-fourier#tabla","x":"01 The table you need to know how to read The properties of the transform are not curiosities to memorize: each one says what happens to the spectrum when you modify the signal, and vice versa. They are what lets you solve problems without ever integrating again. In time In frequency What it means \\( a\\,x(t) + b\\,y(t) \\) \\( a\\,X(j\\omega) + b\\,Y(j\\omega) \\) Linearity: everything can be decomposed \\( x(t - t_0) \\) \\( X(j\\omega)\\,e^{-j\\omega t_0} \\) The magnitude does not change; only linear phase is added \\( x(t)\\,e^{j\\omega_0 t} \\) \\( X\\big(j(\\omega - \\omega_0)\\big) \\) Modulation: the spectrum is shifted. That is radio \\( x(at) \\) \\( \\dfrac{1}{|a|}X\\!\\left(\\dfrac{j\\omega}{a}\\right) \\) Compressing in time widens in frequency \\( \\dfrac{dx}{dt} \\) \\( j\\omega\\,X(j\\omega) \\) Differentiating boosts the sharp parts, with +90° of phase \\( \\displaystyle\\int_{-\\infty}^{t} x\\,d\\tau \\) \\( \\dfrac{X(j\\omega)}{j\\omega} + \\pi X(0)\\delta(\\omega) \\) Integrating attenuates the sharp parts \\( x(t) * h(t) \\) \\( X(j\\omega)\\,H(j\\omega) \\) Convolution becomes a product \\( x(t)\\,h(t) \\) \\( \\dfrac{1}{2\\pi}X * H \\) Multiplying in time convolves the spectra \\( x^{*}(t) \\) \\( X^{*}(-j\\omega) \\) This is where the Hermitian symmetry of real signals comes from The two rows in bold are the ones used most. The time-shift row explains why a delay cannot be heard: the ear perceives the magnitude of the spectrum, not the phase. And the convolution row is the one that turns all circuit analysis into multiplying nu"},{"t":"Seeing both sides at once","d":"Properties of the transform","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/propiedades-fourier#lab","x":"02 Seeing both sides at once The way to make these properties stop being a table is to look at both domains at the same time while you modify the signal. The lab does exactly that: on the left \\( x(t) \\), on the right its magnitude and phase, and the original signal in gray for comparison. Lab · one operation, two domains Choose the operation and move its parameter. Watch which of the two plots on the right changes and which one stays the same. no change shift in time scale in time modulate · multiply by a cosine differentiate integrate add a sinusoid signal: pulse signal: bell curve signal: decaying exponential signal: two tones Parameter = 1.0"},{"t":"The trade-off you cannot avoid","d":"Properties of the transform","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/propiedades-fourier#escalamiento","x":"03 The trade-off you cannot avoid The scaling property has a consequence that shows up everywhere. If \\( x(at) \\) has spectrum \\( X(j\\omega/a)/|a| \\), then shortening a signal makes it wider in frequency, in the same proportion. The product of duration and bandwidth is a constant: \\[ \\Delta t \\cdot \\Delta f \\ge \\text{constant} \\] There is no way to have a signal that is both short and narrow. It is the same principle that in physics is known as the uncertainty relation. Situation Consequence A 1 μs pulse Occupies on the order of 1 MHz of bandwidth A narrow radio channel Cannot carry fast pulses Doubling the speed of a link Requires twice the bandwidth A 100 MHz oscilloscope Cannot display an edge faster than 3.5 ns The last row is the most widely used rule of thumb in the lab: the rise time of an instrument and its bandwidth satisfy \\( t_r \\approx 0.35/B \\). If the edge you want to measure is faster than that, what you see on the screen is the oscilloscope, not the signal."},{"t":"Modulation: shifting the spectrum","d":"Properties of the transform","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/propiedades-fourier#modulacion","x":"04 Modulation: shifting the spectrum Multiplying by a complex exponential shifts the whole spectrum. With a real cosine, which is two exponentials, the spectrum splits into two copies: \\[ x(t)\\cos(\\omega_0 t) \\;\\longleftrightarrow\\; \\tfrac12 X\\big(j(\\omega - \\omega_0)\\big) + \\tfrac12 X\\big(j(\\omega + \\omega_0)\\big) \\] Two replicas at half amplitude, one on each side of the carrier. That is exactly amplitude modulation. This is where the sidebands come from: if the modulating signal extends up to 5 kHz, the modulated signal occupies 10 kHz around the carrier. And it explains something that was already anticipated when discussing LTI systems: to shift a signal in frequency you need a multiplier, because an LTI system cannot do it. Duality: everything can be read backwards The transform and its inverse are so similar that each property has its counterpart. If a rectangular pulse in time gives a sinc in frequency, then a sinc in time gives a rectangular pulse in frequency —the ideal filter—. And if shifting in time adds linear phase, shifting in frequency multiplies by an exponential in time. Once you have learned one property, you get the other for free."},{"t":"In the lab","d":"Properties of the transform","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/propiedades-fourier#laboratorio","x":"05 In the lab Activity 1 · A delay does not change the magnitude Capture a pulse and the same pulse delayed by 1 ms, and compare the two transforms using the oscilloscope’s FFT. Verify that the magnitude is identical and that the phase differs by a straight line with slope \\( -t_0 \\). Activity 2 · Measure the rise time of the instrument Inject an edge much faster than the bandwidth of the oscilloscope and measure the rise time it displays. Compare with \\( 0.35/B \\) and conclude which of the two is being measured. Activity 3 · The sidebands Modulate a 1 MHz carrier with a 10 kHz tone and observe the spectrum. Identify the carrier and the two sidebands, and verify that they are at half amplitude."},{"t":"Common mistakes","d":"Properties of the transform","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/propiedades-fourier#errores","x":"06 Common mistakes Believing that a delay changes the spectrum. It changes the phase, not the magnitude. Mixing up the direction of scaling. Compressing in time widens in frequency. Forgetting the \\( 1/|a| \\) in scaling: the area of the spectrum has to be conserved. Confusing convolution with multiplication. Convolution in one domain is a product in the other. Asking for a signal that is both short and narrowband. It does not exist; the product is bounded."},{"t":"Self-assessment","d":"Properties of the transform","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/propiedades-fourier#autoevaluacion","x":"07 Self-assessment What does a delay do to the spectrum? It multiplies by \\( e^{-j\\omega t_0} \\): it adds linear phase and leaves the magnitude intact. If a signal is compressed to half its duration, what happens to its bandwidth? It doubles, and the amplitude of the spectrum is halved. How much bandwidth does a 5 kHz signal modulated in amplitude occupy? 10 kHz: two sidebands of 5 kHz each, around the carrier. What is the minimum rise time of a 100 MHz oscilloscope? \\( 0.35/100\\text{ MHz} = 3.5 \\) ns. A faster edge is displayed distorted. What does convolution become when moving to frequency? A product. That is why cascading filters means multiplying responses. What does duality say? That each property has its counterpart when the domains are swapped: a pulse gives a sinc, and a sinc gives a pulse."},{"t":"Further reading","d":"Properties of the transform","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/propiedades-fourier#ampliar","x":"08 Further reading Alan V. Oppenheim and Alan S. Willsky. Signals and Systems. 2nd ed. (Spanish edition, “Señales y sistemas”), Prentice Hall, 1997. Chapter 4, with the complete table of properties and their proofs. Ronald N. Bracewell. The Fourier Transform and Its Applications. 3rd ed., McGraw-Hill, 1999. Every property illustrated with the corresponding pair of plots. Unbeatable for building intuition. Leon W. Couch. Digital and Analog Communication Systems. 7th ed. (Spanish edition, “Sistemas de comunicación digitales y analógicos”), Pearson Prentice Hall, 2007. Modulation as a direct application of the frequency shift."},{"t":"Three responses to the field","d":"Magnetic properties of matter","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/propiedades-magneticas#tres","x":"01 Three responses to the field Every material responds to a magnetic field, even though in almost all of them the response is imperceptible. The quantity that measures it is the relative permeability, and according to its value materials split into three very unequal groups. \\[ \\vec{B} = \\mu_0\\mu_r\\vec{H} \\qquad\\qquad \\mu_r = 1 + \\chi_m \\] \\( H \\) is the magnetizing field (the excitation), the one produced by the currents you control; \\( B \\) is the resulting flux density, which includes what the material contributes. In vacuum \\( \\mu_r = 1 \\) exactly. Type \\( \\mu_r \\) Why Examples Diamagnetic Barely less than 1 The field induces opposing moments: it repels Copper, water, graphite, bismuth Paramagnetic Barely greater than 1 Permanent moments that partly align Aluminum, platinum, liquid oxygen Ferromagnetic From 100 to 100,000 Coupled moments that align as a block Iron, nickel, cobalt, ferrites The difference between the first two rows and the third is five orders of magnitude, and that is why in practice there are only two categories: magnetic materials and all the others. An aluminum shield is useless against a static field for exactly that reason: its permeability is practically that of vacuum. Domains In a ferromagnet the moments are not disordered but grouped into domains: regions a few microns across where all of them point the same way. A demagnetized piece has its domains oriented at random and the net field is zero. When \\( H \\) is applied, the favorably oriented dom"},{"t":"The hysteresis loop","d":"Magnetic properties of matter","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/propiedades-magneticas#histeresis","x":"02 The hysteresis loop Since the motion of the domain walls costs energy and does not undo itself, \\( B \\) does not depend only on \\( H \\): it also depends on how you got there. Going through a complete cycle of excitation, the curve encloses a loop. Parameter What it is What it determines \\( B_{sat} \\) Saturation flux density The limit of the core: beyond it, it contributes nothing \\( B_r \\) Remanence: what is left at \\( H = 0 \\) Whether the material works as a permanent magnet \\( H_c \\) Coercivity: the \\( H \\) that demagnetizes it Whether it is soft or hard Loop area Energy per unit volume per cycle The hysteresis losses The last row is the key for electronics: the area of the loop is energy lost in every cycle, and multiplied by the frequency it gives dissipated power. That is why transformer cores are made of narrow-loop materials —magnetically soft— and permanent magnets of wide-loop ones. Lab · hysteresis loop Six real materials with their saturation and their coercivity. You vary the excitation and the loop is traced in full; below appear the loss per cycle and the power dissipated at the chosen frequency. silicon steel · transformer lamination soft iron MnZn ferrite · switching power supplies mu-metal · magnetic shielding alnico · permanent magnet neodymium · rare-earth magnet Maximum excitation = 200 A/m Frequency = 50 Hz"},{"t":"Soft and hard","d":"Magnetic properties of matter","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/propiedades-magneticas#blandos","x":"03 Soft and hard Magnetically soft Magnetically hard \\( H_c \\) Less than 1000 A/m More than 10,000 A/m Loop Narrow, low losses Wide: that is what is wanted What for Cores of transformers and inductors Permanent magnets, motors, loudspeakers Examples Silicon steel, ferrite, mu-metal Alnico, hard ferrite, neodymium The Curie temperature marks the end of all this: above it, thermal agitation overcomes the coupling between moments, the domains disappear and the material becomes a plain paramagnet. It is 770 °C for iron, 358 for nickel and barely 310 for a common neodymium magnet, which is why a permanent-magnet motor has a strict thermal limit: past a certain point the magnets degrade and the motor is never the same again. Why switching power supplies use ferrite At 50 Hz the hysteresis losses of silicon steel are tolerable and its saturation of 2 T makes it compact. But those losses grow with frequency, and at 100 kHz they would be two thousand times greater. Ferrite has a much narrower loop and, above all, is a ceramic that barely conducts, so it has no eddy currents either. It pays for this with a saturation of only 0.4 T —five times lower—, but at that frequency the core needs much less flux and it does not matter."},{"t":"In the lab","d":"Magnetic properties of matter","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/propiedades-magneticas#laboratorio","x":"04 In the lab Activity 1 · Tracing the loop on the oscilloscope With a transformer whose core is accessible, send the drop across a series resistor to the X axis —proportional to \\( H \\)— and the output of an RC integrator on the secondary to the Y axis —proportional to \\( B \\)—. View the loop in XY mode and measure \\( B_r \\) and \\( H_c \\). Activity 2 · Saturating a core Increase the voltage applied to the primary and observe the current with the oscilloscope. Identify the moment when it stops being sinusoidal and spikes appear: that is saturation. Explain why the current shoots up. Activity 3 · Curie with a magnet Heat a nail held by a magnet with a torch until it drops off. Estimate the temperature from the color of the metal and compare it with the 770 °C of iron. Verify that when it cools it is attracted again."},{"t":"Common mistakes","d":"Magnetic properties of matter","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/propiedades-magneticas#errores","x":"05 Common mistakes Confusing \\( B \\) with \\( H \\). One is the resulting flux density; the other, the excitation that you apply. Believing that \\( \\mu_r \\) is a constant. It depends on \\( H \\), on history and on temperature. Shielding a static field with aluminum or copper. They are diamagnetic: \\( \\mu_r \\approx 1 \\) and they do not divert anything. Ignoring saturation when designing an inductor. Beyond \\( B_{sat} \\) the inductance collapses and the current shoots up. Using silicon steel at high frequency. The losses grow with frequency; ferrite goes there. Forgetting the Curie temperature of a magnet. Neodymium degrades well before iron does."},{"t":"Self-assessment","d":"Magnetic properties of matter","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/propiedades-magneticas#autoevaluacion","x":"06 Self-assessment What does the area of the hysteresis loop represent? The energy lost per unit volume in each cycle. Multiplied by the frequency it gives the dissipated power. What distinguishes a magnetically soft material? Its low coercivity and narrow loop: it demagnetizes easily and loses little energy per cycle. Why is aluminum no use as a magnetic shield? Because it is paramagnetic with \\( \\mu_r \\) almost 1: it does not divert the field lines. What happens above the Curie temperature? Thermal agitation overcomes the coupling, the domains disappear and the material becomes paramagnetic. Why do switching power supplies use ferrite and not sheet steel? Because of the losses: ferrite has a narrow loop and barely conducts, so it has no eddy currents either. What happens to an inductor that saturates? Its inductance collapses and the current shoots up, because the core can no longer contribute more flux."},{"t":"Further reading","d":"Magnetic properties of matter","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/propiedades-magneticas#ampliar","x":"07 Further reading Raymond A. Serway and John W. Jewett. Physics for Scientists and Engineers with Modern Physics. 10th ed. (Spanish edition, “Física para ciencias e ingeniería”), Cengage, 2019. Magnetism in matter, domains, hysteresis and the Curie temperature. William D. Callister and David G. Rethwisch. Materials Science and Engineering: An Introduction. 9th ed., Wiley, 2014 (Spanish edition, “Introducción a la ciencia e ingeniería de los materiales”, Reverté, 2019). Soft and hard magnetic materials, with their values and their industrial applications. Colonel Wm. T. McLyman. Transformer and Inductor Design Handbook. 4th ed., CRC Press, 2011. The real design of a core: choice of material, saturation, losses and air gap."},{"t":"Overvoltages","d":"Protection and heat removal","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/protecciones-potencia#sobretensiones","x":"01 Overvoltages When a switch carrying current opens, the parasitic inductance of the circuit —traces, cables, package— generates an overvoltage \\( L\\,di/dt \\) that adds to that of the bus. With the edges of tens of nanoseconds of modern devices, a few nanohenries are enough for tens of volts. It is tackled on three fronts: Layout: laminated busbars and decoupling capacitors right next to the switches, to shrink the switching loop. Damping networks ( snubbers ): an RC or RCD network in parallel with the switch absorbs the energy of the parasitic inductance and slows the rise of the voltage. Clamps: TVS suppressors or an active clamp that turns the switch back on if the voltage exceeds a threshold. Overvoltages that arrive from the grid, from lightning or switching operations, are limited with varistors and surge arresters, as seen in other devices."},{"t":"Overcurrents and short circuits","d":"Protection and heat removal","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/protecciones-potencia#sobrecorrientes","x":"02 Overcurrents and short circuits A power semiconductor has very little thermal capacity: an overcurrent destroys it in microseconds or milliseconds. The protections act at different speeds: Protection Response time How it works Desaturation detection A few microseconds If the collector-emitter voltage of a turned-on IGBT rises, there is a short circuit: the driver turns it off softly. IGBTs typically withstand a short circuit of up to about 10 μs Cycle-by-cycle current limiting One switching cycle Current-mode control cuts the pulse when a threshold is exceeded Ultrafast fuse Milliseconds It is chosen with a melting \\( I^2 t \\) lower than the one the semiconductor can withstand Thermal protection Seconds A sensor on the heat sink or in the module reduces the power or shuts down"},{"t":"Heat removal","d":"Protection and heat removal","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/protecciones-potencia#calor","x":"03 Heat removal In steady state, heat follows a path of thermal resistances in series, like a current through resistors: \\[ T_j = T_a + P\\,(R_{jc} + R_{cs} + R_{sa}) \\] Junction to case, case to heat sink —the thermal interface—, and heat sink to ambient, which depends on the size of the heat sink and on whether there is forced air or liquid cooling. The junction temperature accelerates failures according to Arrhenius, as seen in reliability. But each part also has thermal capacity: in a short pulse, the junction heats up before the heat reaches the heat sink. Datasheets give the transient thermal impedance \\( Z_{th}(t) \\), which is modeled with a Foster network, a sum of exponentials: \\[ Z_{th}(t_p) = \\sum_i R_i \\left(1 - e^{-t_p/\\tau_i}\\right) \\] \\[ Z_{ef} = \\sum_i R_i\\,\\frac{1 - e^{-t_p/\\tau_i}}{1 - e^{-T/\\tau_i}} \\] The first is for a single pulse of duration \\( t_p \\); the second, for a pulse train of period \\( T = t_p / D \\) in steady state, and gives the temperature peak at the end of each pulse: \\( T_{j,pico} = T_a + P\\,Z_{ef} \\). Lab · junction temperature with pulses A transistor with R jc = 0.4 K/W in three Foster stages (0.05, 0.15 and 0.2 K/W with 0.5 ms, 5 ms and 50 ms), an interface of 0.1 K/W and 1 s, and a heat sink of 0.5 K/W and 60 s, at an ambient temperature of 40 °C. The curves are the effective impedance as a function of pulse width for several duty cycles; the blue one is the one chosen. Pulse power P = Pulse width t p = Duty cycle D ="},{"t":"In the laboratory","d":"Protection and heat removal","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/protecciones-potencia#laboratorio","x":"04 In the laboratory Activity 1 · Snubber Measure the turn-off overvoltage of a MOSFET with an inductive load, without an RC network and with several values, and calculate the power dissipated by the resistor. Activity 2 · Thermal resistance Apply constant power to a transistor mounted on a heat sink, measure the case and heat sink temperatures, and deduce the thermal resistances. Activity 3 · Cooling curve Use the voltage of a diode as a thermometer to record the cooling of the junction and fit a Foster network."},{"t":"Common mistakes","d":"Protection and heat removal","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/protecciones-potencia#errores","x":"05 Common mistakes Sizing with the average power when there are long pulses. The temperature peak can be much higher. Forgetting the thermal interface. A dry or poorly tightened mica insulator can double the case-to-heat-sink resistance. Protecting a semiconductor with an ordinary fuse. It is too slow: the semiconductor fails first. Adding a snubber without counting its loss. Its resistor dissipates \\( C V^2 f \\), which at high frequency is a lot."},{"t":"Self-assessment","d":"Protection and heat removal","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/protecciones-potencia#autoevaluacion","x":"06 Self-assessment What junction temperature does the lab transistor reach with 100 W continuous? \\( 40 + 100 \\cdot 1 = 140 \\) °C. And with 200 W pulses, 1 ms, duty cycle 0.1? About 71 °C at the peak: the effective impedance is 0.157 K/W. How long does an IGBT typically withstand a short circuit? Up to about 10 μs, according to its datasheet. What is an RC network in parallel with a switch for? To absorb the energy of the parasitic inductance and limit the overvoltage and the rate of rise at turn-off."},{"t":"Further reading","d":"Protection and heat removal","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/protecciones-potencia#ampliar","x":"07 Further reading Ned Mohan, Tore M. Undeland and William P. Robbins. Power Electronics: Converters, Applications, and Design. 3rd ed., Wiley, 2003. Snubbers, protected drivers and thermal design. Andreas Volke and Michael Hornkamp. IGBT Modules: Technologies, Driver and Application. 2nd ed., Infineon Technologies, 2012. Short circuit, desaturation and the thermal impedance of modules. Frank P. Incropera and David P. DeWitt. Fundamentals of Heat and Mass Transfer. Wiley. (Spanish edition: “Fundamentos de transferencia de calor”, Pearson.) The conduction, convection and radiation behind thermal resistances."},{"t":"Why almost everything is serial","d":"Serial communications and protocols","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/protocolos-seriales#por-que","x":"01 Why almost everything is serial For decades data traveled in parallel: eight, sixteen or thirty-two lines at a time. At high speed that stops working: the bits of a single data word arrive with timing differences because of the different lengths of the traces, and every line that switches adds noise to its neighbors. A single differential line, properly matched, reaches tens of gigabits per second today. That is why USB, SATA, PCI Express and Ethernet replaced their parallel predecessors, and on the board, two or three wires of a serial bus replace an entire data bus. The fundamental difference is how the receiver knows when to read each bit. In a synchronous link a separate clock travels along; in an asynchronous one, the two ends agree on the speed and synchronize with a start bit; in fast links, the clock is embedded in the data and the receiver recovers it. The circuits are presented in the technician program, in serial communication and serial buses."},{"t":"Inside the board: UART, SPI and I²C","d":"Serial communications and protocols","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/protocolos-seriales#placa","x":"02 Inside the board: UART, SPI and I²C UART SPI I²C Lines TX and RX SCLK, MOSI, MISO and one select per slave SDA and SCL, open-drain with pull-up resistors Clock Asynchronous, agreed speed Synchronous, generated by the master Synchronous, generated by the master Typical speed 9600 to 1 Mbit/s 1 to 50 MHz 100 kHz, 400 kHz or 1 MHz Devices Two One per select line Up to 127, by 7-bit address Overhead Start and stop per byte None Address and an acknowledge bit per byte The UART tolerates a limited speed difference between the two ends: the receiver samples at the center of each bit counting from the start edge, and by the tenth bit the accumulated error cannot exceed half a bit. In practice, the sum of the errors of the two clocks must stay below about 4 %, which sometimes rules out the internal RC oscillator. In I²C, the lines are pulled low actively and rise through the pull-up resistors. The rise time, which the standard limits, sets the maximum value of the resistor: \\[ R_{p,max} = \\frac{t_r}{0.8473\\; C_b} \\] \\( t_r \\) is the rise time between 30 % and 70 % and \\( C_b \\) the total capacitance of the bus. At 400 kHz (\\( t_r \\le 300 \\) ns) with 200 pF of bus, the resistor cannot exceed 1.8 kΩ. With more devices or longer cables, you have to lower the speed."},{"t":"Fieldbuses: CAN and RS-485","d":"Serial communications and protocols","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/protocolos-seriales#campo","x":"03 Fieldbuses: CAN and RS-485 Outside the board, between pieces of equipment, buses use differential signals: two twisted wires that carry the signal and its opposite, so that noise that affects both equally is canceled at the receiver. RS-485 defines only the electrical layer, for up to 32 unit loads over 1200 m. CAN, designed for cars, adds a complete protocol: Nondestructive arbitration. The 0 bit is dominant: if two nodes transmit at the same time, the one sending a 1 and reading a 0 backs off without losing anything. The lowest identifier wins, which is the one with the highest priority. Bit stuffing. After five identical bits an opposite one is inserted, so that there are enough edges to resynchronize the clocks. The frame length depends on the data. Error detection. 15-bit CRC, acknowledgment by all receivers, and counters that disconnect a faulty node. Lab · the cost of a frame \\( n \\) bytes are sent over the chosen protocol. The chart shows the first bits of the frame, with its fields (the first data byte is 0x4B), and below, how much of the frame is useful data. In CAN, the identifier is 0x123 and the real stuffing is calculated together with the CRC. UART 8N1 SPI I²C, write to address 0x50 CAN 2.0A, identifier 0x123 Data bytes n ="},{"t":"High speed","d":"Serial communications and protocols","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/protocolos-seriales#alta","x":"04 High speed Above a few hundred megabits per second, each link is a differential transmission line of controlled impedance, typically 90 or 100 Ω, terminated at the receiver. LVDS uses a swing of just 350 mV, which switches fast and consumes little. The clock does not travel separately: it is encoded in the data to guarantee transitions, and the receiver recovers it with a PLL. Link Speed per lane Encoding Efficiency USB 2.0 high speed 480 Mbit/s NRZI with bit stuffing Depends on the data: in the worst case, one extra bit every six PCI Express 2.0 and USB 3.0 5 Gbit/s 8b/10b 80 % PCI Express 3.0 8 Gbit/s 128b/130b 98.5 % Ethernet 10GBASE-R 10.3 Gbit/s 64b/66b 97 % The quality of a fast link is evaluated with the eye diagram: all the bits synchronized with the recovered clock are superimposed on the oscilloscope. An open eye means that the receiver tells zeros from ones well; noise, jitter and reflections close it."},{"t":"Where it appears in electronics","d":"Serial communications and protocols","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/protocolos-seriales#electronica","x":"05 Where it appears in electronics Application Typical bus Temperature sensor or accelerometer on a board I²C or SPI Display or external flash memory SPI, or QSPI with four data lines Microcontroller debug console UART Automobile, agricultural machinery CAN and CAN FD Industrial automation RS-485 with Modbus, or industrial Ethernet Graphics card, solid-state drive PCI Express"},{"t":"In the lab","d":"Serial communications and protocols","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/protocolos-seriales#laboratorio","x":"06 In the lab Activity 1 · Decoding frames With a logic analyzer, capture an I²C transaction with a sensor and an SPI one with a memory. Identify the address, acknowledgments and data, and measure the real clock speed. Activity 2 · Rise time in I²C Measure the rise time of SDA with pull-ups of 10 kΩ, 4.7 kΩ and 1 kΩ, and verify which one meets the standard at 400 kHz. Activity 3 · Two CAN nodes Build a CAN bus with two microcontrollers and their transceivers, with 120 Ω terminations. Send frames with different identifiers at the same time and observe the arbitration."},{"t":"Common mistakes","d":"Serial communications and protocols","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/protocolos-seriales#errores","x":"07 Common mistakes Forgetting the I²C pull-ups, or adding too many. Each module brings its own, and in parallel they lower the resistance so much that the devices cannot pull the line low. Mixing up the SPI mode. The clock polarity and phase have to match on master and slave; otherwise the data arrives shifted by one bit. Not terminating a CAN or RS-485 bus. Without the resistors at the ends, reflections corrupt the bits. Calculating the time of a CAN frame without the stuffing. In the worst case it adds 24 bits to a 111-bit frame: almost 22 %."},{"t":"Self-assessment","d":"Serial communications and protocols","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/protocolos-seriales#autoevaluacion","x":"08 Self-assessment How long does it take to send one byte over 8N1 UART at 115,200 bit/s? 10 bits: 86.8 µs. How many bits does writing 2 bytes to a device take in I²C? Start, 9 address bits and 9 per byte, and stop: \\( 9 \\cdot 3 + 2 = 29 \\). Two CAN nodes transmit identifiers 0x120 and 0x123 at the same time. Which one wins? 0x120, the lower one: at the first bit where they differ it sends a dominant 0. What efficiency does 8b/10b encoding have? 80 %: for every 8 data bits, 10 are transmitted."},{"t":"Further reading","d":"Serial communications and protocols","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/protocolos-seriales#ampliar","x":"09 Further reading NXP Semiconductors. UM10204: I²C-bus Specification and User Manual. Rev. 7, 2021. The I²C bus standard, with the timing, the speeds and the calculation of the pull-up resistors. Wilfried Voss. A Comprehensible Guide to Controller Area Network. 2nd ed., Copperhill, 2005. CAN explained from the physical layer to error handling. Howard Johnson and Martin Graham. High-Speed Signal Propagation: Advanced Black Magic. Prentice Hall, 2003. Fast serial links as a problem of transmission lines, losses and equalization."},{"t":"How a test is set up","d":"Hypothesis tests","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/pruebas-hipotesis#planteo","x":"01 How a test is set up A hypothesis test decides between two statements using data. The structure is always the same and is deliberately asymmetric: you start by assuming that nothing is going on and you only abandon that assumption if the data make it very hard to believe. What it claims Its role \\( H_0 \\), null There is no effect, no difference Upheld unless proven otherwise \\( H_1 \\), alternative There is an effect It is what has to be demonstrated The asymmetry is intentional and resembles that of a trial: innocence is not proven, it is presumed. That is why the outcome of a test is never “\\( H_0 \\) is true,” but “there is not enough evidence to reject it”, which is quite a bit less. \\[ z = \\frac{\\bar{x} - \\mu_0}{\\sigma/\\sqrt{n}} \\qquad\\qquad t = \\frac{\\bar{x} - \\mu_0}{s/\\sqrt{n}} \\] The test statistic measures how many standard errors the observation is from what \\( H_0 \\) predicts. If that number is large, the data are unusual under \\( H_0 \\); how large it has to be is set by the significance level."},{"t":"The two errors","d":"Hypothesis tests","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/pruebas-hipotesis#errores2","x":"02 The two errors Any decision made with limited data can go wrong in two ways, and each has its own name because they are not interchangeable. \\( H_0 \\) is true \\( H_0 \\) is false Reject Type I error, probability \\( \\alpha \\) Correct: power \\( 1-\\beta \\) Do not reject Correct Type II error, probability \\( \\beta \\) In quality control they have names of their own: the producer’s risk is rejecting a batch that was good, and the consumer’s risk is accepting one that was bad. Which of the two hurts more depends on what is being manufactured, and that decision is one of engineering, not of statistics. Lab · the two errors and the threshold The two distributions —that of \\( H_0 \\) and that of \\( H_1 \\)— with the decision threshold in between. Move it and watch how one error grows when the other drops. Then increase the sample size and see what happens to both at once. True effect to detect = 10 mV Sample size = 25 Significance level α = 5 %"},{"t":"The p-value, and what it is not","d":"Hypothesis tests","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/pruebas-hipotesis#valorp","x":"03 The p-value, and what it is not The p-value is the probability of observing a result at least as extreme as the one obtained assuming that \\( H_0 \\) is true. If it is smaller than \\( \\alpha \\), you reject. Three things the p-value is not It is not the probability that \\( H_0 \\) is true: it is calculated assuming that it is. It is not the probability of having made a mistake in rejecting. And it does not measure the size of the effect: with a large enough sample, a negligible difference gives a tiny p. A result can be statistically significant and practically irrelevant, and that distinction is the most important one in the whole unit. Situation Decision What it really means \\( p = 0.001 \\), effect of 0.01 % \\( H_0 \\) is rejected The difference exists and does not matter \\( p = 0.20 \\), effect of 30 % Not rejected There may be a large effect and data are lacking \\( p = 0.049 \\) versus \\( p = 0.051 \\) Reject / do not reject They are practically the same result The third row is against the magic threshold. The 0.05 is a convention of Fisher’s, not a natural law, and treating 0.049 and 0.051 as opposite conclusions is an artifact. The reasonable approach is to report the p-value together with the effect size and its confidence interval, and let the reader judge."},{"t":"In the lab","d":"Hypothesis tests","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/pruebas-hipotesis#laboratorio","x":"04 In the lab Activity 1 · Comparing two batches Measure twenty resistors from each of two different batches of the same nominal value. State the hypothesis that the means are equal, calculate the t statistic and the p-value, and decide with \\( \\alpha = 0.05 \\). Activity 2 · The power of the test With the measured standard deviation, calculate what difference between batches could be detected with 80 % power using twenty measurements per batch. Recalculate for fifty and compare. Activity 3 · Significant is not important Simulate two sets of a thousand data points that differ by 0.1 % of the mean. Verify that the p-value comes out very small and discuss whether that difference would justify rejecting a batch in production."},{"t":"Common mistakes","d":"Hypothesis tests","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/pruebas-hipotesis#errores","x":"05 Common mistakes Saying that \\( H_0 \\) was “accepted.” It was not rejected, which is different: the evidence may be lacking. Interpreting the p-value as the probability that \\( H_0 \\) is true. It is calculated assuming that it is. Confusing significant with important. With a large sample, any difference comes out significant. Lowering \\( \\alpha \\) without looking at \\( \\beta \\). Reducing one error enlarges the other. Choosing the hypothesis after seeing the data. That invalidates the whole test. Testing many things and reporting the one that worked. With twenty tests at 5 %, one comes out significant by pure chance."},{"t":"Self-assessment","d":"Hypothesis tests","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/pruebas-hipotesis#autoevaluacion","x":"06 Self-assessment What is a Type I error? Rejecting \\( H_0 \\) when it is true. Its probability is \\( \\alpha \\), the significance level. What is the power of a test? \\( 1-\\beta \\): the probability of detecting an effect that really exists. How do you reduce both errors at once? By increasing the sample size. Moving the threshold only trades one for the other. What does \\( p = 0.03 \\) mean? That if \\( H_0 \\) were true, a result this extreme would occur 3 % of the time. Can a result be significant and not matter? Yes, and it is common. With large samples, negligible differences give very small p-values. What is the consumer’s risk? Accepting a batch that was bad: a Type II error in the context of quality control."},{"t":"Further reading","d":"Hypothesis tests","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/pruebas-hipotesis#ampliar","x":"07 Further reading Douglas C. Montgomery and George C. Runger. Applied Statistics and Probability for Engineers. 7th ed. (Spanish edition, “Probabilidad y estadística aplicadas a la ingeniería”), Wiley, 2018. Chapters 9 and 10: one- and two-sample tests, power and sample size. Ronald L. Wasserstein and Nicole A. Lazar. The ASA Statement on p-Values: Context, Process, and Purpose. The American Statistician, vol. 70, 2016. The official statement of the American Statistical Association on what a p-value does and does not say. Six pages worth reading. Douglas C. Montgomery. Introduction to Statistical Quality Control. 6th ed. (Spanish edition, “Introducción al control estadístico de la calidad”), Wiley, 2009. Producer’s and consumer’s risk, sampling plans and control charts applied to production."},{"t":"Measuring by comparison","d":"Bridges and component measurement","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/puentes-qmetro#comparar","x":"01 Measuring by comparison A bridge measures a component by comparing it with other known ones. Instead of reading a voltage on an instrument, the known components are adjusted until a detector reads zero. At this balance no current flows through the detector and the result does not depend on its accuracy or on that of the source: only on the reference components. That is why bridges were for a century the most accurate method for measuring resistances, capacitances and inductances. The basic impedance measurement methods are presented in the technician program, in impedance measurements."},{"t":"DC bridges","d":"Bridges and component measurement","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/puentes-qmetro#wheatstone","x":"02 DC bridges The Wheatstone bridge has four resistors in a diamond, a source between two opposite vertices and a galvanometer between the other two. It is balanced when the two dividers give the same voltage: \\[ R_1 R_4 = R_2 R_3 \\] \\[ R_x = \\frac{R_2 R_3}{R_1} \\] With the unknown in position 4. The accuracy is that of the three reference resistors; the sensitivity, that of the detector near zero. For resistances below 1 Ω, the resistance of the leads and contacts adds to the unknown. The Kelvin double bridge eliminates it with a second pair of arms that takes the voltage directly across the resistor, with the same idea as the four-wire measurement used by precision multimeters."},{"t":"AC bridges","d":"Bridges and component measurement","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/puentes-qmetro#alterna","x":"03 AC bridges In AC the arms are impedances and the balance condition \\( Z_1 Z_4 = Z_2 Z_3 \\) is an equality of complex numbers: it requires two things to match at once, the real parts and the imaginary parts. That is why an AC bridge needs two adjustments, and each configuration is designed so that the two are independent. Bridge Measures Reference Maxwell Inductances of low or medium Q (1 to 10) A capacitor in parallel with a resistor Hay High-Q inductances A capacitor in series with a resistor Schering Capacitors and their dissipation factor, also at high voltage A standard capacitor Wien Frequency, or capacitors RC networks The Maxwell bridge measures a real coil, with inductance \\( L_x \\) and series resistance \\( R_x \\), using a capacitor as the reference, which is easier to make accurate than a standard inductance: \\[ L_x = R_2 R_3 C_1 \\] \\[ R_x = \\frac{R_2 R_3}{R_1} \\] \\[ Q = \\omega R_1 C_1 \\] With \\( R_1 \\) in parallel with \\( C_1 \\) in arm 1 and the coil in arm 4. Adjusting \\( C_1 \\) balances the reactive part and adjusting \\( R_1 \\), the resistive part; the quality factor comes from the same two values. Lab · balancing a Maxwell bridge A Maxwell bridge with \\( R_2 = R_3 = 1 \\) kΩ, fed with 1 V at 1 kHz. You choose the unknown coil and adjust \\( C_1 \\) and \\( R_1 \\) until the detector voltage is nulled. The graph shows the detector voltage in decibels; the bridge is considered balanced when it falls below 1% of the voltage across the coil. Coil A Coil B Coil C show t"},{"t":"The Q meter","d":"Bridges and component measurement","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/puentes-qmetro#qmetro","x":"04 The Q meter The Q meter measures the quality factor of a coil by resonance. The coil is connected in series with a calibrated variable capacitor and excited with a small known voltage \\( V_i \\). When the capacitor is adjusted to resonance, the reactances cancel, the current is maximum and the voltage across the capacitor is \\( Q \\cdot V_i \\): a voltmeter across the capacitor is graduated directly in Q. From the capacitance value at resonance the inductance follows, \\( L = 1/(\\omega^2 C) \\). It works at radio frequencies, where bridges lose accuracy because of parasitic capacitances."},{"t":"The modern LCR meter","d":"Bridges and component measurement","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/puentes-qmetro#lcr","x":"05 The modern LCR meter Current LCR meters use a self-balancing bridge: an amplifier holds the voltage of a node at zero by injecting just the right current, and the instrument measures the voltage across the component and that current with a synchronous detector that separates the in-phase and quadrature parts. It obtains instantly what the manual bridge achieved with two adjustments, and at frequencies from 20 Hz to several megahertz. The connection is four-terminal (Kelvin), two for the current and two for the voltage, so that the leads do not add to the result."},{"t":"In the laboratory","d":"Bridges and component measurement","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/puentes-qmetro#laboratorio","x":"06 In the laboratory Activity 1 · A laboratory Wheatstone bridge Build a Wheatstone bridge with a resistance decade box and a galvanometer or an amplifier with instrumentation, measure resistors from 100 Ω to 100 kΩ and compare with a 6½-digit multimeter. Activity 2 · A coil with Maxwell Measure an air-core coil with a Maxwell bridge at 1 kHz and compare L and Q with an LCR meter. Activity 3 · Four-wire resistance Measure a 0.1 Ω resistance with two wires and with four wires, and explain the difference."},{"t":"Common mistakes","d":"Bridges and component measurement","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/puentes-qmetro#errores","x":"07 Common mistakes Adjusting both controls at once on an AC bridge. It is better to alternate: one to the minimum, the other to a lower minimum, and repeat. Measuring low resistances with two wires. The leads and contacts can be worth more than the unknown. Forgetting the measurement frequency. An electrolytic capacitor or a cored inductor changes a lot with it. Ignoring parasitic capacitances. At high frequencies they add to the bridge arms."},{"t":"Self-assessment","d":"Bridges and component measurement","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/puentes-qmetro#autoevaluacion","x":"08 Self-assessment A Wheatstone bridge balances with R₁ = 200 Ω, R₂ = 1 kΩ and R₃ = 470 Ω. What is the unknown? \\( 1000 \\cdot 470 / 200 = 2350\\ \\Omega \\). A Maxwell bridge with R₂ = R₃ = 1 kΩ balances with C₁ = 47 nF and R₁ = 100 kΩ. What are the inductance, the resistance and the Q of the coil at 1 kHz? \\( L_x = 47 \\) mH and \\( R_x = 10\\ \\Omega \\). The quality factor is \\( Q = \\omega R_1 C_1 \\approx 29.5 \\). Why does an AC bridge need two adjustments? Because the balance condition is a complex equality: the real part and the imaginary part have to be matched. In a Q meter, with 10 mV of excitation, the voltmeter across the capacitor reads 1.2 V at resonance. What is the Q? 120."},{"t":"Further reading","d":"Bridges and component measurement","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/puentes-qmetro#ampliar","x":"09 Further reading Albert D. Helfrick and William D. Cooper. Modern Electronic Instrumentation and Measurement Techniques (Spanish edition: “Instrumentación electrónica moderna y técnicas de medición”). Prentice Hall, 1990. DC and AC bridges with their balance conditions and sources of error. Keysight Technologies. Impedance Measurement Handbook. 6th ed., Keysight, 2016. Modern impedance measurement methods, the self-balancing bridge and the four-terminal connection. Ernest O. Doebelin and Dhanesh N. Manik. Measurement Systems: Application and Design. 6th ed., McGraw-Hill, 2011. Bridges as part of measurement systems, with their sensitivities."},{"t":"The atmosphere as a reactor","d":"Environmental chemistry","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/quimica-ambiente#atmosfera","x":"01 The atmosphere as a reactor Environmental chemistry studies reactions that take place in systems that nobody designed and that nobody can opt out of. Two of them get confused all the time and it pays to separate them from the start, because they have different causes, a different altitude and a different solution. Ozone hole Greenhouse effect Where Stratosphere, 15 to 35 km Troposphere, the first 10 km What happens The ozone that filters ultraviolet is destroyed The infrared radiation that would escape is trapped Responsible Chlorofluorocarbons, halons CO₂, CH₄, N₂O and the fluorinated gases Status Recovering since the Montreal Protocol Increasing The last row matters: the ozone hole is the example that a global atmospheric problem can be reversed. The mechanism was identified —a single chlorine atom destroys tens of thousands of ozone molecules in a catalytic cycle—, the culprits were banned and the layer is recovering. It will take until the middle of the century, but it is happening. \\[ \\mathrm{Cl} + \\mathrm{O_3} \\rightarrow \\mathrm{ClO} + \\mathrm{O_2} \\qquad \\mathrm{ClO} + \\mathrm{O} \\rightarrow \\mathrm{Cl} + \\mathrm{O_2} \\] Chlorine comes out of the second reaction intact and starts over: it is a catalyst, and that is why a tiny amount is enough to do enormous damage. It is the same idea of catalysis from the kinetics unit, played against us."},{"t":"The gases used by the electronics industry itself","d":"Environmental chemistry","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/quimica-ambiente#gases","x":"02 The gases used by the electronics industry itself Making chips consumes fluorinated gases for plasma etching and for cleaning the deposition chambers. They are chemically inert, nontoxic and do not touch the ozone layer. The problem is another one: they absorb infrared with extraordinary efficiency and almost never degrade. \\[ \\text{CO}_2\\text{ equivalent} = m \\times \\mathrm{GWP}_{100} \\] The global warming potential compares, over one hundred years, the effect of one kilogram of a gas with that of one kilogram of CO₂. It combines how much it absorbs with how long it lasts in the atmosphere. Lab · global warming potential The process gases of the electronics industry, with their IPCC values. Choose one and the mass emitted, and the calculation translates it into CO₂ equivalent and into kilometers driven by a car. The scale is logarithmic because otherwise they would not fit. carbon dioxide · CO₂ methane · CH₄ nitrous oxide · N₂O carbon tetrafluoride · CF₄ hexafluoroethane · C₂F₆ trifluoromethane · CHF₃ nitrogen trifluoride · NF₃ sulfur hexafluoride · SF₆ Mass emitted = 1 kg NF₃ and the fine print Nitrogen trifluoride entered the industry precisely as a “clean” replacement for other perfluorinated compounds, because it decomposes better in the plasma and leaves less unreacted residue. But what escapes without decomposing has a potential of 16,100, and its use grew with the production of displays and solar panels. It is a good reminder that “less bad than the previous one” i"},{"t":"Electronic waste","d":"Environmental chemistry","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/quimica-ambiente#raee","x":"03 Electronic waste An electronic device at the end of its life is both hazardous waste and a deposit. Both for the same elements. Element Where it comes from Why it is a concern Lead Old solder, tube glass Neurotoxic; it accumulates and is not eliminated Mercury Backlight lamps, some relays It gets methylated and moves up the food chain Cadmium Old batteries, pigments, some semiconductors Kidney damage, highly persistent Hexavalent chromium Anticorrosion coatings Confirmed carcinogen Brominated compounds Flame retardants in circuit boards When burned they give dioxins The RoHS directive restricts six of these substances in devices sold in Europe, and its most visible consequence for anyone who solders was the change to lead-free solder: the tin–silver–copper alloy melts about 30 °C higher than the old tin–lead one, wets less well and demands a higher tip temperature. The WEEE directive is the other half: it requires collecting and treating the device at the end of its life. A ton of boards against a ton of ore A ton of printed circuit boards contains considerably more gold than a ton of ore from an active mine. That turns recycling into urban mining, and explains why the real problem is not technical but logistical: the challenge lies in collecting the material, not in recovering the metal. It also explains the worst scenario, which is open-air burning to recover the copper: it releases dioxins from the brominated compounds and heavy metals into the smoke."},{"t":"Water, which is the other input","d":"Environmental chemistry","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/quimica-ambiente#agua","x":"04 Water, which is the other input A semiconductor plant consumes millions of liters of ultrapure water per day, and returns it loaded with what it carried off: acids, dissolved metals, solvents. Effluent treatment applies in a chain almost everything in this subject. Stage What it does Which unit explains it Neutralization Brings the pH to an acceptable range Equilibrium in solutions Precipitation of metals Converts them to insoluble hydroxides Solubility product Precipitation of fluoride Fixes it as CaF₂ with lime Solubility product Ion exchange Removes the ions that remain Solutions and electrolytes Reverse osmosis Recovers water for reuse Colligative properties The third column is the close of the subject: none of those stages is a new topic. The general chemistry covered in the second level is exactly what is applied so that a chip factory does not ruin the watershed where it is located."},{"t":"In the lab","d":"Environmental chemistry","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/quimica-ambiente#laboratorio","x":"05 In the lab Activity 1 · Neutralize and precipitate Prepare a simulated effluent with acidified copper sulfate. Neutralize with sodium hydroxide while following the pH and observe at what value the copper hydroxide precipitates. Compare with what its \\( K_{sp} \\) predicts. Activity 2 · Compare two solders Solder the same joints with tin–lead alloy and with tin–silver–copper, recording tip temperature and the appearance of the joint. Discuss what changed in the process as a result of the regulatory requirement. Activity 3 · Inventory of a device Disassemble a device no longer in use and take an inventory of its materials: metals, plastics, batteries, display. Classify each fraction by destination —recyclable, hazardous, inert— and estimate what percentage of the mass is recoverable."},{"t":"Common mistakes","d":"Environmental chemistry","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/quimica-ambiente#errores","x":"06 Common mistakes Confusing the ozone hole with the greenhouse effect. Different altitude, different cause, different solution. Believing that an inert gas is harmless. Perfluorinated compounds are inert, and for that very reason they last millennia in the atmosphere. Comparing masses instead of CO₂ equivalent. A kilo of SF₆ is not comparable with a kilo of CO₂. Assuming that lead-free means no problems. It melts higher, wets less well and brought defects of its own such as tin whiskers. Treating electronic waste as ordinary trash. It is hazardous waste and at the same time a resource. Forgetting the atmospheric lifetime. GWP measures not only how much a gas absorbs, but also how long it stays."},{"t":"Self-assessment","d":"Environmental chemistry","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/quimica-ambiente#autoevaluacion","x":"07 Self-assessment Why does one chlorine atom destroy so much ozone? Because it acts as a catalyst: it comes out of the cycle intact and starts over tens of thousands of times. What does GWP measure? How much a kilogram of a gas warms compared with a kilogram of CO₂ over one hundred years, combining absorption and persistence. Why are perfluorinated compounds a concern if they are inert? Precisely for that reason: nothing degrades them, they last thousands of years and absorb infrared very efficiently. What did RoHS change in workshop practice? The solder: lead-free alloy melts about 30 °C higher and wets less well, so it requires a higher tip temperature. What is urban mining? Recovering metals from electronic waste, which has more gold per ton than the ore of an active mine. Why is the ozone hole closing? Because the mechanism was identified and the Montreal Protocol banned chlorofluorocarbons. It is the case of a global problem that can be reversed."},{"t":"Further reading","d":"Environmental chemistry","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/quimica-ambiente#ampliar","x":"08 Further reading Raymond Chang and Kenneth A. Goldsby. Chemistry. 12th ed. (Spanish edition, “Química”), McGraw-Hill, 2017. Chapter 20: atmospheric chemistry, stratospheric ozone, the greenhouse effect and pollution. Colin Baird and Michael Cann. Environmental Chemistry. 2nd ed. (Spanish edition, “Química ambiental”), Reverté, 2014. The complete treatment, with the mechanisms of the stratosphere and persistent pollutants. IPCC. Fifth Assessment Report, Working Group I, Appendix 8.A. Cambridge University Press, 2013. The table of global warming potentials that the values in the lab on this page come from."},{"t":"Why a conductor radiates","d":"Electromagnetic radiation","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/radiacion-electromagnetica#por-que","x":"01 Why a conductor radiates A charge at rest creates an electric field; a charge in uniform motion also creates a magnetic one. But only an accelerated charge sheds fields that move away on their own and carry energy off to infinity: that is radiation. In a conductor carrying alternating current the electrons accelerate and brake all the time, so any conductor with a varying current radiates something. A transmission line hardly radiates because its two conductors carry opposite currents very close to each other, and their far fields cancel. If the ends of a two-wire line are opened out into a T shape, the two currents end up in the same direction, no longer cancel and the structure radiates: it is a dipole, the most elementary antenna."},{"t":"The elementary dipole","d":"Electromagnetic radiation","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/radiacion-electromagnetica#dipolo","x":"02 The elementary dipole The starting point of the calculation is a segment of current \\( I \\) of length \\( \\ell \\) much smaller than the wavelength. Solving Maxwell's equations with retarded potentials, far from the dipole we get: \\[ E_\\theta = j\\,\\frac{\\eta\\, k\\, I \\ell}{4\\pi r}\\, \\sin\\theta \\; e^{-jkr} \\] \\[ H_\\phi = \\frac{E_\\theta}{\\eta} \\] \\( \\theta \\) is the angle measured from the axis of the dipole and \\( k = 2\\pi/\\lambda \\). The far field decreases as \\( 1/r \\), is transverse and behaves locally like a plane wave, with \\( E/H = \\eta = 377\\ \\Omega \\). The fact that the field falls as \\( 1/r \\) is what makes communication possible: the power density falls as \\( 1/r^2 \\), exactly what is needed for the total power that crosses a sphere of any radius to be the same. The factor \\( \\sin\\theta \\) says that the dipole does not radiate along its axis and that it radiates more in the perpendicular plane."},{"t":"Radiation resistance","d":"Electromagnetic radiation","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/radiacion-electromagnetica#resistencia","x":"03 Radiation resistance The radiated power leaves the source just as if it were dissipated in a resistor. That equivalent resistance, the radiation resistance, is defined by \\( P = \\tfrac12 I^2 R_r \\): \\[ R_r = 80\\pi^2 \\left(\\frac{\\ell}{\\lambda}\\right)^2 \\] \\[ R_r = 20\\pi^2 \\left(\\frac{\\ell}{\\lambda}\\right)^2 \\] The first is that of the elementary dipole, with uniform current. The second is that of a real short dipole: the current goes to zero at the tips and falls off linearly, and the resistance is four times smaller. A short dipole has a tiny radiation resistance: one of \\( \\lambda/10 \\) has 2 Ω, and the one-meter antenna of a car in the AM band, a few thousandths of an ohm. Against that, the ohmic resistance of the conductor is no longer negligible, and the efficiency \\( R_r / (R_r + R_p) \\) drops. The half-wave dipole has a radiation resistance of 73 Ω, and that is why it is the reference for practical antennas: it matches effortlessly to a 50 or 75 Ω cable. Its impedance also has a reactive part of about \\( +j42 \\) Ω, which is canceled by shortening it a little."},{"t":"Radiation pattern and directivity","d":"Electromagnetic radiation","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/radiacion-electromagnetica#diagrama","x":"04 Radiation pattern and directivity The radiation pattern is the far field as a function of direction. The directivity compares the intensity in the direction of maximum radiation with what an isotropic source radiating the same power in all directions would have: \\[ D = \\frac{4\\pi\\, U_{max}}{P_{rad}} \\] \\( U \\) is the radiation intensity, in watts per steradian. The short dipole has \\( D = 1.5 \\) (1.76 dBi); the half-wave one, 1.64 (2.15 dBi). Decibels referred to the isotropic source are written dBi. A longer dipole concentrates the radiation more in the perpendicular plane, up to 1.25 λ, where the directivity reaches 5.2 dBi. Longer still, the current reverses direction in parts of the conductor and the pattern opens up into several lobes. Lab · the dipole as a function of its length A thin center-fed dipole, with a sinusoidal current distribution. The graph shows the relative far field in a plane that contains the dipole (vertical, at the center). The values are calculated by integrating the pattern; the efficiency assumes copper wire 2 mm in diameter. 1 MHz (medium wave) 27 MHz (CB band) 146 MHz (VHF) Dipole length \\( \\ell \\) ="},{"t":"Near field and far field","d":"Electromagnetic radiation","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/radiacion-electromagnetica#regiones","x":"05 Near field and far field Close to the antenna, besides the field that moves away, there are fields that store energy and return it on each cycle, as in a capacitor or an inductor. They decrease as \\( 1/r^2 \\) and \\( 1/r^3 \\), and dominate up to a distance of about \\( \\lambda / 2\\pi \\): 48 m for a 1 MHz AM station, 5 cm at 1 GHz. In that region neither the relation \\( E/H = 377\\ \\Omega \\) nor the radiation pattern holds. For antennas that are large compared with the wavelength, the pattern only forms at the Fraunhofer distance, \\( 2D^2/\\lambda \\), with \\( D \\) the largest dimension of the antenna: a 1 m parabolic dish at 10 GHz needs 67 m. That is why antennas are measured on long test ranges or in anechoic chambers with near-field techniques. Some systems work in the near field on purpose: contactless cards and NFC, at 13.56 MHz (wavelength of 22 m), communicate through inductive coupling over a few centimeters, radiating almost nothing."},{"t":"Where it appears in electronics","d":"Electromagnetic radiation","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/radiacion-electromagnetica#electronica","x":"06 Where it appears in electronics Case What decides the radiation Antenna of a transmitter Radiation resistance that is high compared with the losses, and a pattern toward where the receivers are Electromagnetic compatibility of a board Every current loop and every connected cable is an unintentional antenna; a λ/4 cable radiates very well AM antenna of a receiver Electrically short: compensated with a tuned ferrite coil Contactless card or NFC Works in the near field, through magnetic coupling How dipoles are combined to give more gain, and the budget of a complete radio link, are covered in the antennas unit of this course and in antennas, in the technician program."},{"t":"In the lab","d":"Electromagnetic radiation","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/radiacion-electromagnetica#laboratorio","x":"07 In the lab Activity 1 · The pattern of a dipole With a 433 MHz generator and a half-wave dipole as the transmitter, rotate the receiving dipole, connected to a field strength meter, in 15° steps and plot the polar pattern. Activity 2 · Polarization Rotate the receiving dipole around the axis of the link and measure the drop in the signal when it ends up perpendicular to the transmitter. Activity 3 · The board that radiates With a near-field probe and a spectrum analyzer, scan a board with a microcontroller and locate the traces and cables that radiate the most at the harmonics of the clock."},{"t":"Common mistakes","d":"Electromagnetic radiation","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/radiacion-electromagnetica#errores","x":"08 Common mistakes Measuring the pattern in the near field. Within a few wavelengths, the field does not have the shape of the pattern. Confusing radiation resistance with losses. The power in \\( R_r \\) is the power that is radiated: it is the useful one. Ignoring the efficiency of a short antenna. With 0.02 Ω of radiation resistance, a few hundredths of an ohm of loss in the conductor or in the loading coil take most of the power. Believing that a short cable does not radiate. At the harmonics of a clock of tens of MHz, a cable one meter long is an efficient antenna."},{"t":"Self-assessment","d":"Electromagnetic radiation","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/radiacion-electromagnetica#autoevaluacion","x":"09 Self-assessment What radiation resistance does a short dipole of \\( \\lambda/10 \\) have? \\( 20\\pi^2 \\cdot 0.01 \\approx 2 \\) Ω. In what direction does a dipole not radiate? Along its axis, where \\( \\sin\\theta = 0 \\). Up to what distance does the near field of a 100 MHz antenna dominate? Up to about \\( \\lambda/2\\pi = 3 / 2\\pi \\approx 0.48 \\) m. How much more directive is a half-wave dipole than a short one? 1.64 versus 1.5: barely 0.39 dB."},{"t":"Further reading","d":"Electromagnetic radiation","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/radiacion-electromagnetica#ampliar","x":"10 Further reading Constantine A. Balanis. Antenna Theory: Analysis and Design. 4th ed., Wiley, 2016. The reference on antenna theory: the elementary dipole, the finite-length one and the field regions, fully derived. John D. Kraus and Ronald J. Marhefka. Antennas for All Applications. 3rd ed., McGraw-Hill, 2002. A classic with a lot of physical intuition about how an antenna radiates. Henry W. Ott. Electromagnetic Compatibility Engineering. Wiley, 2009. Radiation seen from the other side: how to keep a board or a cable from radiating unintentionally."},{"t":"The ultraviolet catastrophe","d":"Radiation, the electron and X-rays","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/radiacion-electron#cuerponegro","x":"01 The ultraviolet catastrophe Every hot body radiates. Nineteenth-century physics could calculate how: the radiation inside a cavity was treated as a set of modes, each one was assigned the average energy dictated by classical thermodynamics, and the results were summed. The outcome is the Rayleigh-Jeans law: \\[ B_\\lambda = \\frac{2ckT}{\\lambda^4} \\] It works well at long wavelengths and becomes absurd as they get shorter: it grows without limit, and integrated over all wavelengths it gives infinite energy. A glowing coal would radiate X-rays nonstop. It was not an arithmetic mistake: it was the best physics available producing nonsense. Ehrenfest named it the ultraviolet catastrophe, and it was the first clear sign that something was wrong at the foundations. In 1900 Planck found the formula that fit the data, and then looked for where it could come from. The only way he found was to suppose that the oscillators in the cavity could not have just any energy, but only multiples of a minimum packet proportional to the frequency: \\[ E = h\\nu \\qquad\\qquad B_\\lambda = \\frac{2hc^2}{\\lambda^5}\\,\\frac{1}{e^{hc/\\lambda kT} - 1} \\] With \\( h = 6.626\\times10^{-34} \\) J·s. When \\( hc/\\lambda kT \\ll 1 \\) the exponential can be approximated and the formula reduces to Rayleigh-Jeans: classical physics is contained inside as a special case. Lab · Planck's law The Planck curve and the classical prediction on the same graph. See where they coincide and where the classical one shoots up. The ob"},{"t":"The electron","d":"Radiation, the electron and X-rays","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/radiacion-electron#electron","x":"02 The electron In 1897 Thomson studied cathode rays by deflecting them with crossed electric and magnetic fields. By measuring the deflection he obtained the charge-to-mass ratio, and found something remarkable: it always came out the same, regardless of which metal the cathode was made of or what gas was in the tube. \\[ \\frac{e}{m} = 1.759\\times10^{11}\\ \\mathrm{C/kg} \\qquad\\qquad v = \\frac{E}{B} \\ \\text{ with crossed fields and no deflection} \\] The velocity selector in the second expression is the same principle as the mass spectrometer, and the same as the deflection in a cathode-ray tube. That \\( e/m \\) was universal meant that this particle was a constituent of all matter, not a property of the metal: the first hint that the atom had an internal structure. In 1909 Millikan measured the charge separately with his oil-drop experiment, and with the two results the mass of the electron was determined: \\( 9.109\\times10^{-31} \\) kg, about 1836 times smaller than that of the proton."},{"t":"X-rays","d":"Radiation, the electron and X-rays","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/radiacion-electron#rayosx","x":"03 X-rays Röntgen discovered them in 1895 by accident: a fluorescent screen glowed several meters from a cathode-ray tube covered with black cardboard. In an X-ray tube the electrons are accelerated through tens of kilovolts and stopped abruptly against a metal target, and that braking produces two superimposed spectra. Component Origin What defines it Continuous or bremsstrahlung The braking of the electrons The accelerating voltage Characteristic Inner electrons of the target that are knocked out and replaced The target material \\[ \\lambda_{min} = \\frac{hc}{eV} = \\frac{1240}{V[\\mathrm{V}]}\\ \\mathrm{nm} \\] The Duane-Hunt limit: the minimum wavelength of the continuous spectrum, corresponding to the electron that gives up all its energy in a single braking event. With 50 kV it is 0.0248 nm. The very existence of this abrupt limit is already proof of quantization: classically there would be no reason for any cutoff. The characteristic lines are the fingerprint of the element, and from them comes X-ray fluorescence, which identifies materials without destroying them. In electronics it is used to verify RoHS compliance —detecting lead in solder— and X-ray inspection is the only method for seeing the solder joints of a BGA package, which are hidden underneath the chip."},{"t":"In the lab","d":"Radiation, the electron and X-rays","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/radiacion-electron#laboratorio","x":"04 In the lab Activity 1 · Verifying Wien with a filament Power an incandescent lamp at different voltages, measuring the filament resistance to estimate its temperature. Observe the color shift and verify the trend predicted by the displacement law. Activity 2 · Stefan-Boltzmann With a radiation sensor, measure the power emitted by a hot body at several temperatures. Plot it on a logarithmic scale against \\( T \\) and verify that the slope is four. Activity 3 · The charge-to-mass ratio With a fine-beam tube and Helmholtz coils, measure the radius of the electron orbit as a function of the field and of the accelerating voltage. Solve for \\( e/m \\) and compare it with the tabulated value."},{"t":"Common mistakes","d":"Radiation, the electron and X-rays","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/radiacion-electron#errores","x":"05 Common mistakes Believing that Planck proposed that light is quantized. He quantized the oscillators in the wall; light was quantized by Einstein. Using Rayleigh-Jeans outside the far infrared. There it agrees with Planck; beyond that it diverges. Confusing the peak with the perceived color. The eye integrates the whole spectrum, not just the maximum. Forgetting emissivity. The laws hold for an ideal blackbody; a real body emits less. Confusing the continuous spectrum with the characteristic one. One depends on the voltage; the other, on the material. Thinking that X-rays are a different kind of particle. They are light, with a wavelength a thousand times shorter than visible light."},{"t":"Self-assessment","d":"Radiation, the electron and X-rays","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/radiacion-electron#autoevaluacion","x":"06 Self-assessment What is the ultraviolet catastrophe? The classical prediction that the radiation from a hot body grows without limit as the wavelength gets shorter, with infinite total energy. What did Planck assume to avoid it? That the energy of the oscillators could only take multiples of \\( h\\nu \\). Where is the emission peak of the human body? Near 9.4 µm, in the far infrared. That is why thermal cameras work between 8 and 14 µm. What did Thomson measure? The charge-to-mass ratio of the electron, and he found that it was the same with any cathode and any gas. What does the Duane-Hunt limit depend on? Only on the accelerating voltage: \\( \\lambda_{min} = 1240/V \\) in nanometers with \\( V \\) in volts. What are X-rays used for in electronics? To inspect hidden solder joints of BGA packages and, through fluorescence, to verify RoHS compliance."},{"t":"Further reading","d":"Radiation, the electron and X-rays","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/radiacion-electron#ampliar","x":"07 Further reading Robert Eisberg and Robert Resnick. Quantum Physics of Atoms, Molecules, Solids, Nuclei, and Particles. 2nd ed., John Wiley & Sons, 1985 (Spanish edition, “Física cuántica: átomos, moléculas, sólidos, núcleos y partículas”, Limusa). Chapter 1 develops thermal radiation and Planck's hypothesis with all the historical detail. Kenneth Krane. Modern Physics. 3rd ed., Wiley, 2019 (Spanish edition, “Física moderna”). A good treatment of the discovery of the electron, Millikan and the production of X-rays. Thomas Kuhn. Black-Body Theory and the Quantum Discontinuity, 1894-1912. University of Chicago Press, 1987. What Planck really thought when he introduced the quantum. A corrective to the textbook version."},{"t":"The superheterodyne receiver and its successors","d":"Receivers","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/receptores#super","x":"01 The superheterodyne receiver and its successors A receiver has to find a weak signal among others that are much stronger. The superheterodyne, invented by Edwin Armstrong in 1918, solves it with a mixer that translates each channel to a fixed intermediate frequency, where a selective filter and an amplifier with automatic gain control do the work. Current integrated receivers also use direct conversion, which brings the channel down to baseband with an I/Q demodulator, or the low-IF approach, and do the channel filtering digitally. Whatever the architecture, three figures describe it: sensitivity, selectivity and dynamic range."},{"t":"Sensitivity","d":"Receivers","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/receptores#sensibilidad","x":"02 Sensitivity The weakest signal that the receiver can demodulate with the required quality is set by the thermal noise, the bandwidth and the noise figure of the chain, computed with the Friis formula from the introduction of Communication Systems: \\[ P_{floor} = -174\\ \\mathrm{dBm/Hz} + 10\\log B + NF \\] \\[ S_{min} = P_{floor} + SNR_{min} \\] −174 dBm/Hz is kT at 290 K. A receiver with a 200 kHz bandwidth and a 6 dB noise figure has a floor of −115 dBm; if the demodulation needs 10 dB of signal-to-noise ratio, its sensitivity is −105 dBm."},{"t":"Dynamic range and intermodulation","d":"Receivers","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/receptores#rango","x":"03 Dynamic range and intermodulation At the upper end, the limit is set by strong signals. Two signals in neighboring channels generate third-order intermodulation products that can fall on the desired channel. Referred to the input, they grow 3 dB for each dB of the signals, and the line meets that of the fundamental at the input intercept point IIP3. The spurious-free dynamic range is the difference between the strongest signal that produces no intermodulation visible above the floor and that floor: \\[ SFDR = \\tfrac{2}{3}\\,\\big(IIP3 - P_{floor}\\big) \\] Improving the noise figure lowers the floor and improving the linearity raises IIP3: both enlarge the range, but a low-noise amplifier with a lot of gain at the front worsens the IIP3 of the whole receiver. It is the central trade-off in the design of the chain. Lab · floor, intermodulation and dynamic range Everything referred to the receiver input. The blue line is the signal; the red one, the third-order intermodulation product with two tones of equal level; the gray line, the noise floor. The spurious-free dynamic range goes from the floor to the level at which the intermodulation reaches it. Noise figure = IIP3 = 12.5 kHz channel 200 kHz channel 20 MHz channel"},{"t":"Selectivity and blocking","d":"Receivers","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/receptores#selectividad","x":"04 Selectivity and blocking Selectivity is the ability to reject a strong adjacent channel, and it is provided by IF or digital filters. But a very strong signal can cause trouble even if the filter rejects it: it compresses the input amplifier ( blocking ), or it mixes with the phase noise of the local oscillator and translates it onto the desired channel ( reciprocal mixing ). That is why the phase noise of oscillators is a receiver specification."},{"t":"In the lab","d":"Receivers","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/receptores#laboratorio","x":"05 In the lab Activity 1 · Sensitivity Measure the sensitivity of an FM receiver by lowering the level of a generator until the audio signal-to-noise ratio falls to 12 dB SINAD, and compare it with the calculated one. Activity 2 · Intermodulation Apply two out-of-channel tones whose third-order product falls in the channel, and measure the level that makes it audible. Calculate IIP3. Activity 3 · Blocking With a desired signal near the sensitivity, raise a signal 1 MHz away until the desired one degrades by 3 dB."},{"t":"Common mistakes","d":"Receivers","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/receptores#errores","x":"06 Common mistakes Maximizing the input gain. It improves the noise figure, but worsens the linearity of the whole receiver. Forgetting the bandwidth in the sensitivity. A channel ten times wider has a floor 10 dB higher. Ignoring the phase noise of the local oscillator. With strong neighboring signals, reciprocal mixing masks the weak one. Confusing IIP3 with OIP3. They differ by the gain of the chain."},{"t":"Self-assessment","d":"Receivers","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/receptores#autoevaluacion","x":"07 Self-assessment What noise floor does a receiver with a 1 MHz bandwidth and a 3 dB noise figure have? \\( -174 + 60 + 3 = -111 \\) dBm. With that floor and IIP3 = −5 dBm, what is the SFDR? \\( \\tfrac23 (-5 + 111) \\approx 70.7 \\) dB. What is reciprocal mixing? The translation of the local oscillator's phase noise onto the desired channel, caused by a strong neighboring signal. What advantage does direct conversion have? It has no image frequency and is easily integrated, with channel filtering at baseband."},{"t":"Further reading","d":"Receivers","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/receptores#ampliar","x":"08 Further reading Behzad Razavi. RF Microelectronics. 2nd ed., Prentice Hall, 2011. Receiver architectures, sensitivity and dynamic range. Kevin McClaning. Wireless Receiver Design for Digital Communications. 2nd ed., SciTech, 2012. The design of the receive chain with its noise and linearity budgets. Ulrich L. Rohde and Jerry C. Whitaker. Communications Receivers: DSP, Software Radios, and Design. 3rd ed., McGraw-Hill, 2001. The classic reference on receivers, from the superheterodyne to software-defined radio."},{"t":"Two pieces of data are enough","d":"Lines and planes","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/recta-y-plano#idea","x":"01 Two pieces of data are enough All the analytic geometry of space rests on two simple ideas: A line It is defined by a point and a direction. The direction is given by a vector, called the direction vector. A plane It is defined by a point and a perpendicular. That perpendicular is given by the normal vector. There are other ways to fix them too —two points determine a line; three non-collinear points, a plane—, but they all end up reducing to the two above. It is worth keeping in mind: almost all exercises are solved by finding the missing point and vector."},{"t":"Equations of the line","d":"Lines and planes","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/recta-y-plano#recta","x":"02 Equations of the line With a point P₀(x₀, y₀, z₀) and a direction vector d = (a, b, c), a generic point of the line is reached by walking from P₀ a multiple of d: \\[ \\vec{r} = {\\vec{r}}_0 + t \\vec{d} \\quad \\begin{array}{c} x = x_0 + a t \\\\ y = y_0 + b t \\\\ z = z_0 + c t \\end{array} \\quad \\frac{x -x_0}{a} = \\frac{y -y_0}{b} = \\frac{z -z_0}{c} \\] Vector, parametric and symmetric equations: the same line written three ways. The parameter t covers the whole line as it goes from −∞ to +∞. The symmetric equations fail if any component is zero You cannot divide by a = 0. If the direction vector is (0, 2, 3), the line is written x = x₀ together with the other two fractions. The parametric equations do not have this problem, and that is why they are the ones to use when working. In the plane, the same line also admits the slope-intercept form y = mx + h, the general form Ax + By + C = 0 and the intercept form x/p + y/q = 1, where p and q are the intercepts with the axes."},{"t":"Equations of the plane","d":"Lines and planes","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/recta-y-plano#plano","x":"03 Equations of the plane If n = (A, B, C) is normal to the plane and P₀ belongs to it, then any point P of the plane satisfies that the vector P₀P is perpendicular to n. Writing that condition with the dot product gives everything: \\[ \\vec{n} \\cdot (\\vec{r} -{\\vec{r}}_0) = 0 \\quad \\implies \\quad A x + B y + C z + D = 0 \\] The coefficients of the general equation are the components of the normal. Reading the normal of a plane is reading the first three numbers. If the plane is given by three points, the normal is obtained with a cross product: n = AB × AC. If it is given by two directions contained in it, the same. The cross product is the tool that goes from “directions inside the plane” to “direction perpendicular to it”. Worked example Plane through A(1, 0, 2), B(3, 1, 0) and C(0, 2, 1). AB = (2, 1, −2) and AC = (−1, 2, −1). n = AB × AC = (1·(−1) − (−2)·2, (−2)(−1) − 2(−1), 2·2 − 1·(−1)) = (3, 4, 5). With point A: 3(x − 1) + 4(y − 0) + 5(z − 2) = 0, that is 3x + 4y + 5z − 13 = 0. Check with B: 9 + 4 + 0 − 13 = 0 ✔ and with C: 0 + 8 + 5 − 13 = 0 ✔"},{"t":"Relative positions","d":"Lines and planes","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/recta-y-plano#posiciones","x":"04 Relative positions Between What is compared Cases Two lines The direction vectors and a point Parallel (proportional direction vectors and no shared points), coincident, intersecting (they meet at a point) or skew: they neither meet nor are parallel, something that exists only in space Line and plane \\( d \\cdot n \\) If \\( \\vec{d} \\cdot \\vec{n} \\ne 0 \\) they meet at a point; if \\( \\vec{d} \\cdot \\vec{n} = 0 \\), the line is parallel to the plane, and it is contained in it if, in addition, one of its points satisfies the plane’s equation Two planes The normals Proportional normals: parallel or coincident. Otherwise, they meet in a line Working out relative positions is, once again, solving a linear system: two planes that intersect are a consistent system with infinitely many solutions and one free parameter —the line of intersection—, and two distinct parallel planes give an inconsistent system. Everything in systems of linear equations applies here under geometric names."},{"t":"Distances and angles","d":"Lines and planes","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/recta-y-plano#distancias","x":"05 Distances and angles \\[ d (P, \\pi) = \\frac{\\left| A x_0 + B y_0 + C z_0 + D \\right|}{\\sqrt{A^2 + B^2 + C^2}} \\quad d (P, r) = \\frac{\\left| \\vec{P_0 P} \\times \\vec{d} \\right|}{\\left| \\vec{d} \\right|} \\] The first is the projection of the vector going from the plane to the point onto the unit normal. The second comes from the area of the parallelogram divided by the base. Angle between two planes: the angle between their normals, cos α = |n₁ · n₂| / (|n₁| |n₂|). Angle between two lines: that of their direction vectors, with the same formula. Angle between a line and a plane: the complement of the angle formed by the direction vector and the normal: sin α = |d · n| / (|d| |n|). Lab · plane, point and another plane The coefficients are those of the normal. The computation is shown worked out, not just the result. π₁: x + y + z + = 0 P: (,, ) π₂: x + y + z + = 0 Distance from the point to plane π₁ The two planes with respect to each other"},{"t":"Where this shows up","d":"Lines and planes","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/recta-y-plano#aplicaciones","x":"06 Where this shows up Drafting and CAD. Every part modeled in three dimensions is made of planes and lines: views, sections and intersections are computed this way. It is the foundation of technical drawing and computer-aided design. Antenna pointing. The direction of maximum radiation is a vector; the elevation angle and the azimuth are, exactly, the angles between that vector and reference planes. Positioning. A GPS solves the intersection of spheres; a time-difference location, the intersection of hyperboloids. The setup is the same: analytic geometry with systems. Working planes. The optimal tilt of a solar panel, the plane of a printed circuit board relative to the enclosure or the alignment of a sensor are computed with angles between normals."},{"t":"In the lab","d":"Lines and planes","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/recta-y-plano#laboratorio","x":"07 In the lab Exercise 1 · From the point to the plane Find the equation of the plane through three chosen points, verify that all three satisfy it and compute the distance from a fourth point. Check with the lab above. Exercise 2 · Skew lines Propose two lines in space and decide their relative position. If they are skew, compute the distance between them with the scalar triple product: d = |(P₂ − P₁) · (d₁ × d₂)| / |d₁ × d₂|. Exercise 3 · Measuring it in the workshop Take a real part —a heat sink, an enclosure— and describe two of its faces as planes using three points measured with a caliper. Compute the angle between them and compare it with the direct measurement with a protractor."},{"t":"Common mistakes","d":"Lines and planes","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/recta-y-plano#errores","x":"08 Common mistakes Confusing the direction vector with the normal. The direction vector goes along the line; the normal goes perpendicular to the plane. Writing the symmetric equations with a zero in the denominator. Forgetting the absolute value in the distance formulas, and getting negative distances. Assuming that two lines that do not meet are parallel: in space they can be skew. Not normalizing when projecting onto the normal: the distance has the magnitude of n in the denominator. Giving the angle between planes as that of the normals without taking the absolute value, and getting the supplementary angle."},{"t":"Self-assessment","d":"Lines and planes","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/recta-y-plano#autoevaluacion","x":"09 Self-assessment What is the normal of the plane 2x − y + 3z = 7? (2, −1, 3): the coefficients of x, y and z. Distance from the origin to the plane 3x + 4y + 5z − 13 = 0. |−13| / √(9 + 16 + 25) = 13 / √50 ≈ 1.84. How do you decide whether a line is parallel to a plane? By checking that d · n = 0. If, in addition, a point of the line satisfies the plane’s equation, the line is contained in the plane. What are two skew lines? Two lines in space that do not meet and are not parallel: they lie in different planes. They do not exist in the plane. Write the line through (1, 2, 3) with direction (0, 1, −2) in parametric form. x = 1, y = 2 + t, z = 3 − 2t. What is the angle between the planes x + y = 0 and x − y = 0? Normals (1, 1, 0) and (1, −1, 0): cos α = |1 − 1| / (√2·√2) = 0, so α = 90°."},{"t":"Further reading","d":"Lines and planes","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/recta-y-plano#ampliar","x":"10 Further reading Charles H. Lehmann. Analytic Geometry. (Spanish edition, “Geometría analítica”), Limusa. The classic treatment of the line and the plane, with a systematic account of relative positions. Stanley I. Grossman. Álgebra lineal. 6th ed., McGraw-Hill, 2008 (in Spanish). Approaches the line and the plane from vectors, which is the short route for this course. Howard Anton. Elementary Linear Algebra. 5th ed. (Spanish edition, “Introducción al álgebra lineal”), Limusa Wiley, 2011. Good figures and exercises on distances and angles in space."},{"t":"Uncontrolled rectifiers","d":"Power rectifiers","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/rectificadores-potencia#no-controlados","x":"01 Uncontrolled rectifiers A diode rectifier delivers a fixed DC voltage, which depends only on the line. With a highly inductive load, the output current is nearly constant and each diode conducts for a portion of the cycle: Configuration Average voltage Pulses per cycle Single-phase bridge \\( \\frac{2\\sqrt2}{\\pi} V \\approx 0.90\\,V \\) 2 Three-phase bridge (six-pulse) \\( \\frac{3\\sqrt2}{\\pi} V_{LL} \\approx 1.35\\,V_{LL} \\) 6 Two bridges with a wye-delta transformer Twice as much, with less ripple 12 With a capacitor at the output, on the other hand, the line current is a series of narrow peaks near the voltage maximum: this is how the rectifiers of nearly all electronic equipment work, and how they pollute the grid with harmonics, a topic of power quality. Active power factor correction is covered in switching power supplies."},{"t":"Controlled rectifiers","d":"Power rectifiers","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/rectificadores-potencia#controlados","x":"02 Controlled rectifiers By replacing the diodes with thyristors, each one is triggered with a delay \\( \\alpha \\) relative to the instant at which a diode would start to conduct. In a three-phase bridge with an inductive load: \\[ V_{dc} = \\frac{3\\sqrt2}{\\pi} V_{LL} \\cos\\alpha - \\frac{3}{\\pi}\\,\\omega L_s I_d \\] The second term is the commutation drop: the line inductance \\( L_s \\) prevents the current from passing instantaneously from one thyristor to the next, and during an angle \\( \\mu \\) two conduct at the same time. Between 90° and about 150° the average voltage is negative: the bridge works as an inverter and returns energy to the line, if there is a source on the DC side, such as a braking motor. The current of each phase is a nearly rectangular 120° wave, shifted by \\( \\alpha \\) relative to its voltage. It contains the harmonics of order \\( 6k \\pm 1 \\) —5, 7, 11, 13…— with amplitudes close to \\( 1/h \\) of the fundamental, and its power factor is approximately \\( 0.955 \\cos\\alpha \\). Lab · controlled three-phase bridge A six-thyristor bridge connected to a 400 V, 50 Hz line, with a highly inductive load of constant current I d. Top, the output voltage (blue) and its average value (yellow); bottom, the current of phase a (orange) along with the shape of its voltage, in gray. Firing angle α = Line inductance L s = Load current I d ="},{"t":"Applications","d":"Power rectifiers","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/rectificadores-potencia#aplicaciones","x":"03 Applications Controlled rectifiers feed DC motors ( DC machines ), electrolysis plants, furnaces and high-power battery chargers, and are the basis of high-voltage direct-current transmission, where one bridge rectifies at one end and another, with α close to 150°, inverts at the other. For small and medium powers they were replaced by diode rectifiers followed by a switching converter, or by active rectifiers with transistors, which draw sinusoidal current with unity power factor."},{"t":"In the laboratory","d":"Power rectifiers","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/rectificadores-potencia#laboratorio","x":"04 In the laboratory Activity 1 · Single-phase bridge With a half-controlled bridge on a resistive and an inductive load, measure the average voltage as a function of the firing angle and compare it with the formula. Activity 2 · Harmonics With a power quality analyzer, measure the current spectrum of a three-phase bridge and verify orders 5, 7, 11 and 13. Activity 3 · Commutation Add inductances in series with the line and observe the notches in the output voltage."},{"t":"Common mistakes","d":"Power rectifiers","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/rectificadores-potencia#errores","x":"05 Common mistakes Forgetting the commutation drop. With a weak grid, the average voltage is considerably lower than the ideal one. Pushing the firing angle close to 180° in inverter mode. The outgoing thyristor does not manage to turn off and the bridge fails. Confusing the displacement factor with the power factor. Harmonics lower the power factor even if cos φ is high. Applying the inductive-load formulas to a resistive load. With a resistive load and α greater than 60° the current becomes discontinuous and the average voltage is different."},{"t":"Self-assessment","d":"Power rectifiers","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/rectificadores-potencia#autoevaluacion","x":"06 Self-assessment What average voltage does a three-phase diode bridge give on 400 V? \\( 1.35 \\cdot 400 \\approx 540 \\) V. What firing angle gives 270 V, with no line inductance? 60°, because cos 60° = 0.5. What harmonics does the current of a six-pulse bridge have? Those of order 6k ± 1: 5, 7, 11, 13, 17, 19… What is needed for the bridge to work as an inverter? An energy source on the DC side that sustains the current, and α between 90° and about 150°."},{"t":"Further reading","d":"Power rectifiers","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/rectificadores-potencia#ampliar","x":"07 Further reading Ned Mohan, Tore M. Undeland and William P. Robbins. Power Electronics: Converters, Applications, and Design. 3rd ed., Wiley, 2003. Diode and thyristor rectifiers, including commutation. Muhammad H. Rashid. Power Electronics: Devices, Circuits, and Applications. 4th ed., Pearson. (Spanish edition: “Electrónica de potencia”.) Controlled rectifiers with their performance parameters. Jos Arrillaga and Neville R. Watson. Power System Harmonics. 2nd ed., Wiley, 2003. The harmonics of converters and their effect on the grid."},{"t":"The human resources functions","d":"Human resources and labor relations","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/recursos-humanos#funciones","x":"01 The human resources functions People are the resource that decides how all the others are used. The human resources area takes care of their complete cycle in the company: Function What it does Planning Estimates how many people and with what profiles are needed, based on the production plans Recruitment and selection Attracts candidates and chooses, through interviews, technical tests and references, the one who best fits the position Onboarding and training Integrates newcomers and develops everyone’s competencies, including safety training Performance appraisal Compares what was achieved with what was expected and guides training and career Compensation Sets salaries that are equitable between positions and competitive against the market Health, safety and well-being Safe working conditions, as covered in occupational risk management"},{"t":"Motivation","d":"Human resources and labor relations","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/recursos-humanos#motivacion","x":"02 Motivation The Hawthorne studies showed that performance does not depend on pay alone. Maslow proposed a hierarchy of needs —physiological, safety, social, esteem and self-actualization— in which the higher ones motivate when the lower ones are met. Herzberg distinguished hygiene factors —salary, conditions, supervision—, whose absence produces dissatisfaction but whose presence does not motivate, from motivators —achievement, recognition, responsibility, the work itself—. McGregor contrasted two management assumptions: Theory X, which sees the worker as someone who avoids effort and has to be controlled, and Theory Y, which sees the worker as capable of self-direction if the work has meaning."},{"t":"Point-factor job evaluation","d":"Human resources and labor relations","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/recursos-humanos#puestos","x":"03 Point-factor job evaluation For salaries to be equitable, different positions have to be compared with the same yardstick. In the point-factor method, factors are chosen —education, experience, responsibility, effort, working conditions—, a weight is assigned to them, each degree of each factor is described and the position is rated, not the person who holds it: \\[ \\text{points} = \\sum_i w_i\\, g_i \\] With the points of benchmark positions and their salaries, a pay line is fitted by least squares. A position far above or below the line indicates an internal inequity; the comparison with market surveys indicates whether the company pays more or less than its competitors. Lab · point-factor job evaluation Five factors with weights education 25, experience 20, responsibility 25, effort 15 and conditions 15, in degrees from 1 to 5. The blue dots are five benchmark positions with their salary index (operator 100); the line is the fit. The yellow dot is the position being evaluated with the chosen degrees. Education = Experience = Responsibility = Effort = Working conditions ="},{"t":"Labor relations","d":"Human resources and labor relations","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/recursos-humanos#relaciones","x":"04 Labor relations Labor relations link the company, the workers and their unions, and the State. The working conditions of each industry are set in collective agreements, negotiated between employers’ chambers and unions, which establish job categories, minimum wages, working hours and leave above the legal minimums. In Argentina, the general framework is the Employment Contract Law (Ley de Contrato de Trabajo), No. 20,744, which was covered in labor law; like any rule, its current text should be consulted on InfoLEG (the national legislative information service). Conflicts are channeled through negotiation, conciliation and, where applicable, direct action; a good work climate prevents them."},{"t":"In the laboratory","d":"Human resources and labor relations","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/recursos-humanos#laboratorio","x":"05 In the laboratory Activity 1 · Job description Write the description and profile of the test technician position at an electronics company: tasks, responsibilities, requirements. Activity 2 · Point evaluation In groups, evaluate three positions of the university laboratory with the point-factor method and compare the results between groups. Activity 3 · Collective agreement Read the job categories of the collective agreement for the electronics or metalworking industry and place the evaluated positions within them."},{"t":"Common mistakes","d":"Human resources and labor relations","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/recursos-humanos#errores","x":"06 Common mistakes Evaluating the person instead of the position. Job evaluation rates what the position demands, not how its holder performs it. Believing that salary motivates everything. Its absence demotivates, but its presence is not enough. Selecting only for technical knowledge. Teamwork and communication weigh as much as technical skill. Quoting a law from memory. Rules change; the current text has to be consulted."},{"t":"Self-assessment","d":"Human resources and labor relations","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/recursos-humanos#autoevaluacion","x":"07 Self-assessment How many points does a position with degrees 3, 2, 3, 2 and 2 have? \\( 25 \\cdot 3 + 20 \\cdot 2 + 25 \\cdot 3 + 15 \\cdot 2 + 15 \\cdot 2 = 250 \\). What salary index does the lab’s pay line give it? About 118. What are Herzberg’s hygiene factors? Those that, if absent, produce dissatisfaction, but whose presence does not motivate: salary, conditions, supervision. What does a collective agreement establish? The categories, minimum wages, working hours and other working conditions of an industry, above the legal minimums."},{"t":"Further reading","d":"Human resources and labor relations","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/recursos-humanos#ampliar","x":"08 Further reading Idalberto Chiavenato. Gestión del talento humano. McGraw-Hill. (In Spanish.) The human resources functions, from selection to compensation. Gary Dessler. Human Resource Management. Pearson. (Spanish edition: “Administración de recursos humanos”.) Job evaluation and compensation systems, with cases. Frederick Herzberg. One More Time: How Do You Motivate Employees? Harvard Business Review, 1968. The classic article on hygiene factors and motivators."},{"t":"Layers and encapsulation","d":"Data networks and protocols","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/redes-datos#capas","x":"01 Layers and encapsulation A data network solves many problems at once: transmitting bits over a medium, sharing the medium among stations, finding the path between networks, delivering the data complete and in order, and giving it meaning. So that each problem can be solved separately, protocols are organized in layers: each one uses the service of the one below and offers one to the one above. The OSI model has seven; the TCP/IP stack, which is the one actually used, has four: TCP/IP layer Function Examples Unit Application The meaning of the data HTTP, DNS, MQTT, SSH Message Transport Process-to-process communication, reliability TCP, UDP Segment Internet Addressing and routing between networks IPv4, IPv6, ICMP Packet Network access Transmission on a link Ethernet, Wi-Fi Frame Each layer adds its header to the data from the layer above: this is encapsulation. A TCP segment with 1460 bytes of data travels with 20 bytes of TCP, 20 of IP and, on Ethernet, 14 of header, 4 of check sequence, 8 of preamble and 12 of inter-frame gap: 1538 bytes on the wire, 95% efficiency. Short-range buses were covered in serial protocols."},{"t":"Link and network: Ethernet and IP","d":"Data networks and protocols","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/redes-datos#enlace","x":"02 Link and network: Ethernet and IP Ethernet identifies each interface with a 48-bit physical address. Switches learn which port each address is on and forward each frame only toward its destination; with full-duplex links there are no collisions anymore. Wi-Fi, by contrast, shares the medium and uses carrier-sense access with collision avoidance. IP gives each interface a logical address, 32 bits in IPv4 and 128 in IPv6, organized in networks with a prefix: in 192.168.1.0/24, the first 24 bits identify the network and 254 addresses remain for hosts. Routers forward each packet according to their table, toward the next hop, without guaranteeing that it arrives or in what order. Within a local network, the ARP protocol translates the IP address into the physical one."},{"t":"Transport: UDP and TCP","d":"Data networks and protocols","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/redes-datos#tcp","x":"03 Transport: UDP and TCP UDP delivers datagrams with no guarantees and no connection: it is what suits voice, live video or periodic measurements, where a late datum is useless. TCP builds a reliable, ordered stream: it numbers the bytes, the receiver acknowledges what it received and the sender retransmits what is lost. To avoid waiting for an acknowledgment for each segment, it keeps a window of unacknowledged data in flight, and that limits its speed: \\[ R \\le \\frac{W}{RTT} \\] \\[ R \\lesssim \\frac{MSS}{RTT} \\cdot \\frac{1.22}{\\sqrt{p}} \\] \\( W \\) is the window, \\( RTT \\) the round-trip time, \\( MSS \\) the maximum segment size and \\( p \\) the loss probability. The second bound, by Mathis and coauthors, comes from congestion control: TCP halves the window at each loss and grows it by one segment per RTT. To fill a link, the window has to reach the bandwidth-delay product: the data that fit \"in the wire\". The original TCP window was 64 KB; with a 100 ms RTT that limits the rate to 5.2 Mbit/s even if the link is 1 Gbit/s. The window scaling option extends it up to 1 GB. Lab · what limits a TCP connection? The three bounds on the throughput of a connection: the link, the window and the losses. The throughput is the smallest; the highlighted bar is the one in control. Segments of 1460 bytes. Link speed = Round-trip time = 64 KB window (no scaling) 1 MB window 16 MB window Loss probability ="},{"t":"Applications and the Internet of Things","d":"Data networks and protocols","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/redes-datos#aplicacion","x":"04 Applications and the Internet of Things Application protocols run on top of TCP and UDP. HTTP transfers pages and web service data; DNS translates names into addresses; TLS encrypts and authenticates the connection beneath HTTPS. In the Internet of Things, where devices have little memory and energy, lightweight protocols are used: MQTT, publish-subscribe over TCP, and CoAP, over UDP. A sensor that sends one measurement per minute hardly uses any bandwidth: what matters is how long the radio has to stay on to do it."},{"t":"In the laboratory","d":"Data networks and protocols","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/redes-datos#laboratorio","x":"05 In the laboratory Activity 1 · Capturing the encapsulation Use Wireshark to capture the loading of a page and identify in one frame the Ethernet, IP, TCP and HTTP headers. Activity 2 · Throughput Measure the throughput between two hosts with iperf, and add delay and losses with the operating system’s network emulator. Compare with the bounds from the lab. Activity 3 · MQTT Publish sensor measurements from a Wi-Fi microcontroller to an MQTT server and subscribe from a computer."},{"t":"Common mistakes","d":"Data networks and protocols","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/redes-datos#errores","x":"06 Common mistakes Expecting a faster link to speed up a distant transfer. With a small window or losses, the link is not the limit. Confusing the physical address with the IP. The first identifies the interface on the link; the second, in the network. Using TCP for real-time audio. Retransmissions arrive late; UDP with its own loss tolerance is better. Forgetting the header on small packets. With few bytes of data, the efficiency drops a lot."},{"t":"Self-assessment","d":"Data networks and protocols","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/redes-datos#autoevaluacion","x":"07 Self-assessment How many host addresses does a /26 network have? \\( 2^6 - 2 = 62 \\). What window is needed to fill 1 Gbit/s with a 50 ms RTT? \\( 10^9 \\cdot 0.05/8 \\approx 6.25 \\) MB. With a 64 KB window and a 20 ms RTT, what is the throughput bound? \\( 65\\,535 \\cdot 8/0.02 \\approx 26 \\) Mbit/s. Why is MQTT suitable for sensors? Because it has minimal headers and a publish-subscribe model that decouples sensors from consumers."},{"t":"Further reading","d":"Data networks and protocols","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/redes-datos#ampliar","x":"08 Further reading Andrew S. Tanenbaum, Nick Feamster and David Wetherall. Computer Networks. 6th ed., Pearson, 2021. The classic reference, layer by layer. James F. Kurose and Keith W. Ross. Computer Networking: A Top-Down Approach. 7th ed., Pearson, 2016. Spanish edition: “Redes de computadoras: un enfoque descendente”, Pearson, 2017. From the applications downward, with TCP and its congestion control in detail. Matthew Mathis, Jeffrey Semke, Jamshid Mahdavi and Teunis Ott. The Macroscopic Behavior of the TCP Congestion Avoidance Algorithm. ACM SIGCOMM Computer Communication Review, 27(3), 1997. The paper on the throughput bound with losses."},{"t":"A radar for cables","d":"Reflectometry","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/reflectometria#principio","x":"01 A radar for cables Time-domain reflectometry (TDR) injects a fast step into a transmission line and observes the voltage at the input. While the step travels along a uniform line, the input sees the characteristic impedance and the voltage stays at a fixed value. When the wavefront meets a discontinuity, part of it comes back, and the voltage at the input changes after the round-trip time. The theory of reflections was covered in transmission lines, in Transmission Media. \\[ d = \\frac{v_p\\, t}{2} = \\frac{v_f\\, c\\, t}{2} \\] \\[ \\Gamma = \\frac{Z_L - Z_0}{Z_L + Z_0} \\] \\( t \\) is the time from the step to the change in the trace, and \\( v_f \\) the velocity factor of the cable: 0.66 for solid polyethylene, about 0.8 for foam dielectrics. With a 50 Ω generator and a 50 Ω line, the trace goes from half the step to \\( (1 + \\Gamma)/2 \\)."},{"t":"How to read a trace","d":"Reflectometry","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/reflectometria#lectura","x":"02 How to read a trace Discontinuity What is seen Open circuit The voltage doubles: Γ = +1 Short circuit The voltage drops to zero: Γ = −1 Resistance greater than Z 0 A step upward, proportional to Γ Resistance less than Z 0 A step downward Capacitance First it drops, like a short circuit, and rises as it charges Inductance First it rises, like an open, and falls as the current builds up The time constant of the capacitive response is \\( Z_0 C \\) and that of the inductive one, \\( L/Z_0 \\): the value is deduced from the shape of the trace. A loose connector, a crushed cable, a splice or a via on a printed circuit show up as small discontinuities at their position. Lab · the trace of a reflectometer A 50 Ω TDR with an ideal 1 V step on a 50 Ω cable terminated in the chosen load. The trace is the voltage at the cable input as a function of time. Distance to the load = Cable with solid dielectric (factor 0.66) Cable with foam dielectric (factor 0.80) Resistive load Capacitive load Inductive load Load value ="},{"t":"Resolution and range","d":"Reflectometry","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/reflectometria#resolucion","x":"03 Resolution and range Two discontinuities can be told apart if they are separated by more than the wavefront travels during its rise time: with a 35 ps rise time and a velocity factor of 0.66, about 3.5 mm of separation, enough to see vias and connectors on a board. TDRs for installation cables use slower, more powerful pulses, which reach kilometers but resolve meters. In optical fiber, the equivalent is the OTDR, which measures the backscattered light and shows splices, connectors and breaks along the link, as seen in optical fibers."},{"t":"In the laboratory","d":"Reflectometry","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/reflectometria#laboratorio","x":"04 In the laboratory Activity 1 · TDR with an oscilloscope With a fast pulse generator and an oscilloscope in parallel with the input of a coaxial cable, measure the length of the cable with the far end open and shorted. Calculate the velocity factor. Activity 2 · Loads Terminate the cable with 25 Ω, 50 Ω, 100 Ω, a capacitor and an inductor, and compare the traces with those of the lab. Activity 3 · Hidden fault Crush a point on a cable and locate the discontinuity with the TDR."},{"t":"Common mistakes","d":"Reflectometry","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/reflectometria#errores","x":"05 Common mistakes Forgetting to divide by two. The measured time is a round trip. Using the wrong velocity factor. A 20% error in the factor is a 20% error in the distance. Reading a distant reflection without considering losses. The cable’s attenuation shrinks the far-end steps. Confusing a capacitance with a short circuit. You have to look at the trace for a long time: the capacitance charges."},{"t":"Self-assessment","d":"Reflectometry","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/reflectometria#autoevaluacion","x":"06 Self-assessment A TDR sees a change 500 ns after the step on a cable with a factor of 0.66. How far away is it? \\( 0.66 \\cdot 3\\cdot10^8 \\cdot 500\\cdot10^{-9}/2 \\approx 49.5 \\) m. What level does the trace show with a 75 Ω load on a 50 Ω line? Γ = 0.2: 60% of the step. What time constant does the response of a 100 pF capacitor at the end of a 50 Ω line have? 5 ns. Which instrument performs the function of the TDR in optical fiber? The optical time-domain reflectometer, OTDR."},{"t":"Further reading","d":"Reflectometry","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/reflectometria#ampliar","x":"07 Further reading Eric Bogatin. Signal and Power Integrity — Simplified. 3rd ed., Prentice Hall, 2018. TDR as a tool for seeing the discontinuities of a board. Keysight Technologies. Time Domain Reflectometry Theory. Application Note 1304-2, Keysight. Reading TDR traces with resistive, capacitive and inductive loads. Howard Johnson and Martin Graham. High-Speed Digital Design: A Handbook of Black Magic. Prentice Hall, 1993. Reflections in fast digital signals and how to measure them."},{"t":"Any periodic signal is a sum of sinusoids","d":"Steady state and spectra","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/regimen-espectros#fourier","x":"01 Any periodic signal is a sum of sinusoids Everything learned about sinusoidal steady state would seem to be useful only for pure sinusoidal signals, which in practice hardly exist. Fourier solved the problem in 1807: every reasonable periodic signal is a sum of sinusoids whose frequencies are integer multiples of the fundamental. Since linear circuits obey superposition, it is enough to solve the circuit once for each of those sinusoids and add the results. \\[ x(t) = a_0 + \\sum_{n=1}^{\\infty} c_n \\cos(n\\omega_0 t + \\theta_n) \\qquad \\omega_0 = \\frac{2\\pi}{T} \\] \\( a_0 \\) is the average value; each term \\( n \\) is a harmonic, with amplitude \\( c_n \\) and phase \\( \\theta_n \\). The one with \\( n=1 \\) is the fundamental."},{"t":"The line spectrum","d":"Steady state and spectra","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/regimen-espectros#espectro","x":"02 The line spectrum Plotting the amplitudes \\( c_n \\) against frequency gives the spectrum of the signal: a series of lines at \\( f_0, 2f_0, 3f_0, \\ldots \\). Each waveform has its signature. Waveform, amplitude 1 Harmonics present Amplitude of harmonic \\( n \\) How they decay Symmetric square wave Odd only \\( 4/(\\pi n) \\) Like \\( 1/n \\): slowly Symmetric triangle wave Odd only \\( 8/(\\pi^2 n^2) \\) Like \\( 1/n^2 \\): quickly Sawtooth All \\( 2/(\\pi n) \\) Like \\( 1/n \\) The rule behind this is general: the more abrupt a signal is, the more slowly its harmonics decay. A sudden jump, like that of the square wave, needs very high frequencies to be built; a continuous ramp, like the triangle wave, is put together almost entirely from the first few terms."},{"t":"The response, harmonic by harmonic","d":"Steady state and spectra","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/regimen-espectros#respuesta","x":"03 The response, harmonic by harmonic If the input is a sum of sinusoids, the output of a linear circuit is the sum of the responses to each one. Each harmonic comes out multiplied by the magnitude of \\( H \\) at its own frequency and shifted by its phase. \\[ y(t) = H(0)\\,a_0 + \\sum_{n=1}^{\\infty} c_n' \\cos(n\\omega_0 t + \\theta_n') \\] \\[ c_n' = |H(jn\\omega_0)|\\,c_n \\qquad \\theta_n' = \\theta_n + \\angle H(jn\\omega_0) \\] A filter does not “deform” the signal in some mysterious way: it changes the proportion among its harmonics. What looks different on the oscilloscope is the sum of those new proportions. Lab · a wave through an RC filter, harmonic by harmonic On top, the spectrum: in gray the input harmonics and in color those that come out of the low-pass filter. Below, the output waveform reconstructed by summing the first 99 harmonics. square wave triangle wave sawtooth filter cutoff = 0.3 · f₀ filter cutoff = f₀ filter cutoff = 3 · f₀ filter cutoff = 10 · f₀ filter cutoff = 100 · f₀"},{"t":"RMS value and harmonic distortion","d":"Steady state and spectra","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/regimen-espectros#potencia","x":"04 RMS value and harmonic distortion Since harmonics of different frequencies do not exchange average power with one another, the square of the RMS value of a signal is the sum of the squares of the RMS values of its harmonics: the Parseval identity. \\[ X_{rms}^2 = a_0^2 + \\sum_{n=1}^{\\infty} \\frac{c_n^2}{2} \\] \\[ THD = \\frac{\\sqrt{\\sum_{n\\geq2} c_n^2}}{c_1} \\] The total harmonic distortion compares the energy of all the harmonics with that of the fundamental. A square wave has a THD of 48.3 %; a triangle wave, 12.1 %; a pure sinusoid, zero."},{"t":"From the line spectrum to the continuous spectrum","d":"Steady state and spectra","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/regimen-espectros#aperiodicas","x":"05 From the line spectrum to the continuous spectrum If the period grows without bound, the lines of the spectrum move closer to one another until they form a continuum: the Fourier series becomes the Fourier transform, which describes the frequency content of a signal that does not repeat, such as a single pulse. The power signals of the signals unit have a line spectrum; the energy signals, a continuous spectrum. It is the same idea with two tools, and the Signals and Systems course develops them in detail."},{"t":"Where it shows up in electronics","d":"Steady state and spectra","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/regimen-espectros#electronica","x":"06 Where it shows up in electronics Case What to look at in the spectrum Audio amplifier Its THD at rated power: below 0.1 % it is inaudible Mains with many switched-mode power supplies The third harmonic, at 150 Hz, which in three-phase systems adds up in the neutral and overheats it Electromagnetic compatibility The harmonics of a fast-edged digital clock reach hundreds of MHz and have to be kept under the emission limits Spectrum analyzer The instrument that directly displays the lines of a signal"},{"t":"In the lab","d":"Steady state and spectra","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/regimen-espectros#laboratorio","x":"07 In the lab Activity 1 · Seeing the harmonics Apply a 1 kHz square wave to an oscilloscope with an FFT function and measure the amplitude of harmonics 1, 3, 5 and 7. Verify that they follow the ratio \\( 1: 1/3: 1/5: 1/7 \\). Activity 2 · Building a square wave with a filter Using this page’s lab, observe what happens to the output waveform when the filter cutoff goes down from 100·f₀ to 0.3·f₀, and relate the result to the RC charging in the passive components topic. Activity 3 · Calculating a THD Calculate the THD of a square wave taking only the harmonics up to the 9th and compare it with the exact value of 48.3 %. Explain why the partial sum gives a lower value."},{"t":"Common mistakes","d":"Steady state and spectra","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/regimen-espectros#errores","x":"08 Common mistakes Applying harmonic superposition to a nonlinear circuit. A diode or a saturated transistor generates new harmonics; the recipe in this unit holds only for linear circuits. Forgetting that \\( H \\) changes from harmonic to harmonic. Each one is multiplied by \\( |H(jn\\omega_0)| \\), not by a single value. Adding harmonic amplitudes to obtain the RMS value. What gets added are the squares of the RMS values, not the amplitudes. Expecting even harmonics in a wave with half-wave symmetry. The symmetric square and triangle waves have only odd ones. Confusing a line spectrum with a continuous spectrum. The first belongs to periodic signals; the second, to signals that do not repeat."},{"t":"Self-assessment","d":"Steady state and spectra","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/regimen-espectros#autoevaluacion","x":"09 Self-assessment Which harmonics does a symmetric square wave have? Only the odd ones, with amplitude \\( 4/(\\pi n) \\). Why do the harmonics of the triangle wave decay faster than those of the square wave? Because the triangle wave has no jumps: it is continuous, and only its slope changes abruptly. The smoother the signal, the faster its spectrum decays. How is the output of a linear circuit calculated for a periodic wave? The input is decomposed into harmonics, each one is multiplied by \\( H(jn\\omega_0) \\) and the responses are added. What does the Parseval identity say? That the square of the RMS value is the sum of the squares of the RMS values of each harmonic component, plus that of the average value. What is the THD of a square wave? 48.3 %."},{"t":"Further reading","d":"Steady state and spectra","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/regimen-espectros#ampliar","x":"10 Further reading Charles K. Alexander and Matthew N. O. Sadiku. Fundamentals of Electric Circuits. 7th ed., McGraw-Hill, 2021. The chapter on Fourier series applies exactly this procedure to RC and RLC circuits. Alan V. Oppenheim, Alan S. Willsky and S. Hamid Nawab. Signals and Systems. 2nd ed., Prentice Hall, 1996. The formal bridge between the Fourier series and the transform, for whoever continues on to Signals and Systems. Keysight Technologies. Spectrum Analysis Basics (Application Note 150). Keysight. How the instrument that displays the line spectrum in the lab works on the inside."},{"t":"The complex frequency \\( s \\)","d":"Sinusoidal steady state and the s-plane","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/regimen-sinusoidal#frecuenciacompleja","x":"01 The complex frequency \\( s \\) The previous topic used \\( j\\omega \\) to turn derivatives into products. That \\( j\\omega \\) is a particular case of something more general: the complex frequency \\( s = \\sigma + j\\omega \\). A signal of the form \\( e^{st} \\) covers, in a single stroke, all the elementary signals of the signals unit: Value of \\( s \\) Signal \\( e^{st} \\) \\( s = 0 \\) A constant: pure DC \\( s = \\sigma \\) real and negative A decaying exponential: a transient \\( s = j\\omega \\), purely imaginary A sinusoid of constant amplitude: steady state \\( s = \\sigma + j\\omega \\) with \\( \\sigma A damped sinusoid: an oscillatory transient \\( \\sigma>0 \\) Something that grows without bound: an unstable circuit Sinusoidal steady state is, then, what happens along a single line of the plane: the imaginary axis. The rest of the \\( s \\)-plane describes transients, and is studied in the transient response unit."},{"t":"The transfer function, its poles and its zeros","d":"Sinusoidal steady state and the s-plane","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/regimen-sinusoidal#transferencia","x":"02 The transfer function, its poles and its zeros With impedances written in \\( s \\) —\\( Z_L = sL \\), \\( Z_C = 1/sC \\)— the relationship between the output and the input of a linear circuit is a ratio of polynomials in \\( s \\): the transfer function \\( H(s) \\). The roots of the numerator are the zeros; those of the denominator, the poles. They are enough to know the entire circuit, up to a constant. \\[ H(s) = \\frac{V_o(s)}{V_i(s)} = \\frac{1}{1 + sRC} \\qquad \\text{pole at } \\; p = -\\frac{1}{RC} \\] The RC low-pass filter: a single real pole, on the negative half-axis. Its frequency response is \\( H(j\\omega) \\): evaluating \\( H(s) \\) by walking along the imaginary axis. That evaluation has a geometric reading that is worth internalizing. The magnitude of \\( H(j\\omega) \\) is inversely proportional to the distance between the point \\( j\\omega \\) and the pole: when \\( \\omega \\) is small, the point is close to the origin and the distance to the pole hardly changes; when \\( \\omega \\) exceeds \\( |p| \\), the distance grows with \\( \\omega \\) and the gain falls. The phase angle is, with its sign changed, the angle of that same vector. \\[ |H(j\\omega)| = \\frac{|p|}{|j\\omega - p|} \\qquad \\angle H(j\\omega) = -\\arg(j\\omega - p) \\] At \\( \\omega = |p| \\) the vector makes 45° with the real axis: the gain drops to \\( 1/\\sqrt2 \\) (−3 dB) and the phase is −45°. That is the cutoff frequency, \\( f_c = 1/(2\\pi RC) \\). Lab · from the s-plane to the Bode plot On the left, the \\( s \\)-plane with the RC f"},{"t":"Decibels and Bode plots","d":"Sinusoidal steady state and the s-plane","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/regimen-sinusoidal#bode","x":"03 Decibels and Bode plots The gain of a circuit varies over many orders of magnitude, and so does the frequency. That is why the frequency response is plotted with both axes logarithmic: the frequency in decades and the gain in decibels, \\( 20\\log_{10}|H| \\). On that scale, each pole contributes a straight line with a slope of −20 dB per decade starting at its cutoff frequency, and each zero, +20 dB per decade. \\( f/f_c \\) \\( |H| \\) Gain Phase 0.1 0.995 −0.04 dB −5.7° 1 0.707 −3.01 dB −45° 10 0.0995 −20.04 dB −84.3° 100 0.0100 −40.00 dB −89.4° One decade above the cutoff, the filter already attenuates ten times; two decades above, a hundred. That rule of −20 dB per decade per pole is what makes it possible to sketch by hand, in a minute, the response of a circuit with several poles: you add up straight lines."},{"t":"Power in sinusoidal steady state","d":"Sinusoidal steady state and the s-plane","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/regimen-sinusoidal#potencia","x":"04 Power in sinusoidal steady state With sinusoidal voltage and current out of phase by an angle \\( \\varphi \\), the instantaneous power oscillates, and only part of it is turned into useful work. Three powers organize the matter: \\[ \\mathbf{S} = \\mathbf{V}\\,\\mathbf{I}^* = P + jQ \\qquad P = V_{rms}I_{rms}\\cos\\varphi \\qquad Q = V_{rms}I_{rms}\\sin\\varphi \\qquad pf = \\cos\\varphi = \\frac{P}{|S|} \\] \\( P \\) is the active (real) power, in watts: the one that heats, moves and lights. \\( Q \\) is the reactive power, in volt-amperes reactive: the one that goes back and forth between the source and the fields of inductors and capacitors without doing net work. \\( |S| \\), the apparent power, in volt-amperes, is the one that sizes cables and transformers. A refrigerator motor At 230 V it draws 10 A with a power factor of 0.8 lagging. The apparent power is 2300 VA; the active power, 1840 W; the reactive power, 1380 var. The utility has to carry 10 A even though only 8 A “work”: that is why it charges surcharges to industrial users with a low power factor, and why power-factor-correction capacitors exist, which locally supply the reactive power the motor asks for."},{"t":"Where it shows up in electronics","d":"Sinusoidal steady state and the s-plane","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/regimen-sinusoidal#electronica","x":"05 Where it shows up in electronics Case What is read from the s-plane or from the Bode plot Anti-aliasing filter before an A/D converter How many poles are needed to attenuate enough above half the sampling frequency Stability of a feedback amplifier If any closed-loop pole crosses into the right half-plane, the amplifier oscillates Op amp datasheet The gain-bandwidth product is, literally, the location of its dominant pole Switched-mode power supply with power factor correction The PFC stage makes the mains current follow the voltage, with a \\( pf \\) close to 1"},{"t":"In the lab","d":"Sinusoidal steady state and the s-plane","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/regimen-sinusoidal#laboratorio","x":"06 In the lab Activity 1 · Measuring a Bode plot with an oscilloscope Build an RC with 1.6 kΩ and 100 nF, drive it with a function generator and measure the output amplitude and phase shift at 100 Hz, 1 kHz and 10 kHz. Compare with the table in section 3. Activity 2 · Reading the pole on the plane Using this page’s lab, verify that at \\( f = f_c \\) the vector joining the pole to \\( j\\omega \\) makes 45° with the real axis, and that at \\( f = 10f_c \\) it makes almost 84°. Activity 3 · Correcting a power factor Calculate the parallel capacitor that takes the motor of the example from a pf of 0.8 to a pf of 0.95, at 230 V and 50 Hz."},{"t":"Common mistakes","d":"Sinusoidal steady state and the s-plane","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/regimen-sinusoidal#errores","x":"07 Common mistakes Confusing \\( s \\) with \\( j\\omega \\). Sinusoidal steady state is \\( s=j\\omega \\); the rest of the plane describes transients. Using \\( 10\\log \\) for a voltage ratio. For voltages and currents it is \\( 20\\log \\); \\( 10\\log \\) is for powers. Forgetting the sign of a pole’s phase. A pole lags: it contributes negative phase, down to −90°. Adding apparent powers as if they were active. \\( S \\) is a complex number; its magnitudes do not add unless all the loads have the same power factor. Believing that reactive power is “lost.” It is not dissipated; what it costs is the extra current it makes circulate through the conductors."},{"t":"Self-assessment","d":"Sinusoidal steady state and the s-plane","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/regimen-sinusoidal#autoevaluacion","x":"08 Self-assessment On which part of the \\( s \\)-plane is the frequency response evaluated? On the imaginary axis, \\( s = j\\omega \\). Where is the pole of an RC low-pass filter? At \\( s = -1/RC \\), on the negative real half-axis. What is the gain at the cutoff frequency? \\( 1/\\sqrt2 \\approx 0.707 \\), that is, −3.01 dB, with −45° of phase. What slope does each pole contribute on a Bode plot? −20 dB per decade above its cutoff frequency. What does the power factor measure? The fraction of the apparent power that is active: \\( \\cos\\varphi = P/|S| \\). What does a pole in the right half-plane indicate? An unstable circuit: its response grows without bound."},{"t":"Further reading","d":"Sinusoidal steady state and the s-plane","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/regimen-sinusoidal#ampliar","x":"09 Further reading Charles K. Alexander and Matthew N. O. Sadiku. Fundamentals of Electric Circuits. 7th ed., McGraw-Hill, 2021. The chapters on AC power and frequency response, with hand-drawn Bode plots. Mac E. Van Valkenburg. Network Analysis. 3rd ed., Prentice Hall, 1974 (Spanish edition, “Análisis de redes”, Limusa). The classic text that builds the whole theory of networks around the s-plane, with the geometric interpretation of poles and zeros. Adel S. Sedra and Kenneth C. Smith. Microelectronic Circuits. 8th ed., Oxford University Press, 2020. The appendix on transfer functions and Bode plots, oriented toward what amplifier stability later demands."},{"t":"The line of best fit","d":"Regression analysis","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/regresion#minimos","x":"01 The line of best fit Measuring a sensor’s output at several known temperatures gives a cloud of points, not a line. Regression looks for the line that best represents them, and “best” has a precise definition: the one that minimizes the sum of the squares of the vertical distances. \\[ \\hat{y} = b_0 + b_1 x \\qquad b_1 = \\frac{\\sum (x_i-\\bar{x})(y_i-\\bar{y})}{\\sum (x_i-\\bar{x})^2} \\qquad b_0 = \\bar{y} - b_1\\bar{x} \\] The vertical distances are minimized because the error is assumed to be in \\( y \\) and \\( x \\) to be well known. That asymmetry matters: the line of \\( y \\) on \\( x \\) is not the same as that of \\( x \\) on \\( y \\). In electronics this is calibration: you apply known values, record what the instrument reads and fit the line that converts one into the other. The intercept is the offset and the slope is the gain, which are exactly the two parameters that a datasheet specifies."},{"t":"How well it fits","d":"Regression analysis","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/regresion#bondad","x":"02 How well it fits \\[ R^2 = 1 - \\frac{\\sum (y_i-\\hat{y}_i)^2}{\\sum (y_i-\\bar{y})^2} \\qquad\\qquad r = \\frac{S_{xy}}{\\sqrt{S_{xx}S_{yy}}} \\] The coefficient of determination \\( R^2 \\) is the fraction of the variability of \\( y \\) that the line explains. The correlation coefficient \\( r \\) is its signed root, and measures the linear association between the two variables: it equals 1 or −1 with perfectly aligned points and 0 with no linear relationship. A high \\( R^2 \\) guarantees nothing It is the most widespread mistake in all of applied statistics. An \\( R^2 \\) of 0.95 can come from a relationship that is not linear, from a single point that drags the line, or from a cloud with no structure plus one distant value. The Anscombe quartet, which is in the lab below, consists of four data sets with the same mean, the same standard deviation, the same line and the same \\( R^2 \\), and with shapes that look nothing alike. It is the same argument as the histogram in the first unit: you have to look at the data. Lab · regression and residuals Go through the four Anscombe sets and compare their statistics: they are identical. The residual plot below is what gives them away, and it is the first thing to look at in any fit. Anscombe 1 · reasonable scatter Anscombe 2 · the relationship is curved Anscombe 3 · perfect line with an outlier Anscombe 4 · a single point decides calibration of a real sensor Show the residual plot"},{"t":"Residuals are the diagnostic","d":"Regression analysis","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/regresion#residuos","x":"03 Residuals are the diagnostic The residual of each point is what the line did not explain: \\( e_i = y_i - \\hat{y}_i \\). Plotting them against \\( x \\) is the most useful diagnostic tool there is, because if the linear model is correct no structure should remain in them: just even noise around zero. The residuals show It means What to do A shapeless cloud around zero The linear model works well Nothing A curve The relationship is not linear Add a quadratic term or transform A funnel that opens up The spread grows with \\( x \\) Weight the fit, or work with the logarithm A very distant point Outlier or influential point Investigate it before deciding Correlation is not causation, and extrapolating is making things up Two different things, and both are common. Two variables being correlated does not mean one causes the other: there may be a third behind them, or pure coincidence. And a line fitted between 0 and 100 °C says nothing about what happens at 300 °C: outside the measured range, the model has no backing from any data. Extrapolating a calibration is one of the most common ways of introducing a large error with a lot of confidence."},{"t":"In the lab","d":"Regression analysis","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/regresion#laboratorio","x":"04 In the lab Activity 1 · Calibrating a sensor Measure the output of a signal-conditioned thermistor at eight known temperatures. Fit the line, obtain the gain and offset, and calculate \\( R^2 \\). Plot the residuals and decide whether the linear model is sufficient. Activity 2 · The influential point Repeat the fit from the previous activity adding a point measured with a gross error. Compare slope, intercept and \\( R^2 \\) with and without it, and evaluate how far a single data point moved it. Activity 3 · Linearizing Take the nonlinear curve of a thermistor and plot \\( \\ln R \\) against \\( 1/T \\). Fit the line on the transformed variables, obtain the constant \\( B \\) and verify that the residuals now have no structure."},{"t":"Common mistakes","d":"Regression analysis","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/regresion#errores","x":"05 Common mistakes Trusting a high \\( R^2 \\) without looking at the data. The Anscombe quartet exists for that. Not plotting the residuals. It is the cheapest and most informative diagnostic. Extrapolating outside the measured range. There the model has no data to support it. Reading causation into a correlation. There may be a third variable behind it. Forgetting that the line of \\( y \\) on \\( x \\) is not that of \\( x \\) on \\( y \\). Vertical distances are minimized. Adding terms until \\( R^2 \\) goes up. With enough parameters you can fit anything, including the noise."},{"t":"Self-assessment","d":"Regression analysis","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/regresion#autoevaluacion","x":"06 Self-assessment What does the least-squares method minimize? The sum of the squares of the vertical distances between the points and the line. What does \\( R^2 \\) represent? The fraction of the variability of \\( y \\) that the model explains. What is the Anscombe quartet? Four data sets with the same line, the same \\( R^2 \\) and the same mean, and completely different shapes. The proof that you have to plot the data. What should a correct residual plot show? Nothing: even noise around zero, with no structure. What are the gain and the offset of a calibration? The slope and the intercept of the fitted line. Why should you not extrapolate? Because outside the measured range no data support the model, and the error can be large without anything warning you."},{"t":"Further reading","d":"Regression analysis","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/regresion#ampliar","x":"07 Further reading Douglas C. Montgomery and George C. Runger. Applied Statistics and Probability for Engineers. 7th ed. (Spanish edition, “Probabilidad y estadística aplicadas a la ingeniería”), Wiley, 2018. Chapters 11 and 12: simple and multiple linear regression, with inference on the coefficients. Francis J. Anscombe. Graphs in Statistical Analysis. The American Statistician, vol. 27, 1973. Four pages in which the quartet first appeared. It is still the best argument in favor of plotting. John Fox. Applied Regression Analysis and Generalized Linear Models. 3rd ed., SAGE, 2015. Residual diagnostics, influential points and what to do when the linear model is not enough."},{"t":"Two postulates and everything else","d":"Special relativity","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/relatividad#postulados","x":"01 Two postulates and everything else Einstein did not add complications: he removed an assumption. Faced with the null result of Michelson-Morley, instead of inventing mechanisms to save the ether, he declared it unnecessary and postulated two things. Postulate It says What it forces us to abandon Relativity The laws of physics are the same in every inertial frame The idea of a privileged frame Constancy of \\( c \\) Light travels at \\( c \\) for all observers Absolute time and the simple addition of velocities The second one is the hard one. If an observer chases a pulse of light at 0.9 c, he should see it moving away at 0.1 c; what he measures is \\( c \\). For that to be possible, what has to give way is the measurement of time and space. Everything else follows from there, with no new physics. \\[ \\gamma = \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1 - v^2/c^2}} \\qquad \\Delta t = \\gamma\\,\\Delta t_0 \\qquad L = \\frac{L_0}{\\gamma} \\qquad E = \\gamma\\,m_0c^2 \\] The Lorentz factor \\( \\gamma \\) is the only parameter of all relativistic kinematics. It equals 1 at low speed —which is why nobody notices anything in daily life—, 1.15 at 50 % of \\( c \\) and tends to infinity as it approaches \\( c \\). Lab · the Lorentz factor You move the speed and all the effects appear at once. The cases in the selector are real situations, and some are much closer to home than you would expect. car on the highway · 120 km/h space station · 7.66 km/s GPS satellite · 3.87 km/s electron in a TV tube · 25 kV atmospheric muon · 0.998 c"},{"t":"Where it shows up in a device","d":"Special relativity","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/relatividad#electronica","x":"02 Where it shows up in a device Relativity is usually presented as an astrophysics topic, and it is inside several everyday devices. Case The effect Consequence Cathode-ray tube Electrons at 30 % of \\( c \\) The deflection has to be calculated with \\( \\gamma \\) GPS The satellite clocks run at a different rate 11 km of error per day if not corrected Atmospheric muons They reach the ground when they should not The most accessible experimental proof Magnetism It is electrostatics seen from another frame They are not two phenomena: it is one Magnetism is relativity This is the nicest result in the whole unit. Take a current-carrying wire, neutral: the positive charges of the lattice are at rest and the electrons drift. An external charge also at rest feels nothing. But if that charge moves parallel to the wire, from its frame the two charge distributions contract differently —because they have different velocities relative to it— and the wire stops being neutral. A net electric force appears. That is exactly what in the laboratory we call magnetic force. Magnetism is not a separate phenomenon: it is what electrostatics looks like when there is relative motion, and \\( c \\) appears in Maxwell's equations precisely for that reason."},{"t":"Mass and energy","d":"Special relativity","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/relatividad#energia","x":"03 Mass and energy \\[ E = \\gamma m_0 c^2 \\qquad E_c = (\\gamma - 1)m_0c^2 \\qquad E^2 = (pc)^2 + (m_0c^2)^2 \\] The rest energy of the electron is \\( m_0c^2 = 511 \\) keV. That is why an electron accelerated through 25 kV carries 4.9 % of its rest energy as kinetic energy, and there \\( \\gamma \\) is already 1.049. The classical kinetic energy \\( \\tfrac12 mv^2 \\) is the first term in the expansion of the relativistic one, and that is why it works at low speed. As \\( c \\) is approached the difference becomes enormous: the classical expression keeps growing like a polynomial and the relativistic one shoots up, which is another way of saying that you cannot reach \\( c \\) with finite energy. The last relation, \\( E^2 = (pc)^2 + (m_0c^2)^2 \\), includes the photon: with \\( m_0 = 0 \\) it becomes \\( E = pc \\), and from there comes the fact that light has momentum without having mass. That is the radiation pressure of the waves unit, and also the link that the next unit will use for the photoelectric effect. Relativistic mass is a concept in retreat Many books speak of a “mass that increases with speed”, \\( m = \\gamma m_0 \\). It is a way of writing things that works for rectilinear motion and confuses in everything else, and today it is preferred to say that mass is invariant and that what changes with speed are energy and momentum. If a text uses relativistic mass it is not wrong, but you should know that it is an old convention."},{"t":"In the lab","d":"Special relativity","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/relatividad#laboratorio","x":"04 In the lab Activity 1 · Relativistic correction in a CRT Calculate the speed of the electrons in a tube accelerated through 5, 15 and 25 kV, first with the classical expression and then with the relativistic one. Tabulate the percentage error of the classical one in each case. Activity 2 · The GPS error budget Calculate the daily delay due to orbital speed and the daily advance due to gravity for a GPS satellite. Obtain the net value and translate it into position error, knowing that the signal travels at \\( c \\). Activity 3 · Muons that should not arrive With a mean proper lifetime of 2.2 µs and a speed of 0.998 c, calculate how far a muon would travel without time dilation and how far it travels with it. Compare with the 10 km it actually crosses."},{"t":"Common mistakes","d":"Special relativity","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/relatividad#errores","x":"05 Common mistakes Adding velocities as if nothing were different. It holds at low speed; near \\( c \\) you have to use the relativistic composition. Believing the effect is “apparent”. The measured time really is different, and muons prove it. Forgetting which one is the proper time. \\( \\Delta t_0 \\) is the one measured by the clock that travels with the event. Using \\( \\tfrac12 mv^2 \\) with accelerated electrons. Above a few kV the error is already noticeable. Thinking relativity only matters in astronomy. The GPS in your phone corrects for it every day. Confusing mass with rest energy. \\( m_0c^2 \\) is energy; mass is invariant."},{"t":"Self-assessment","d":"Special relativity","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/relatividad#autoevaluacion","x":"06 Self-assessment What are the two postulates? That the laws of physics are the same in every inertial frame, and that the speed of light is the same for all observers. What is \\( \\gamma \\) at 50 % of \\( c \\)? 1.155. It only reaches 2 near 87 % of \\( c \\). What error would GPS accumulate without relativistic correction? About 38 microseconds per day, which at the speed of light is more than eleven kilometers. What is the rest energy of the electron? 511 keV. That is why accelerating it through 25 kV already puts it 4.9 % above that energy. Which relation holds for the photon? \\( E = pc \\), which follows from \\( E^2 = (pc)^2 + (m_0c^2)^2 \\) with zero mass. What is the relationship between magnetism and relativity? The magnetic force is the electrostatic force seen from a moving frame: the charge densities contract differently and the wire no longer looks neutral."},{"t":"Further reading","d":"Special relativity","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/relatividad#ampliar","x":"07 Further reading Robert Eisberg and Robert Resnick. Quantum Physics of Atoms, Molecules, Solids, Nuclei, and Particles. 2nd ed., John Wiley & Sons, 1985 (Spanish edition, “Física cuántica”, Limusa). The relativity appendix, oriented toward what quantum physics needs afterward. Edward M. Purcell and David J. Morin. Electricity and Magnetism. 3rd ed., Cambridge University Press, 2016 (Spanish edition, “Electricidad y magnetismo”). Chapter 5 derives the magnetic field from electrostatics plus relativity. It is the best treatment of that idea there is. Neil Ashby. Relativity in the Global Positioning System. Living Reviews in Relativity, vol. 6, 2003. All the relativistic corrections that a GPS receiver applies, in detail and with the numbers."},{"t":"The same system, four descriptions","d":"Representation of signals and systems","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/representacion-sistemas#cuatro","x":"01 The same system, four descriptions An LTI system can be described in four different ways, and all four say exactly the same thing. They are not alternatives you have to choose between: they are points of view, and each problem is easier to solve from one of them. Representation Form Good for Differential equation \\( \\sum a_k \\dfrac{d^k y}{dt^k} = \\sum b_k \\dfrac{d^k x}{dt^k} \\) Starting from the circuit: it is what Kirchhoff’s laws and the component laws give Impulse response \\( h(t) \\), with \\( y = x * h \\) Seeing the time response; it is measured by striking the system Transfer function \\( H(s) = \\dfrac{Y(s)}{X(s)} \\) Cascades, stability, filter design State variables \\( \\dot{\\mathbf{q}} = A\\mathbf{q} + B x,\\quad y = C\\mathbf{q} + D x \\) Systems with several inputs and outputs; numerical simulation The most frequent path in practice goes from the first to the third. You set up the circuit, obtain the differential equation, transform it —so that derivatives become powers of \\( s \\)— and are left with a ratio of polynomials that is analyzed with poles and zeros. \\[ \\frac{d}{dt} \\;\\longrightarrow\\; s \\qquad\\qquad \\frac{d^k}{dt^k} \\;\\longrightarrow\\; s^k \\] With zero initial conditions. That is all the magic: a differential equation becomes algebraic."},{"t":"All four faces at once","d":"Representation of signals and systems","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/representacion-sistemas#lab","x":"02 All four faces at once So that the equivalence does not stay in a table, it helps to see the four move together. The lab takes a second-order system —the most common in electronics— and shows what happens to each representation when a parameter is changed. Lab · one thing, four ways of saying it Move the parameters and all four descriptions change at the same time: the equation, the poles, the impulse response and the frequency response. second-order low-pass band-pass high-pass first-order low-pass ω₀ = 1.00 rad/s Q = 0.71"},{"t":"The discrete version","d":"Representation of signals and systems","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/representacion-sistemas#discreto","x":"03 The discrete version Everything repeats with square brackets. The differential equation becomes a difference equation, which is also directly programmable: \\[ y[n] = \\sum_{k=0}^{M} b_k\\,x[n-k] \\;-\\; \\sum_{k=1}^{N} a_k\\,y[n-k] \\] Each term is a stored sample, multiplied by a coefficient. That is, literally, the filter’s program. And the transfer function is obtained with the same trick, except that now the delay of one sample becomes \\( z^{-1} \\): \\[ x[n-k] \\;\\longrightarrow\\; z^{-k}X(z) \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad H(z) = \\frac{\\sum b_k z^{-k}}{1 + \\sum a_k z^{-k}} \\] How to go from one to another, in practice From the equation to \\( H \\): transform and solve. From \\( H \\) to the impulse response: inverse transform, which with residues means adding one term per pole. From \\( h \\) to \\( H \\): transform. And from \\( H \\) to the state variables: choose a set of variables —typically the capacitor voltages and the inductor currents— and write their derivatives. That last path is the one any circuit simulator uses internally."},{"t":"Signals also have several representations","d":"Representation of signals and systems","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/representacion-sistemas#senales","x":"04 Signals also have several representations Representation When it is useful \\( x(t) \\), the waveform Seeing amplitudes, times, edges Fourier series Periodic signals: which harmonics they have Fourier transform Isolated signals: how much bandwidth they occupy Laplace transform Transients and initial conditions Samples \\( x[n] \\) Anything that is going to pass through a processor The fact that there are so many is not excessive: each one makes visible something that the others hide. The waveform says nothing about the bandwidth, and the spectrum does not show whether the signal clips. A good part of the craft consists of choosing where to look from."},{"t":"In the lab","d":"Representation of signals and systems","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/representacion-sistemas#laboratorio","x":"05 In the lab Activity 1 · From the circuit to the four forms For a series RLC with the output across the capacitor, set up the differential equation, obtain \\( H(s) \\), inverse transform to get \\( h(t) \\) and write the state equations taking \\( v_C \\) and \\( i_L \\) as variables. Verify that all four describe the same circuit. Activity 2 · Measure h(t) and deduce H Measure the step response of the RLC, differentiate it to obtain \\( h(t) \\), extract \\( \\sigma \\) and \\( \\omega \\) from the envelope and the period, and reconstruct \\( H(s) \\). Compare with the one calculated from the component values. Activity 3 · Programming the difference equation Discretize the same filter and program its difference equation on a microcontroller. Compare its step response with that of the analog circuit."},{"t":"Common mistakes","d":"Representation of signals and systems","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/representacion-sistemas#errores","x":"06 Common mistakes Forgetting the zero initial conditions when replacing \\( d/dt \\) by \\( s \\). With nonzero initial conditions, extra terms appear. Confusing \\( H(s) \\) with \\( H(j\\omega) \\). The latter is the former evaluated on the imaginary axis. Believing that one representation is more “true”. All four describe the same system. Mixing up \\( z^{-1} \\) and \\( s \\). One is a delay of one sample; the other, a derivative. Choosing the state variables badly. It is best to take those that store energy: capacitor voltages and inductor currents."},{"t":"Self-assessment","d":"Representation of signals and systems","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/representacion-sistemas#autoevaluacion","x":"07 Self-assessment What are the four representations of an LTI system? Differential equation, impulse response, transfer function and state variables. What replaces \\( d/dt \\) when transforming? The variable \\( s \\), assuming zero initial conditions. What does \\( z^{-1} \\) represent? A delay of one sample. It is the memory element of any digital filter. How do you go from \\( H(s) \\) to \\( h(t) \\)? By inverse transforming, which with residues means adding one exponential term per pole. What should be taken as state variables in a circuit? Those that store energy: capacitor voltages and inductor currents. Why are there so many representations of a signal? Because each one makes visible something that the others hide: the waveform does not show the bandwidth, and the spectrum does not show whether it clips."},{"t":"Further reading","d":"Representation of signals and systems","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/representacion-sistemas#ampliar","x":"08 Further reading Alan V. Oppenheim and Alan S. Willsky. Signals and Systems. 2nd ed. (Spanish edition, “Señales y sistemas”), Prentice Hall, 1997. The representations and the passage from one to another, with examples in both domains. Katsuhiko Ogata. Modern Control Engineering. 5th ed. (Spanish edition, “Ingeniería de control moderna”), Prentice Hall, 2010. State variables in full detail, and their relationship with the transfer function. Charles K. Alexander and Matthew N. O. Sadiku. Fundamentals of Electric Circuits. 7th ed. (Spanish edition, “Fundamentos de circuitos eléctricos”), McGraw-Hill, 2021. The path from the circuit to the differential equation, which is where everything starts."},{"t":"A real integral, solved from the outside","d":"Residues applied to real integrals","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/residuos-aplicaciones#idea","x":"01 A real integral, solved from the outside Some real integrals have no elementary antiderivative and yet come out in two lines if you take them off the real axis and close them in the complex plane. The maneuver is always the same: The real variable is replaced by a complex one and an arc is added to close the path. The residue theorem is applied to the closed contour. It is shown —with the ML bound— that the arc contributes zero as its radius grows. What remains is the integral being sought. \\[ \\underbrace{\\int_{-R}^{R} f(x)\\,dx}_{\\text{what is sought}} \\;+\\; \\underbrace{\\int_{\\Gamma_R} f(z)\\,dz}_{\\to\\,0} \\;=\\; 2\\pi j \\sum_{\\text{poles above}} \\operatorname{Res} f \\] The third step is the one that needs care, and it is the one the lab lets you see: if the integrand does not decay fast enough, the arc does not vanish and the whole method collapses."},{"t":"First type: integrals of rational functions","d":"Residues applied to real integrals","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/residuos-aplicaciones#racionales","x":"02 First type: integrals of rational functions For \\( \\int_{-\\infty}^{\\infty} \\dfrac{P(x)}{Q(x)}\\,dx \\) with \\( Q \\) having no real roots, the condition is that the denominator wins by at least two degrees: \\[ \\operatorname{deg}(Q) \\ge \\operatorname{deg}(P) + 2 \\;\\Longrightarrow\\; \\left|\\int_{\\Gamma_R} f\\,dz\\right| \\le \\frac{C}{R^2}\\cdot \\pi R \\xrightarrow[R \\to \\infty]{} 0 \\] The lead example is the simplest possible one. With \\( f(z) = 1/(1+z^2) \\), the only pole in the upper half-plane is at \\( z = j \\), with residue \\( 1/(2j) \\): \\[ \\int_{-\\infty}^{\\infty} \\frac{dx}{1 + x^2} = 2\\pi j \\cdot \\frac{1}{2j} = \\pi \\] Which is the same value the antiderivative, \\( \\arctan x \\), gives when evaluated between \\( -\\infty \\) and \\( \\infty \\). The appeal shows up when the antiderivative does not exist. With \\( 1/(1+x^4) \\) no arctangent will do, but the poles in the upper half-plane are \\( e^{j\\pi/4} \\) and \\( e^{j3\\pi/4} \\), and the computation is just as short: the result is \\( \\pi/\\sqrt{2} \\)."},{"t":"The arc that has to fade out","d":"Residues applied to real integrals","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/residuos-aplicaciones#lab","x":"03 The arc that has to fade out Lab · the contour being closed On the left, the contour: the segment of the real axis plus the semicircle of radius \\( R \\). On the right, how each contribution evolves as \\( R \\) grows. If the arc fades out, the segment converges to the value of the integral. f(x) = 1/(1 + x²) · equals π f(x) = 1/(1 + x⁴) · equals π/√2 f(x) = 1/(x² + 2x + 2) · equals π f(x) = cos(2x)/(1 + x²) · equals πe⁻² f(x) = x/(1 + x²) · the arc does not fade out Radius R = 4.0"},{"t":"Second type: Fourier integrals","d":"Residues applied to real integrals","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/residuos-aplicaciones#fourier","x":"04 Second type: Fourier integrals These are the ones that appear when transforming. They have the form \\( \\int R(x)\\,e^{j a x}\\,dx \\) and the exponential factor changes the analysis of the arc, because \\( |e^{jaz}| = e^{-a y} \\) decays for \\( y \\gt 0 \\) when \\( a \\gt 0 \\). The result that formalizes this is Jordan’s lemma: \\[ \\lim_{R\\to\\infty}\\int_{\\Gamma_R} R(z)\\,e^{jaz}\\,dz = 0 \\quad\\text{if } a \\gt 0 \\text{ and } |R(z)| \\to 0 \\text{ on the arc} \\] With \\( a \\gt 0 \\) you close above; with \\( a \\lt 0 \\), below. It is enough for the rational function to tend to zero; two degrees are not required. With that, the transform of a Lorentzian pulse comes out in one line: \\[ \\int_{-\\infty}^{\\infty} \\frac{e^{jax}}{1 + x^2}\\,dx = 2\\pi j \\cdot \\frac{e^{ja\\cdot j}}{2j} = \\pi\\,e^{-a}, \\qquad a \\gt 0 \\] And taking the real part, \\( \\int \\cos(ax)/(1+x^2)\\,dx = \\pi e^{-a} \\), which is the one in the lab. This family is the one that justifies the title of this topic in the curriculum: they are the Fourier integrals, and the residue method is the standard way of solving them."},{"t":"Third type: integrals of sines and cosines","d":"Residues applied to real integrals","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/residuos-aplicaciones#trigonometricas","x":"05 Third type: integrals of sines and cosines For \\( \\int_0^{2\\pi} R(\\cos\\theta, \\operatorname{sin}\\theta)\\,d\\theta \\) the trick is different and more elegant: there is no need to invent an arc, because the path is already closed. Setting \\( z = e^{j\\theta} \\), the interval \\( [0, 2\\pi] \\) traces exactly the unit circle: \\[ \\cos\\theta = \\frac{z + z^{-1}}{2}, \\qquad \\operatorname{sin}\\theta = \\frac{z - z^{-1}}{2j}, \\qquad d\\theta = \\frac{dz}{jz} \\] Applied to a specific case, \\( \\int_0^{2\\pi} \\dfrac{d\\theta}{2 + \\cos\\theta} \\), it becomes a contour integral with two poles at \\( z = -2 \\pm \\sqrt{3} \\), of which only one falls inside the circle, and the result is \\( 2\\pi/\\sqrt{3} \\). No trigonometric substitutions or tables. When there is a pole on the path \\( \\int_{-\\infty}^{\\infty} \\dfrac{\\operatorname{sin} x}{x}\\,dx \\) has a problem at \\( x = 0 \\). The way out is to go around it with a tiny semicircle and take the principal value. A simple pole surrounded by half an arc contributes \\( j\\pi \\) times its residue —half of \\( 2\\pi j \\)— and that is where the value \\( \\pi \\) comes from. That is the result behind the overshoot in the step response of an ideal filter: the Gibbs phenomenon."},{"t":"Where it is used in electronics","d":"Residues applied to real integrals","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/residuos-aplicaciones#electronica","x":"06 Where it is used in electronics Problem Integral What it gives Inverse transform \\( \\frac{1}{2\\pi j}\\int F(s)e^{st}ds \\) The time response, pole by pole Noise bandwidth \\( \\int_0^{\\infty} |H(j\\omega)|^2 d\\omega \\) The total noise at the output of a filter Signal energy \\( \\int |X(j\\omega)|^2 d\\omega \\) Parseval, from the frequency side Kramers-Kronig Principal value over the axis The real part of a response from the imaginary part The second row is the most everyday one. The equivalent noise bandwidth of a first-order low-pass filter comes from integrating \\( 1/(1 + (\\omega/\\omega_c)^2) \\) from zero to infinity, which is half of the integral of the first example on this page. The result, \\( B_n = \\frac{\\pi}{2}f_c \\), says that a real filter lets through 57 % more noise than an ideal one of the same width, and it is the number used to compute the noise of an amplifier."},{"t":"In the lab","d":"Residues applied to real integrals","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/residuos-aplicaciones#laboratorio","x":"07 In the lab Exercise 1 · The four roots Solve \\( \\int_{-\\infty}^{\\infty} \\dfrac{dx}{1+x^4} \\) by locating the four poles, keeping the two in the upper half-plane and adding their residues. Check that it gives \\( \\pi/\\sqrt{2} \\approx 2.221 \\) and compare it with a numerical integration. Exercise 2 · The noise bandwidth, measured Build an RC low-pass filter with \\( f_c = 1 \\) kHz, inject white noise and measure the RMS voltage at the output. Compute the equivalent bandwidth from the measurement and compare it with \\( \\frac{\\pi}{2}f_c = 1571 \\) Hz. Exercise 3 · The trigonometric integral Solve \\( \\int_0^{2\\pi} \\dfrac{d\\theta}{a + \\cos\\theta} \\) for \\( a \\gt 1 \\) with the substitution \\( z = e^{j\\theta} \\). Check that the result, \\( 2\\pi/\\sqrt{a^2-1} \\), tends to infinity when \\( a \\to 1 \\), and explain what happens to the pole."},{"t":"Common mistakes","d":"Residues applied to real integrals","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/residuos-aplicaciones#errores","x":"08 Common mistakes Closing the contour without checking the arc. It is the step that makes everything else valid; the lab shows a case where it fails. Adding up the residues of all four poles. When you close above, only those in the upper half-plane count. Closing above with \\( a \\lt 0 \\). In Fourier integrals the sign of the exponent decides the half-plane. Ignoring poles on the real axis. The contour has to be indented and the principal value kept. Forgetting the \\( 1/(jz) \\) when substituting \\( d\\theta \\) in trigonometric integrals."},{"t":"Self-assessment","d":"Residues applied to real integrals","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/residuos-aplicaciones#autoevaluacion","x":"09 Self-assessment What condition on the degrees does the first type require? That the denominator exceed the numerator by at least two degrees, so that the arc contributes zero. What is \\( \\int_{-\\infty}^{\\infty} dx/(1+x^2) \\) and why? \\( \\pi \\). The only pole above is \\( j \\), with residue \\( 1/(2j) \\), and \\( 2\\pi j \\cdot 1/(2j) = \\pi \\). What does Jordan’s lemma add? It allows the contour to be closed for integrands that decay more slowly, as long as there is a factor \\( e^{jaz} \\) with the right sign. How is \\( \\int_0^{2\\pi} \\) turned into a contour integral? With \\( z = e^{j\\theta} \\): the interval traces the unit circle, the sines and cosines are written in terms of \\( z \\) and \\( d\\theta = dz/(jz) \\). What does a simple pole surrounded by half an arc contribute? \\( j\\pi \\) times its residue: half of what a full contour would contribute. What is the noise bandwidth of a first-order RC? \\( B_n = \\frac{\\pi}{2}f_c \\approx 1.57\\,f_c \\). It is the integral of \\( |H|^2 \\), which comes out by residues."},{"t":"Further reading","d":"Residues applied to real integrals","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/residuos-aplicaciones#ampliar","x":"10 Further reading Ruel V. Churchill and James W. Brown. Complex Variables and Applications. 7th ed. (Spanish edition, “Variable compleja y aplicaciones”), McGraw-Hill, 2004. The chapter on improper integrals, with Jordan’s lemma and the principal value. Murray R. Spiegel. Complex Variables. Schaum’s Outline Series (Spanish edition, “Variable compleja”), McGraw-Hill, 2011. The three types with dozens of worked examples, classified by the form of the integrand. C. D. Motchenbacher and J. A. Connelly. Low-Noise Electronic System Design. Wiley, 1993. The equivalent noise bandwidth computed for each type of filter, with the integrals worked out."},{"t":"It all comes down to one coefficient","d":"The residue theorem","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/residuos#enunciado","x":"01 It all comes down to one coefficient When the Laurent series is integrated term by term over a closed contour, all the terms give zero except one: the \\( 1/(z - z_0) \\) term, which gives \\( 2\\pi j \\). That coefficient, the \\( b_1 \\) of the expansion, is the residue, and it is the only thing about the function that survives the integral. \\[ \\oint_C f(z)\\,dz = 2\\pi j \\sum_{k} \\operatorname{Res}\\big(f, z_k\\big) \\] The sum runs over the singularities enclosed by \\( C \\), traversed once counterclockwise. It is a result of remarkable economy: an integral over an entire curve is reduced to adding up a few numbers, one per singularity inside. Everything else —the shape of the contour, the behavior of the function far from the poles— plays no part. You have been using it under another name When a rational function is decomposed into partial fractions, the coefficients obtained are the residues. The expansion \\( \\frac{1}{(s+1)(s+2)} = \\frac{1}{s+1} - \\frac{1}{s+2} \\) says that the residue at \\( -1 \\) is 1 and the one at \\( -2 \\) is \\( -1 \\). The residue method is the general version of a calculation you already did in Calculus."},{"t":"The three ways to compute it","d":"The residue theorem","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/residuos#calcular","x":"02 The three ways to compute it There is no need to expand the full series. Depending on the case: Case Formula Simple pole \\( \\operatorname{Res} = \\lim_{z \\to z_0} (z - z_0)\\,f(z) \\) Simple pole with \\( f = g/h \\) \\( \\operatorname{Res} = \\dfrac{g(z_0)}{h'(z_0)} \\) Pole of order \\( m \\) \\( \\operatorname{Res} = \\dfrac{1}{(m-1)!}\\lim_{z \\to z_0} \\dfrac{d^{\\,m-1}}{dz^{\\,m-1}}\\Big[(z - z_0)^{m} f(z)\\Big] \\) Essential singularity No shortcut: you have to expand the Laurent series and read \\( b_1 \\) The second row is the one most used in practice and worth keeping at hand: with \\( f(z) = 1/(z^3 - 1) \\), the denominator has derivative \\( 3z^2 \\), so the residue at each cube root of unity is \\( 1/(3z_k^2) \\). And since \\( z_k^3 = 1 \\), that simplifies to \\( z_k/3 \\). The three residues add up to zero, and that is why a contour that encloses all three gives zero. A zero residue is not the same as having no pole \\( 1/z^2 \\) has a double pole at the origin and residue zero, because its expansion has no \\( 1/z \\) term. The pole is there, but it contributes nothing to the integral. Confusing the two things leads to wrong conclusions about which singularities matter."},{"t":"Each pole contributes its share","d":"The residue theorem","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/residuos#lab","x":"03 Each pole contributes its share Lab · the sum of the contributions On the left, the contour and the poles; on the right, the contributions \\( 2\\pi j \\operatorname{Res} \\) chained one after another. The end of the chain is the value of the integral, and the yellow point is what the numerical calculation over the contour gives. Click or tap the canvas to move the center. f(z) = 1/(z² − 1) f(z) = 1/(z³ − 1) f(z) = 1/(z² + 1) f(z) = (z + 2)/(z²(z − 1)) f(z) = e^z/(z(z − 1)) Contour radius = 1.50"},{"t":"What it is really for: inverse transforms","d":"The residue theorem","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/residuos#laplace","x":"04 What it is really for: inverse transforms The inverse Laplace transform is defined as a line integral along a vertical line to the right of all the poles —the Bromwich line—: \\[ f(t) = \\frac{1}{2\\pi j}\\int_{\\sigma - j\\infty}^{\\sigma + j\\infty} F(s)\\,e^{st}\\,ds \\] Nobody computes it that way. What is done is to close the contour with a semicircle on the left, which by the ML bound contributes zero as the radius grows, and apply the residue theorem to the closed contour that results. The outcome is the formula that is always used: \\[ f(t) = \\sum_{k} \\operatorname{Res}\\Big[F(s)\\,e^{st}\\Big]_{s = p_k} \\] The time response is the sum of the contributions of the poles. Each one brings its own \\( e^{p_k t} \\). With \\( F(s) = \\dfrac{1}{(s+1)(s+2)} \\), the residue at \\( s = -1 \\) is \\( e^{-t} \\) and the one at \\( s = -2 \\) is \\( -e^{-2t} \\), so \\( f(t) = e^{-t} - e^{-2t} \\). It is the same answer that the table gives, obtained without a table. And here the circle closes, the one that began with poles and zeros: each pole of the transfer function contributes an exponential term to the response, and the residue is precisely how much amplitude it does so with. A pole with a small residue is there but is barely noticeable."},{"t":"In the lab","d":"The residue theorem","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/residuos#laboratorio","x":"05 In the lab Activity 1 · Three paths to the same number Compute \\( \\operatorname{Res}\\big(1/(z^2+1),\\, j\\big) \\) in the three ways: by the limit, by \\( g/h' \\) and by expanding in partial fractions. Verify that all three give \\( -j/2 \\). Activity 2 · Inverse transform without a table For \\( F(s) = \\dfrac{s+3}{s(s+1)(s+2)} \\), compute the three residues of \\( F(s)e^{st} \\) and build \\( f(t) \\). Check the initial and final values with the corresponding theorems. Activity 3 · The double pole Inverse-transform \\( F(s) = \\dfrac{1}{(s+1)^2(s+2)} \\) using the formula for a pole of order 2. Verify that a \\( t\\,e^{-t} \\) term appears and compare it with the measured response of a critically damped RLC."},{"t":"Common mistakes","d":"The residue theorem","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/residuos#errores","x":"06 Common mistakes Adding residues of poles that ended up outside. Only those enclosed by the contour count. Using the simple-pole formula on a multiple pole. The limit gives infinity; you have to differentiate \\( m-1 \\) times. Forgetting the \\( (m-1)! \\) in the formula for a pole of order \\( m \\). Confusing a zero residue with the absence of a pole. \\( 1/z^2 \\) has a pole and residue zero. Inverse-transforming without checking that the semicircle contributes zero. If the degree of the numerator is not less than that of the denominator, the polynomial part has to be separated first."},{"t":"Self-assessment","d":"The residue theorem","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/residuos#autoevaluacion","x":"07 Self-assessment What is the residue, in one line? The coefficient of \\( 1/(z-z_0) \\) in the Laurent series: the only term that survives integration over a closed contour. What is \\( \\operatorname{Res}(1/(z^2+1),\\, j) \\)? \\( 1/(2j) = -j/2 \\), using \\( g/h' \\) with \\( h' = 2z \\). What does \\( \\oint dz/(z^3-1) \\) give over \\( |z| = 2 \\)? Zero: it encloses the three poles, and their residues \\( z_k/3 \\) add up to zero because the three cube roots of unity add up to zero. How is the residue computed at a pole of order 2? Multiply by \\( (z-z_0)^2 \\), differentiate once and take the limit. The \\( (m-1)! \\) is 1 in this case. What is the relationship between residues and partial fractions? They are the same thing: the coefficients of the partial fraction decomposition are the residues at each pole. How do you inverse-transform with residues? \\( f(t) = \\sum \\operatorname{Res}[F(s)e^{st}] \\) at the poles of \\( F \\). Each pole contributes its exponential, with the amplitude given by its residue."},{"t":"Further reading","d":"The residue theorem","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/residuos#ampliar","x":"08 Further reading Ruel V. Churchill and James W. Brown. Complex Variables and Applications. 7th ed. (Spanish edition, “Variable compleja y aplicaciones”), McGraw-Hill, 2004. The residue theorem with its computation formulas and the chapter on integral transforms. Murray R. Spiegel. Complex Variables. Schaum’s Outline Series (Spanish edition, “Variable compleja”), McGraw-Hill, 2011. Residues applied to dozens of integrals, organized by type of problem. Alan V. Oppenheim and Alan S. Willsky. Signals and Systems. 2nd ed. (Spanish edition, “Señales y sistemas”), Prentice Hall, 1998. The inverse Laplace transform, with the relationship between residues and partial fractions."},{"t":"When series and parallel are not enough","d":"Systematic circuit analysis","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/resolucion-sistematica#porque","x":"01 When series and parallel are not enough As long as a circuit can be reduced by combining resistors in series and in parallel, that is enough. But it takes just one element connecting two branches “diagonally” for there to be no pair of resistors strictly in series or in parallel. The classic example is the Wheatstone bridge with a detector between its two midpoints: four nodes, six branches and no possible simplification. For those cases you need systematic methods, which directly set up a system of linear equations and always work, whatever the topology. There are two, one for each Kirchhoff law: nodal analysis, which uses the current law, and mesh analysis, which uses the voltage law."},{"t":"Nodal analysis","d":"Systematic circuit analysis","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/resolucion-sistematica#nodos","x":"02 Nodal analysis A reference node is chosen —the ground— and the unknowns are the voltages of the remaining \\( N-1 \\) nodes. At each one you write that the sum of the currents leaving is zero, with each current expressed as a voltage difference across a resistor. The result arranges itself in matrix form: \\[ \\mathbf{G}\\,\\mathbf{V} = \\mathbf{I} \\] \\( \\mathbf{G} \\) is the conductance matrix. It is built “by inspection”: on the diagonal, the sum of the conductances connected to each node; off the diagonal, minus the conductance joining the two nodes. \\( \\mathbf{I} \\) are the currents the sources inject into each node. The matrix \\( \\mathbf{G} \\) of a resistor circuit is symmetric, and that regularity is what makes it possible to write it without setting up a single equation by hand: you read it off the schematic."},{"t":"Mesh analysis","d":"Systematic circuit analysis","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/resolucion-sistematica#mallas","x":"03 Mesh analysis The dual method: in a planar circuit a current is assigned to each mesh —each “window” of the drawing— and in each one you write that the sum of voltages around it is zero. \\[ \\mathbf{R}\\,\\mathbf{I}_m = \\mathbf{V} \\] On the diagonal of \\( \\mathbf{R} \\), the sum of the resistances of each mesh; off it, minus the resistance two meshes share. \\( \\mathbf{V} \\) are the voltage sources traversed in each mesh. Nodal Mesh Law it uses Kirchhoff’s current law Kirchhoff’s voltage law Unknowns \\( N-1 \\) node voltages \\( B-N+1 \\) mesh currents In the Wheatstone bridge (N = 4, B = 6) 2 equations (the source fixes one node) 3 equations Sources that complicate things Floating voltage sources: supernode Shared current sources: supermesh Works for Any circuit Planar circuits only"},{"t":"A Wheatstone bridge with a Pt100","d":"Systematic circuit analysis","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/resolucion-sistematica#puente","x":"04 A Wheatstone bridge with a Pt100 The Pt100 is a platinum temperature sensor: it is 100 Ω at 0 °C and, according to the IEC 60751 standard, 138.51 Ω at 100 °C. Placed in a bridge with three other 100 Ω resistors, at 0 °C the bridge is balanced and the detector reads nothing; any change in temperature unbalances it. With the source fixing the top node and ground the bottom one, two unknowns remain: the voltages of the two midpoints. \\[ \\begin{bmatrix} G_{aa} & -G_d \\\\ -G_d & G_{bb} \\end{bmatrix} \\begin{bmatrix} V_a \\\\ V_b \\end{bmatrix} = \\begin{bmatrix} G_1 V_s \\\\ G_3 V_s \\end{bmatrix} \\] \\( G_{aa} = G_1+G_2+G_d \\) and \\( G_{bb} = G_3+G_4+G_d \\) are the sums of the conductances touching each midpoint; \\( G_d \\) is that of the detector joining them, and it enters subtracted off the diagonal. Lab · the bridge solved by nodal analysis R₁ = R₂ = R₃ = 100 Ω, R₄ is the Pt100 and the source is 5 V. You move the temperature and choose the detector; the panel builds the conductance matrix, solves it and verifies the current law at each node. Pt100 temperature = 25 °C detector: ideal voltmeter (infinite resistance) detector: 1 kΩ galvanometer detector: 50 Ω galvanometer"},{"t":"Where it shows up in electronics","d":"Systematic circuit analysis","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/resolucion-sistematica#electronica","x":"05 Where it shows up in electronics Every circuit simulator —SPICE and its descendants, LTspice, ngspice, the one in any design program— solves exactly this system. It uses a variant called modified nodal analysis, which adds the currents of the voltage sources as unknowns so as not to have to think about supernodes, builds the matrix by inspection by reading the component list, and solves it. With nonlinear components it repeats that step many times, linearizing around each point; in the frequency domain it does so with complex conductances. Case Why it is solved with nodal analysis Sensor bridges: Pt100, strain gauges, load cells The detector between the midpoints prevents series-parallel reduction Transistor biasing networks Several sources and a node shared between base and emitter R-2R ladder D/A converter Every node of the ladder has the same form, and the matrix comes out with a regular pattern"},{"t":"In the lab","d":"Systematic circuit analysis","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/resolucion-sistematica#laboratorio","x":"06 In the lab Activity 1 · Solving by hand and verifying Set up the bridge with the Pt100 at 100 °C and a 1 kΩ galvanometer using nodal analysis. Solve the 2×2 system with Cramer’s rule and compare with the lab panel. Activity 2 · The same bridge by mesh analysis Set up the three mesh equations of the same bridge, solve them and verify that the detector current matches. Count how many operations each method required. Activity 3 · The bridge in SPICE Load the bridge into LTspice or ngspice, sweep the Pt100 resistance from 100 to 175 Ω and plot the detector voltage. Verify that it is not exactly linear."},{"t":"Common mistakes","d":"Systematic circuit analysis","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/resolucion-sistematica#errores","x":"07 Common mistakes Mixing current directions at the same node. It is best to always write all the currents as “leaving”: the sign takes care of itself. Forgetting the minus sign off the diagonal. The shared conductance enters subtracted. Setting up meshes in a nonplanar circuit. The mesh method requires being able to draw the circuit without crossings; the nodal method does not. Choosing the method with more equations. Count first: \\( N-1 \\) against \\( B-N+1 \\). Assuming the bridge is linear. The detector voltage does not grow exactly in proportion to the change in resistance, except for small changes."},{"t":"Self-assessment","d":"Systematic circuit analysis","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/resolucion-sistematica#autoevaluacion","x":"08 Self-assessment Why can a Wheatstone bridge with a detector not be reduced by series-parallel combination? Because the detector joins the two midpoints and no pair of resistors is strictly in series or in parallel. How is the conductance matrix built by inspection? Diagonal: sum of the conductances connected to each node. Off the diagonal: minus the conductance joining the two nodes. How many equations does each method give in a circuit with N nodes and B branches? Nodal: \\( N-1 \\). Mesh: \\( B-N+1 \\). When is the bridge balanced? When \\( R_1/R_2 = R_3/R_4 \\): the two midpoints are at the same voltage and no current flows through the detector. What method does SPICE use? Modified nodal analysis: nodes, plus the voltage-source currents as additional unknowns."},{"t":"Further reading","d":"Systematic circuit analysis","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/resolucion-sistematica#ampliar","x":"09 Further reading Charles K. Alexander and Matthew N. O. Sadiku. Fundamentals of Electric Circuits. 7th ed., McGraw-Hill, 2021. The chapters on nodal and mesh analysis, with supernodes, supermeshes and writing the equations by inspection. Chung-Wen Ho, Albert E. Ruehli and Pierce A. Brennan. The Modified Nodal Approach to Network Analysis. IEEE Transactions on Circuits and Systems, vol. 22, 1975. The paper that defined the modified nodal analysis used by all simulators. IEC 60751. Industrial platinum resistance thermometers and platinum temperature sensors. IEC, 2022. The standard that fixes the Pt100 resistance curve used in the lab."},{"t":"Series resonance","d":"Resonance","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/resonancia#serie","x":"01 Series resonance In a series RLC circuit the inductive reactance \\( \\omega L \\) grows with frequency and the capacitive reactance \\( 1/\\omega C \\) falls. There is a single frequency at which the two are equal and cancel: the resonant frequency. There the impedance is reduced to \\( R \\), its minimum, and the current reaches its maximum. \\[ \\omega_0 = \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{LC}} \\qquad f_0 = \\frac{1}{2\\pi\\sqrt{LC}} \\qquad Z(\\omega_0) = R \\] At resonance the source “sees” a pure resistance: voltage and current are in phase, even though inside the circuit the inductor and the capacitor keep exchanging energy. That exchange is the key to the whole phenomenon. The energy goes back and forth between the magnetic field of the inductor and the electric field of the capacitor, and the source only has to replenish what the resistor loses in each cycle. That is why, at resonance, the voltage across \\( L \\) and across \\( C \\) can be much greater than that of the source."},{"t":"The quality factor Q and the bandwidth","d":"Resonance","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/resonancia#q","x":"02 The quality factor Q and the bandwidth The quality factor measures how much energy the circuit stores compared with what it loses per cycle, and with that it decides two things at once: how sharp the resonance peak is and how much the voltage across the reactive elements is magnified. \\[ Q = \\frac{\\omega_0 L}{R} = \\frac{1}{\\omega_0 C R} = \\frac{1}{R}\\sqrt{\\frac{L}{C}} \\qquad B = \\frac{f_0}{Q} \\qquad V_L = V_C = Q\\,V_{source} \\] \\( B \\) is the bandwidth between the two −3 dB points. A circuit with \\( Q = 100 \\) at 1 MHz passes a band of 10 kHz and multiplies the source voltage by a hundred across its capacitor. Resonator Typical Q LC circuit of a crystal AM radio 50 to 200 Coil of a Qi wireless charger 50 to 100 Copper microwave cavity 10,000 32.768 kHz quartz crystal of a clock 50,000 to 100,000 The multiplied voltage is real With \\( Q = 100 \\) and a 10 V source, 1000 V appears across the capacitor at resonance. It is not a calculation artifact: it is the reason that the capacitors of a resonant circuit are chosen with a wide margin on rated voltage, and that touching the tuning capacitor of a transmitter is dangerous even when the source is modest."},{"t":"Tuning in a station","d":"Resonance","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/resonancia#sintonia","x":"03 Tuning in a station The oldest use of resonance is choosing one station among all those that reach an antenna. A ferrite-rod coil of about 240 µH and a variable capacitor of 30 to 365 pF sweep from about 540 kHz to 1.9 MHz: the whole AM band. In Argentina AM stations are spaced 10 kHz apart, and the design question is how much the circuit attenuates the neighboring station. \\[ \\frac{I}{I_{max}} = \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1 + Q^2\\left(\\dfrac{f}{f_0} - \\dfrac{f_0}{f}\\right)^2}} \\] The universal resonance curve: with \\( Q \\) and \\( f_0 \\) it is completely determined. Lab · the AM tuner 240 µH coil. You turn the variable capacitor to tune and choose the loss resistance. The plot shows the resonance curve around the tuned station, with the neighboring stations at ±10 and ±20 kHz. Variable capacitor = 100 pF R = 5 Ω (good-quality coil) R = 20 Ω R = 60 Ω (very lossy coil) The result is surprising: with a realistic Q, a single tuned circuit attenuates the neighboring station by barely a few decibels. That limitation is what led, in the 1920s, to the superheterodyne receiver, which shifts every station to a fixed intermediate frequency of 455 kHz and filters it there with several cascaded resonant circuits, with no need to retune them any more."},{"t":"Parallel resonance","d":"Resonance","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/resonancia#paralelo","x":"04 Parallel resonance With \\( L \\) and \\( C \\) in parallel the roles are reversed: at resonance the susceptances cancel, the admittance is minimum and the impedance is maximum. The circuit, called a tank, draws almost no current from the source, while inside a current \\( Q \\) times larger circulates between the inductor and the capacitor. Series RLC Parallel RLC At \\( f_0 \\), the impedance is Minimum, equal to \\( R \\) Maximum What is magnified The voltage across \\( L \\) and \\( C \\) The current circulating between \\( L \\) and \\( C \\) Typical use Filter that passes a band, in series with the signal Load of an oscillator or of a tuned amplifier"},{"t":"Where it shows up in electronics","d":"Resonance","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/resonancia#electronica","x":"05 Where it shows up in electronics Application Frequency What resonance does Quartz clock 32.768 kHz The crystal is a mechanical resonator with an enormous Q: it fixes the frequency with parts-per-million precision Qi wireless charging 100 to 205 kHz Two resonant circuits at the same frequency transfer energy by induction with good efficiency RFID / SUBE card 13.56 MHz The card is powered by the energy its resonant antenna picks up from the reader Electronic ballast of a fluorescent tube Tens of kHz The series-resonance overvoltage strikes the tube"},{"t":"In the lab","d":"Resonance","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/resonancia#laboratorio","x":"06 In the lab Activity 1 · Measuring f₀ and Q Build a series RLC with 1 mH, 10 nF and 10 Ω, sweep the frequency with a generator and measure the voltage across the resistor. Locate \\( f_0 \\) and the two −3 dB points, and calculate \\( Q \\) as \\( f_0/B \\). Compare with \\( (1/R)\\sqrt{L/C} \\). Activity 2 · The overvoltage In the same circuit, use the oscilloscope probe to measure the voltage across the capacitor at resonance and verify that it is approximately \\( Q \\) times that of the generator. Activity 3 · Selectivity Using this page’s lab, determine what \\( Q \\) would be needed for the neighboring station at 10 kHz to be attenuated 20 dB at 1 MHz, and discuss whether a real coil can achieve it."},{"t":"Common mistakes","d":"Resonance","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/resonancia#errores","x":"07 Common mistakes Believing that at resonance the voltage across \\( L \\) and \\( C \\) is zero. What vanishes is their sum; each one separately is \\( Q \\) times that of the source. Confusing series resonance with parallel resonance. One is a minimum of impedance and the other, a maximum. Calculating \\( Q \\) at the wrong frequency. \\( Q = \\omega_0 L/R \\) is evaluated at resonance, not at any frequency. Forgetting the resistance of the coil. In a real tuned circuit, almost all of the \\( R \\) is that of the coil’s wire and the core losses. Expecting a single LC circuit to separate nearby stations. With a realistic Q it is not enough; that is why the superheterodyne exists."},{"t":"Self-assessment","d":"Resonance","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/resonancia#autoevaluacion","x":"08 Self-assessment What is the impedance of a series RLC at resonance? \\( R \\): the inductive and capacitive reactances cancel. What is the relationship between \\( Q \\), \\( f_0 \\) and the bandwidth? \\( B = f_0/Q \\): the higher the Q, the narrower the peak. What voltage appears across the capacitor at resonance? \\( Q \\) times the source voltage. What does a variable capacitor of 30 to 365 pF sweep with a 240 µH coil? From about 540 kHz to 1.9 MHz: the complete AM band. What happens to the impedance of a parallel LC tank at resonance? It is maximum: the circuit draws almost no current from the source. Why is a quartz crystal such a good frequency standard? Because it is a resonator with a Q of tens of thousands: its peak is so narrow that the oscillation frequency can hardly move away from it."},{"t":"Further reading","d":"Resonance","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/resonancia#ampliar","x":"09 Further reading Charles K. Alexander and Matthew N. O. Sadiku. Fundamentals of Electric Circuits. 7th ed., McGraw-Hill, 2021. The frequency response chapter develops series and parallel resonance, Q and bandwidth. Frederick E. Terman. Radio Engineers' Handbook. McGraw-Hill, 1943. The classic handbook of vacuum-tube radio: tuned circuits treated from the viewpoint of someone who designed them for production. Horowitz and Hill. The Art of Electronics. 3rd ed., Cambridge University Press, 2015. Resonant circuits and crystal oscillators from the practical design point of view."},{"t":"Three independent liabilities","d":"Professional liability: civil, administrative and criminal","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/responsabilidad-profesional#tres","x":"01 Three independent liabilities When something goes wrong in work signed by an engineer, the engineer can be held liable through three channels at once, each with its own purpose, rules and tribunal: Civil Administrative and disciplinary Criminal Purpose Repairing the damage caused Penalizing the breach of the rules of the profession or of an agency Punishing a crime Who decides A civil judge, or arbitrators The disciplinary tribunal of the association or council; the oversight agency A criminal judge Consequence Paying compensation Warning, fine, suspension or revocation of the license; fines and closures Imprisonment, fine, special disqualification from practicing Main rule Civil and Commercial Code Professional-practice law and code of ethics; the agency’s rules Criminal Code They are independent: a professional can be acquitted in the criminal case and still have to pay compensation, or be sanctioned by the association without any damage having occurred. The order in which they are resolved is regulated by Articles 1774 to 1780 of the Civil and Commercial Code."},{"t":"Civil liability","d":"Professional liability: civil, administrative and criminal","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/responsabilidad-profesional#civil","x":"02 Civil liability Article 1716 establishes the duty to repair damage caused by failing to perform an obligation or by violating the general duty not to harm others. For liability to exist, four elements must be present: Element What it means Unlawfulness (antijuridicidad) Conduct that causes unjustified harm (Article 1717) Damage An injury to a right or an interest: pecuniary or non-pecuniary Causation The damage must be an adequate consequence of the conduct (Article 1726) Basis of liability (factor de atribución) Subjective: fault or intent; objective: the risk or defect of a thing, or a guarantee, regardless of fault Fault, according to Article 1724, is the omission of due diligence, and includes imprudence, negligence and lack of skill in the art or profession. For a professional, diligence is measured against the expert standard of Article 1725. Their liability is in general fault-based, unless they promised a result (Article 1768). Electricity is a hazardous thing Article 1757 imposes strict liability, with no need to show fault, on the owner and the custodian of hazardous or defective things and on whoever carries out dangerous activities. Electrical energy is one such thing. The activity of the liberal professional as such does not fall under that rule, but an installation company, the owner of an installation or the manufacturer of defective equipment can be caught by it. A claim for damages is subject to a limitation period of, in general, three years (Article 2561"},{"t":"Administrative and disciplinary liability","d":"Professional liability: civil, administrative and criminal","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/responsabilidad-profesional#administrativa","x":"03 Administrative and disciplinary liability The disciplinary tribunal of the association or council judges breaches of the professional-practice law and of the code of ethics: lending a signature, practicing outside your own competence, failing to meet obligations to the client, breaching secrecy. Penalties range from a warning to revocation of the license, and do not require that any damage have occurred. In addition, oversight agencies impose their own penalties on those who breach their rules: the ENACOM for an installation that causes interference or is not authorized, a municipality for work without a permit, the labor authority for breaching health and safety rules."},{"t":"Criminal liability","d":"Professional liability: civil, administrative and criminal","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/responsabilidad-profesional#penal","x":"04 Criminal liability The crimes that a professional can commit at work are almost always negligent (culposos): the professional did not want the result, but caused it through imprudence, negligence, lack of skill in their art or profession, or non-compliance with regulations or with the duties of their position. The main ones: Crime Article of the Criminal Code Example Negligent homicide (homicidio culposo) 84 A person is electrocuted by an installation without residual-current protection Negligent injuries (lesiones culposas) 94 A worker suffers burns from an arc flash in a poorly designed switchboard Negligent fire or devastation (incendio o estrago culposo) 189 A fire originating in an installation that did not comply with the regulations In addition to imprisonment or a fine, these crimes carry special disqualification: a ban on practicing the profession for a period of time. “Non-compliance with regulations” is the formula that connects criminal law with technical standards: failing to comply with a mandatory regulation is already, in itself, a form of fault. Classifier · which liabilities are triggered? You choose a situation and the tool indicates which channels of liability could be opened and why. It is a classification exercise: in a real case, each channel depends on proof of its elements."},{"t":"How a professional protects themselves","d":"Professional liability: civil, administrative and criminal","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/responsabilidad-profesional#proteccion","x":"05 How a professional protects themselves Work within your own competence and keep your license up to date. Follow the technical standards and document it: calculation reports, measurements, test protocols, as-built drawings. Write down the scope and limits of the work, and keep a record of the warnings given to the client. Take out professional liability insurance, which covers compensation —not the criminal penalty— up to the insured amount. Keep the documentation for the entire period during which claims may be brought."},{"t":"Activities","d":"Professional liability: civil, administrative and criminal","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/responsabilidad-profesional#laboratorio","x":"06 Activities Activity 1 · Analyze a ruling Find in SAIJ a ruling on an electrical accident and identify the elements of civil liability that the court considered proven. Activity 2 · A case on three tracks For a fire originating in a switchboard, describe what the property owner could claim, what the professional association could do and what the criminal courts would investigate. Activity 3 · The project file Build the index of the documentation that an engineer should keep for an installation in order to defend against a claim."},{"t":"Common mistakes","d":"Professional liability: civil, administrative and criminal","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/responsabilidad-profesional#errores","x":"07 Common mistakes Believing that a criminal acquittal closes the matter. The civil claim can continue. Thinking that without damage there is no penalty. The disciplinary tribunal judges the breach, whether or not there is damage. Relying on insurance for everything. It covers compensation up to an amount, not the criminal penalty or the disciplinary sanction. Not documenting compliance with the regulations. What cannot be proven was not done."},{"t":"Self-assessment","d":"Professional liability: civil, administrative and criminal","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/responsabilidad-profesional#autoevaluacion","x":"08 Self-assessment What are the elements of civil liability? Unlawfulness, damage, causation and a basis of liability. What forms of fault does the Criminal Code list for negligent crimes? Imprudence, negligence, lack of skill in the art or profession, and non-compliance with regulations or with the duties of the position. What is the general limitation period for a claim for damages? Three years (Article 2561 of the Civil and Commercial Code). What is special disqualification? The penalty that prohibits practicing the profession for a period of time."},{"t":"Further reading","d":"Professional liability: civil, administrative and criminal","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/responsabilidad-profesional#ampliar","x":"09 Further reading Félix A. Trigo Represas and Marcelo J. López Mesa. Tratado de la responsabilidad civil. La Ley (in Spanish). The elements of liability and their application to professionals and builders. Carlos Creus. Derecho penal. Parte especial. Astrea (in Spanish). Negligent crimes against persons and crimes of devastation. Civil and Commercial Code and Criminal Code. Articles 1708 to 1780; 84, 94 and 189. InfoLEG (in Spanish). The current texts on civil liability and negligent crimes."},{"t":"Three frequency regions","d":"Frequency response of amplifiers","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/respuesta-frecuencia-amplificadores#bandas","x":"01 Three frequency regions The gain of a transistor amplifier is constant only over a band, the midband. Below it the gain falls because of the coupling and bypass capacitors, which stop being short circuits; above it, because of the internal capacitances of the transistor, which stop being open circuits. Since the two causes act in regions separated by several decades, they are analyzed separately: Region External capacitors Transistor capacitances Method Low frequency Act Open Short-circuit time constants Midband Short circuits Open Resistive small-signal model High frequency Short circuits Act Miller effect and open-circuit time constants The small-signal model is that of the bipolar transistor as an amplifier, and the frequency response of linear systems is that of frequency response, in Signals and Systems."},{"t":"Low frequency: short-circuit time constants","d":"Frequency response of amplifiers","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/respuesta-frecuencia-amplificadores#baja","x":"02 Low frequency: short-circuit time constants With several capacitors, calculating the exact poles is laborious. A widely used estimate takes each capacitor separately, with the others short-circuited, calculates the resistance \\( R_{is} \\) it sees and adds the reciprocals of the time constants: \\[ \\omega_L \\approx \\sum_i \\frac{1}{C_i\\, R_{is}} \\] The estimate is good if one pole dominates. In a common-emitter stage, the emitter bypass capacitor sees a very small resistance, on the order of \\( 1/g_m \\) plus the source resistance divided by β: it is almost always the one that sets the lower cutoff frequency and the one that is worth making large."},{"t":"High frequency: the Miller effect","d":"Frequency response of amplifiers","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/respuesta-frecuencia-amplificadores#alta","x":"03 High frequency: the Miller effect In the hybrid-π model, the bipolar transistor has the diffusion and junction capacitance of the base–emitter junction \\( C_\\pi \\), and that of the base–collector junction \\( C_\\mu \\), much smaller. Its transition frequency, where the current gain falls to 1, is: \\[ f_T = \\frac{g_m}{2\\pi (C_\\pi + C_\\mu)} \\] A 300 MHz small-signal transistor at 1 mA has \\( C_\\pi + C_\\mu \\) ≈ 20 pF. Since \\( g_m \\) grows with current and so does \\( C_\\pi \\), \\( f_T \\) is almost constant over a wide range. The problem is in \\( C_\\mu \\): it connects the input to the output, which has a gain \\( -g_m R_L \\). The current through it is that of a capacitor \\( 1 + g_m R_L \\) times larger connected to ground. Seen from the input, the total capacitance is: \\[ C_{in} = C_\\pi + C_\\mu\\,(1 + g_m R_L) \\] \\[ f_H \\approx \\frac{1}{2\\pi\\, R'\\, C_{in}} \\] \\( R' = R_s \\parallel r_\\pi \\). With a gain of 190, 2 pF of \\( C_\\mu \\) become almost 400 pF at the input: this multiplication is the Miller effect, and it is what limits the bandwidth of the common-emitter stage."},{"t":"Open-circuit time constants","d":"Frequency response of amplifiers","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/respuesta-frecuencia-amplificadores#octc","x":"04 Open-circuit time constants The Miller approximation ignores that \\( C_\\mu \\) also loads the output. A more complete method, by Gray and Searle, adds the time constants of each capacitor seen with the others open, and gives exactly the first-order coefficient of the denominator: \\[ \\tau = C_\\pi R' + C_\\mu \\big[R'(1 + g_m R_L) + R_L\\big] \\] \\[ f_H \\approx \\frac{1}{2\\pi \\tau} \\] The term \\( C_\\mu R_L \\) is the one that Miller forgets. When the source has a small resistance, it matters and the Miller estimate errs on the high side. Lab · the common emitter at high frequency A common-emitter stage with β = 100 and \\( f_T \\) = 300 MHz, with the complete hybrid-π model. The blue curve is the exact response, with its two poles and the zero; the green one, that of a single pole with the open-circuit time constants, and the orange one, that of a pole with the Miller capacitance. Collector current I C = Source resistance R s = 50 Ω R s = 600 Ω R s = 1 kΩ R s = 10 kΩ Load R L = C μ = The second pole of the common-emitter stage is hundreds of times higher, and the zero, at \\( g_m/C_\\mu \\), is in the right half-plane: above it the signal passes directly through \\( C_\\mu \\). Both matter when the amplifier is fed back, as will be seen in the next unit."},{"t":"How to gain bandwidth","d":"Frequency response of amplifiers","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/respuesta-frecuencia-amplificadores#remedios","x":"05 How to gain bandwidth If the problem is the voltage gain between base and collector, the solution is not to have it. The cascode, which already appeared in multistage amplifiers, puts a common-base stage on top of the common-emitter: the collector of the first transistor sees \\( 1/g_m \\) and its gain is −1, so \\( C_\\mu \\) only doubles. Other solutions are to drive the base from a follower, which presents a small \\( R' \\), or to use a differential pair with the output taken from the opposite side, with no voltage gain in the input transistor."},{"t":"In the lab","d":"Frequency response of amplifiers","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/respuesta-frecuencia-amplificadores#laboratorio","x":"06 In the lab Activity 1 · Complete response Measure the gain of a common-emitter stage from 10 Hz to 10 MHz and locate the lower and upper cutoff frequencies. Repeat with half the emitter bypass capacitor. Activity 2 · Miller effect Measure the upper cutoff frequency with two different source resistances, and compare with the Miller and time-constant estimates. Activity 3 · Cascode Add a common-base transistor on top of the common emitter, with the same current and load, and measure the new bandwidth."},{"t":"Common mistakes","d":"Frequency response of amplifiers","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/respuesta-frecuencia-amplificadores#errores","x":"07 Common mistakes Forgetting the source resistance. The cutoff frequency depends on \\( R' \\): the same amplifier has a different band with a different source. Applying Miller with the midband gain at any frequency. Near the cutoff the gain has already changed and the approximation worsens. Taking the transition frequency as the bandwidth of the amplifier. \\( f_T \\) belongs to the short-circuit current gain, not to the circuit. Making all the low-frequency capacitors equal. The emitter bypass sees a much smaller resistance and has to be much larger."},{"t":"Self-assessment","d":"Frequency response of amplifiers","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/respuesta-frecuencia-amplificadores#autoevaluacion","x":"08 Self-assessment What C π + C μ does a transistor with f T = 250 MHz at 2 mA have? \\( g_m \\) = 77.4 mS; \\( 77.4\\,\\mathrm{mS}/(2\\pi \\cdot 250\\,\\mathrm{MHz}) \\approx 49 \\) pF. With C μ = 3 pF and g m R L = 100, what Miller capacitance appears at the input? 303 pF. Why does the cascode have more bandwidth than the common emitter? Because the input transistor has a voltage gain of −1 and its \\( C_\\mu \\) only doubles. What term of the time constant does the Miller approximation ignore? \\( C_\\mu R_L \\): the load that \\( C_\\mu \\) places on the output."},{"t":"Further reading","d":"Frequency response of amplifiers","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/respuesta-frecuencia-amplificadores#ampliar","x":"09 Further reading Adel S. Sedra and Kenneth C. Smith. Microelectronic Circuits. 8th ed., Oxford University Press, 2020. Frequency response with the Miller effect and time constants. Paul R. Gray and Campbell L. Searle. Electronic Principles: Physics, Models, and Circuits. Wiley, 1969. The origin of the open-circuit time constants method. Behzad Razavi. Fundamentals of Microelectronics. 2nd ed., Wiley, 2013. The Miller effect and the cascode explained with a designer's intuition."},{"t":"Designing by looking at the loop gain in frequency","d":"Frequency response in control","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/respuesta-frecuencia-control#porque","x":"01 Designing by looking at the loop gain in frequency The root locus needs a model with poles and zeros. The frequency response works with what can be measured: the loop gain \\( G(j\\omega) \\), with a generator and an analyzer, even if the plant has delays or parts that cannot be modeled. Its two representations are the Bode plot, magnitude and phase as a function of frequency, and the Nyquist plot, the curve traced by \\( G(j\\omega) \\) in the complex plane. The fundamentals were covered in frequency response, in Signals and Systems, and their use in amplifiers in stability and compensation."},{"t":"The Nyquist criterion","d":"Frequency response in control","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/respuesta-frecuencia-control#nyquist","x":"02 The Nyquist criterion The closed loop is unstable if \\( 1 + G(s) \\) has zeros in the right half-plane. Harry Nyquist showed in 1932 that they can be counted by looking at how many times the curve of \\( G(j\\omega) \\), traced for ω from −∞ to +∞, encircles the point −1: \\[ Z = N + P \\] \\( Z \\) is the number of unstable closed-loop poles, \\( N \\) the number of clockwise encirclements of −1 and \\( P \\) the number of unstable open-loop poles. If the plant is stable (P = 0), the closed loop is stable if the curve does not encircle −1. The distance from the curve to the point −1 is the measure of relative stability, and is summarized in two margins: \\[ GM = \\frac{1}{|G(j\\omega_{180})|} \\] \\[ PM = 180° + \\angle G(j\\omega_c) \\] \\( \\omega_{180} \\) is where the phase equals −180° and \\( \\omega_c \\), where the magnitude equals 1. The gain margin says how much the gain can grow before oscillating; the phase margin, how much additional lag is tolerated. Usual values: GM of 6 dB or more and PM between 30° and 60°."},{"t":"The delay","d":"Frequency response in control","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/respuesta-frecuencia-control#retardo","x":"03 The delay Many processes have a pure delay: the time hot water takes to travel along a pipe, or a measurement to be processed. A delay \\( T \\) does not change the magnitude but adds a phase \\( -\\omega T \\) that grows without bound, and its Nyquist plot is a spiral. It cannot be represented with a finite number of poles, but the frequency response handles it without difficulty. It is the reason why processes with delay are controlled with low gains. Lab · Nyquist plot and margins The curve of \\( K\\,G(j\\omega) \\) for ω from 0 to infinity (the symmetric half, from −∞ to 0, is drawn faintly). The red dot is −1; the dotted circle is the unit-magnitude one, where the phase margin is read. Three-pole plant, 1/(s+1)³ One pole with a 0.5 s delay, e^(−0.5 s)/(s+1) Gain K ="},{"t":"From open loop to closed loop","d":"Frequency response in control","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/respuesta-frecuencia-control#lazo","x":"04 From open loop to closed loop The margins translate into the closed-loop response: a phase margin of 45° gives an overshoot close to 23 %, and one of 60°, close to 9 %, as in a second-order system. The crossover frequency \\( \\omega_c \\) sets the bandwidth of the closed loop and, with it, the speed of response. Designing in frequency means shaping \\( |G(j\\omega)| \\): high at low frequencies to reject disturbances and reduce the error, low at high frequencies so as not to amplify noise, and with a gentle slope, of about −20 dB per decade, at the crossover, so that the phase has margin."},{"t":"In the lab","d":"Frequency response in control","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/respuesta-frecuencia-control#laboratorio","x":"05 In the lab Activity 1 · Bode plot of a plant Measure with a generator and an oscilloscope the frequency response of a circuit of three RC stages separated by buffers, and draw its Bode plot. Calculate the gain that would drive the loop to oscillate. Activity 2 · Nyquist With the same measurements, draw the Nyquist plot and read the margins for a given gain. Activity 3 · Digital delay Close the loop with a microcontroller that adds one or two sampling periods of delay, and measure how the phase margin drops."},{"t":"Common mistakes","d":"Frequency response in control","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/respuesta-frecuencia-control#errores","x":"06 Common mistakes Looking at the closed-loop Bode plot to judge stability. The margins are read on the loop gain. Forgetting the unstable open-loop poles. With P different from zero, the loop needs to encircle −1 to be stable. Ignoring the delay of a digital controller. Conversion and computation add phase lag at high frequencies. Relying on a single margin. A curve can have a good gain margin and pass very close to −1 elsewhere."},{"t":"Self-assessment","d":"Frequency response in control","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/respuesta-frecuencia-control#autoevaluacion","x":"07 Self-assessment What is the gain margin of K/(s+1)³ with K = 4? The phase reaches −180° at ω = √3, where \\( |G| = 4/8 \\): a margin of 2, that is, 6 dB. How much phase does a 0.1 s delay add at 10 rad/s? 1 rad, about 57°. What overshoot is expected with 45° of phase margin? Around 23 %. What does the Nyquist criterion say if the plant is stable? That the closed loop is stable if the plot does not encircle the point −1."},{"t":"Further reading","d":"Frequency response in control","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/respuesta-frecuencia-control#ampliar","x":"08 Further reading Katsuhiko Ogata. Modern Control Engineering. 5th ed., Prentice Hall, 2010 (Spanish edition: “Ingeniería de control moderna”, Pearson). Bode and Nyquist plots and design by frequency response. Karl J. Åström and Richard M. Murray. Feedback Systems. 2nd ed., Princeton University Press, 2021. The Nyquist criterion, robustness and the trade-offs of frequency-domain design. Harry Nyquist. Regeneration Theory. Bell System Technical Journal, 11, 1932. The original paper on the stability criterion."},{"t":"One pole: the gain–bandwidth product","d":"Frequency response of feedback amplifiers","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/respuesta-frecuencia-realimentados#unpolo","x":"01 One pole: the gain–bandwidth product If the amplifier has a single pole, \\( A(s) = A_0/(1 + s/\\omega_1) \\), feedback shifts it. Substituting into \\( A/(1 + A\\beta) \\): \\[ A_f(s) = \\frac{A_0/(1+T_0)}{1 + s/\\big[\\omega_1 (1 + T_0)\\big]} \\] \\( T_0 = A_0\\beta \\) is the DC loop gain. The gain goes down by \\( 1 + T_0 \\) and the pole goes up by the same factor: the gain–bandwidth product is maintained. A 1 MHz op amp gives 100 kHz at a gain of 10 and 10 kHz at a gain of 100. With a single pole, the feedback system is never unstable: the pole moves along the real axis to the left, the farther the greater the loop gain. The step response is an exponential with no overshoot. The poles of the amplifier without feedback were calculated in frequency response of amplifiers."},{"t":"Two poles: the quality factor","d":"Frequency response of feedback amplifiers","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/respuesta-frecuencia-realimentados#dospolos","x":"02 Two poles: the quality factor With two real poles \\( \\omega_1 \\) and \\( \\omega_2 \\), as the loop gain increases the feedback poles approach each other along the real axis, meet and separate as a complex pair. The system remains stable, but a peak appears in the frequency response and an overshoot in the step response: \\[ \\omega_0 = \\sqrt{(1 + T_0)\\,\\omega_1 \\omega_2} \\] \\[ Q = \\frac{\\sqrt{(1 + T_0)\\,\\omega_1 \\omega_2}}{\\omega_1 + \\omega_2} \\] With Q = 0.5 the poles coincide: it is the fastest response without overshoot. With Q = 0.707 the frequency response is maximally flat, Butterworth, and the step overshoots by 4.3 %. Above that, the peak grows with Q."},{"t":"Three poles: instability","d":"Frequency response of feedback amplifiers","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/respuesta-frecuencia-realimentados#trespolos","x":"03 Three poles: instability With three or more poles, beyond a certain loop gain the complex pair crosses into the right half-plane and the amplifier oscillates. The practical way to assess it is to look at the loop gain \\( T(j\\omega) \\): at the crossover frequency, where \\( |T| = 1 \\), the phase has to be far from −180°. That distance is the phase margin, and it sets the shape of the feedback response: Phase margin Q of the dominant pair Frequency peaking Step overshoot 76° 0.5 None None 65.5° 0.707 None (Butterworth) 4.3 % 60° 0.82 0.3 dB 8.8 % 45° 1.19 2.4 dB 23 % The values in the table are exact for a loop with a dominant pole and a second pole, and a good guide in the other cases. The usual design asks for at least 45°, and preferably 60°. There is a surprising consequence: an amplifier is harder to stabilize the lower the feedback gain. With large β, \\( |T| \\) is larger at all frequencies, the crossover moves up toward where the higher poles act, and the phase margin shrinks. The most demanding case is the voltage follower, with β = 1. Lab · poles, phase margin and peaking An amplifier with gain \\( 10^5 \\) and poles at 1 kHz, 1 MHz and 10 MHz, fed back with a resistive β. The gray curve is the gain without feedback; the dotted yellow one, \\( 1/\\beta \\), and the blue one, the feedback gain. The crossing of the gray with the yellow is the frequency where \\( |T| = 1 \\). Ideal feedback gain 1/β = The remedy, compensation, modifies the gain without feedback so that the cros"}]);
